{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-321",
              "name": "Release build signed with the public Android debug keystore",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Release build signed with the public Android debug keystore"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The release build type is configured to sign with the shared Android debug keystore instead of a private release key. Because the debug keystore and its password are public and identical on every development machine, the shipped release APK is signed with a key any third party can reproduce. Anyone holding that key can build an APK that Android accepts as a legitimate update to this application, and the application can never be published to Google Play under a trustworthy signing identity.\n\nThis finding is based on static source review only and is **unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — no APK was built and no update was delivered in this assessment."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The release build type is configured to sign with the shared Android debug keystore instead of a private release key. Because the debug keystore and its password are public and identical on every development machine, the shipped release APK is signed with a key any third party can reproduce. Anyone holding that key can build an APK that Android accepts as a legitimate update to this application, and the application can never be published to Google Play under a trustworthy signing identity.\n\nThis finding is based on static source review only and is **unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — no APK was built and no update was delivered in this assessment.\n\nThe application's release artifacts are signed with a key that is not secret. If the APK is distributed outside Google Play (which the permission set and feature set make likely for this product), an attacker can produce a tampered APK that carries the same signing identity and installs as an in-place update over the legitimate app, with no signature-mismatch warning to the user. Such an update runs with the application's identity and privileges and can read the app's private data — including the locally stored chat transcripts, the agent memory vault, and the Action Center store — and can exercise every device capability the app has been granted (notifications, accessibility, location, camera, Health Connect). The app also cannot be published to Google Play with this signing configuration, so the release pipeline is not production-ready.\n\n1. Generate a private release keystore and store it outside the repository, with the credentials in a `key.properties` file that is already covered by the Android `.gitignore`.\n2. Declare a `signingConfigs { create(\"release\") { ... } }` block in `android/app/build.gradle.kts` that loads `key.properties`, and point the `release` build type at that config instead of `debug`.\n3. Make the release build fail loudly when `key.properties` is absent, so a debug-signed release can never be produced by accident again.\n4. Enable code shrinking and resource shrinking for the release build type (`isMinifyEnabled = true`, `isShrinkResources = true`) and add a ProGuard/R8 keep rules file.\n5. Store the release keystore and its passwords in the CI secret store, and use Play App Signing if the app is distributed through Google Play.\n6. Rotate the signing identity before any public release — an already-distributed debug-signed build cannot be safely upgraded in place.",
                "markdown": "The release build type is configured to sign with the shared Android debug keystore instead of a private release key. Because the debug keystore and its password are public and identical on every development machine, the shipped release APK is signed with a key any third party can reproduce. Anyone holding that key can build an APK that Android accepts as a legitimate update to this application, and the application can never be published to Google Play under a trustworthy signing identity.\n\nThis finding is based on static source review only and is **unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — no APK was built and no update was delivered in this assessment.\n\nThe application's release artifacts are signed with a key that is not secret. If the APK is distributed outside Google Play (which the permission set and feature set make likely for this product), an attacker can produce a tampered APK that carries the same signing identity and installs as an in-place update over the legitimate app, with no signature-mismatch warning to the user. Such an update runs with the application's identity and privileges and can read the app's private data — including the locally stored chat transcripts, the agent memory vault, and the Action Center store — and can exercise every device capability the app has been granted (notifications, accessibility, location, camera, Health Connect). The app also cannot be published to Google Play with this signing configuration, so the release pipeline is not production-ready.\n\n1. Generate a private release keystore and store it outside the repository, with the credentials in a `key.properties` file that is already covered by the Android `.gitignore`.\n2. Declare a `signingConfigs { create(\"release\") { ... } }` block in `android/app/build.gradle.kts` that loads `key.properties`, and point the `release` build type at that config instead of `debug`.\n3. Make the release build fail loudly when `key.properties` is absent, so a debug-signed release can never be produced by accident again.\n4. Enable code shrinking and resource shrinking for the release build type (`isMinifyEnabled = true`, `isShrinkResources = true`) and add a ProGuard/R8 keep rules file.\n5. Store the release keystore and its passwords in the CI secret store, and use Play App Signing if the app is distributed through Google Play.\n6. Rotate the signing identity before any public release — an already-distributed debug-signed build cannot be safely upgraded in place."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-321",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/321.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-312",
              "name": "Sensitive app data included in Android backups because allowBackup is unset",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Sensitive app data included in Android backups because allowBackup is unset"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The application declares no android:allowBackup attribute on its application element and ships no fullBackupContent or dataExtractionRules rule, so Android applies its default of allowBackup=true and includes the app private data in cloud backups and device-to-device transfers. The app keeps sensitive user content in plaintext app-private stores - chat transcripts, the agent memory vault, imported skills and the Action Center inbox - which are consequently swept into the backup set without any exclusion. This finding is based on static source review only and is unconfirmed, not dynamically validated: no device, emulator or backup extraction was available in this assessment."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The application declares no android:allowBackup attribute on its application element and ships no fullBackupContent or dataExtractionRules rule, so Android applies its default of allowBackup=true and includes the app private data in cloud backups and device-to-device transfers. The app keeps sensitive user content in plaintext app-private stores - chat transcripts, the agent memory vault, imported skills and the Action Center inbox - which are consequently swept into the backup set without any exclusion. This finding is based on static source review only and is unconfirmed, not dynamically validated: no device, emulator or backup extraction was available in this assessment.\n\nThe app private storage is included in Android cloud backups and device-to-device transfers. A backup set obtained through ADB backup, a cloud-account compromise, or forensic acquisition of a backup image yields the plaintext contents of flames_memory.db (the agent memory vault, whose content column holds arbitrary user- and agent-authored text), flames_skills.db, and the Hive action_center box, together with the session titles in flames_chat.db. The LLM API key and server-profile records are held in FlutterSecureStorage and are not directly recoverable from a backup because Android excludes the Keystore master key, and chat message bodies are likewise encrypted at rest rather than stored in flames_chat.db; the exposure is therefore user content rather than credentials or full transcripts. The issue also runs the other way: a backup restored onto a different device reintroduces this data into app storage without the user having chosen to restore it.\n\n1. Add android:allowBackup=\"false\" to the application element in android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml.\n2. Add android:dataExtractionRules=\"@xml/data_extraction_rules\" alongside it and create android/app/src/main/res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml containing a data-extraction-rules document that excludes the root, database, sharedpref and file domains under both cloud-backup and device-transfer.\n3. Add android:fullBackupContent=\"@xml/backup_rules\" with an equivalent full-backup-content exclusion list for API levels below 31.\n4. If chat history should survive device migration, implement it as an explicit, user-initiated, encrypted export rather than relying on the platform backup channel.\n5. Review any future plaintext store against this rule before adding it, since the default include behaviour exposes new files automatically.",
                "markdown": "The application declares no android:allowBackup attribute on its application element and ships no fullBackupContent or dataExtractionRules rule, so Android applies its default of allowBackup=true and includes the app private data in cloud backups and device-to-device transfers. The app keeps sensitive user content in plaintext app-private stores - chat transcripts, the agent memory vault, imported skills and the Action Center inbox - which are consequently swept into the backup set without any exclusion. This finding is based on static source review only and is unconfirmed, not dynamically validated: no device, emulator or backup extraction was available in this assessment.\n\nThe app private storage is included in Android cloud backups and device-to-device transfers. A backup set obtained through ADB backup, a cloud-account compromise, or forensic acquisition of a backup image yields the plaintext contents of flames_memory.db (the agent memory vault, whose content column holds arbitrary user- and agent-authored text), flames_skills.db, and the Hive action_center box, together with the session titles in flames_chat.db. The LLM API key and server-profile records are held in FlutterSecureStorage and are not directly recoverable from a backup because Android excludes the Keystore master key, and chat message bodies are likewise encrypted at rest rather than stored in flames_chat.db; the exposure is therefore user content rather than credentials or full transcripts. The issue also runs the other way: a backup restored onto a different device reintroduces this data into app storage without the user having chosen to restore it.\n\n1. Add android:allowBackup=\"false\" to the application element in android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml.\n2. Add android:dataExtractionRules=\"@xml/data_extraction_rules\" alongside it and create android/app/src/main/res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml containing a data-extraction-rules document that excludes the root, database, sharedpref and file domains under both cloud-backup and device-transfer.\n3. Add android:fullBackupContent=\"@xml/backup_rules\" with an equivalent full-backup-content exclusion list for API levels below 31.\n4. If chat history should survive device migration, implement it as an explicit, user-initiated, encrypted export rather than relying on the platform backup channel.\n5. Review any future plaintext store against this rule before adding it, since the default include behaviour exposes new files automatically."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-312",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/312.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Exported MainActivity accepts unvalidated action extra, letting any co-resident Android app start a microphone voice turn and dispatch agent prompts",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Exported MainActivity accepts unvalidated action extra, letting any co-resident Android app start a microphone voice turn and dispatch agent prompts"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The exported `MainActivity` of the Flames Android app reads the string extra `extra_flames_action` from any incoming `Intent` and forwards it, unvalidated, to the Flutter layer, which dispatches on it without an allowlist. Any third-party app installed on the same device can therefore start `MainActivity` with a crafted extra and trigger privileged app behaviour with no user action beyond the app window becoming visible: `voice_input` starts a 15-second speech-recognition (microphone) capture session, and `quick_status` sends a canned prompt to the AI agent, which may then execute device tools. **This finding is unconfirmed and not dynamically validated — the audit was source-only with no emulator or device.**"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The exported `MainActivity` of the Flames Android app reads the string extra `extra_flames_action` from any incoming `Intent` and forwards it, unvalidated, to the Flutter layer, which dispatches on it without an allowlist. Any third-party app installed on the same device can therefore start `MainActivity` with a crafted extra and trigger privileged app behaviour with no user action beyond the app window becoming visible: `voice_input` starts a 15-second speech-recognition (microphone) capture session, and `quick_status` sends a canned prompt to the AI agent, which may then execute device tools. **This finding is unconfirmed and not dynamically validated — the audit was source-only with no emulator or device.**\n\nAn attacker with no Android permissions and no special access — merely a co-resident app on the same device — can drive Flames' most sensitive local capability without the user tapping anything in the app.\n\n1. **Microphone activation (`voice_input`).** The app opens a 15-second speech-recognition session (`VoiceTurnService.defaultListenFor`). Any speech in the room during that window is transcribed on-device and, when a dispatch transport is ready, transmitted to the configured remote LLM API as a chat prompt. The capture begins with the microphone permission already granted by the user to Flames, so no permission dialog appears. The recording indicator is an overlay bubble that only exists if the user previously enabled it (off by default), so in the default configuration there is no on-screen indication that capture is active.\n\n2. **Autonomous agent dispatch (`quick_status`).** The fixed prompt `Quick status check: summarize battery, device context, and agent readiness.` is injected into the agent loop. The agent can respond by invoking the app's native device tools (clipboard, location, contacts, calendar, notifications, accessibility-tree reading, Health Connect). The dispatch path was read to confirm the resulting state machine, and a `WaitingApproval` terminal state is surfaced as a failure rather than silently bypassing the human-in-the-loop gate, so this leg is bounded by the existing tier gating rather than escalating it.\n\n3. **Persistence and reachability.** `pendingAction` is set on both `onCreate` and `onNewIntent` and is never cleared until the Dart layer drains it, so the value survives a cold start and is replayed when the listener mounts. The chain does not require the app to be in the foreground: the action is delivered through `onNewIntent` while the activity is already alive, and the background service path (`FlamesBackgroundService` action `ACTION_VOICE_INPUT`, reachable from the ongoing notification and the Quick Settings tile) performs the same Dart injection. Injected microphone capture is the demonstrated consequence; no audio exfiltration to an attacker-controlled destination is demonstrated, so the confidentiality rating below reflects initiation of capture of the user's audio, not demonstrated attacker retrieval of it.\n\nTreat the action extra as untrusted input at the Android boundary, because any co-resident app can supply it.\n\n1. Validate the incoming action against an explicit allowlist at the point of extraction — the three values the app actually implements (`voice_input`, `cancel_voice`, `quick_status`) — and ignore anything else. An allowlist is the correct primitive here: the set is closed and known, and a denylist would silently accept future additions.\n2. Do not rely on the intent action or on the `MAIN`/`LAUNCHER` filter for trust. The extra is what selects the behaviour, so the check must be on the extra value itself, not on how the activity was started.\n3. If third-party automation of these actions is a product requirement, move it behind a dedicated exported component protected by a signature-level permission, so only apps signed with the same developer certificate can trigger it. The launcher activity should never be a privileged control surface.\n4. Keep the extraction function as the single choke point for validation so that both the `onCreate` and `onNewIntent` paths are covered by one check.\n5. Add a regression test asserting that an unknown action value is rejected and never reaches the method channel, and that a valid value still works, so the allowlist cannot silently regress.",
                "markdown": "The exported `MainActivity` of the Flames Android app reads the string extra `extra_flames_action` from any incoming `Intent` and forwards it, unvalidated, to the Flutter layer, which dispatches on it without an allowlist. Any third-party app installed on the same device can therefore start `MainActivity` with a crafted extra and trigger privileged app behaviour with no user action beyond the app window becoming visible: `voice_input` starts a 15-second speech-recognition (microphone) capture session, and `quick_status` sends a canned prompt to the AI agent, which may then execute device tools. **This finding is unconfirmed and not dynamically validated — the audit was source-only with no emulator or device.**\n\nAn attacker with no Android permissions and no special access — merely a co-resident app on the same device — can drive Flames' most sensitive local capability without the user tapping anything in the app.\n\n1. **Microphone activation (`voice_input`).** The app opens a 15-second speech-recognition session (`VoiceTurnService.defaultListenFor`). Any speech in the room during that window is transcribed on-device and, when a dispatch transport is ready, transmitted to the configured remote LLM API as a chat prompt. The capture begins with the microphone permission already granted by the user to Flames, so no permission dialog appears. The recording indicator is an overlay bubble that only exists if the user previously enabled it (off by default), so in the default configuration there is no on-screen indication that capture is active.\n\n2. **Autonomous agent dispatch (`quick_status`).** The fixed prompt `Quick status check: summarize battery, device context, and agent readiness.` is injected into the agent loop. The agent can respond by invoking the app's native device tools (clipboard, location, contacts, calendar, notifications, accessibility-tree reading, Health Connect). The dispatch path was read to confirm the resulting state machine, and a `WaitingApproval` terminal state is surfaced as a failure rather than silently bypassing the human-in-the-loop gate, so this leg is bounded by the existing tier gating rather than escalating it.\n\n3. **Persistence and reachability.** `pendingAction` is set on both `onCreate` and `onNewIntent` and is never cleared until the Dart layer drains it, so the value survives a cold start and is replayed when the listener mounts. The chain does not require the app to be in the foreground: the action is delivered through `onNewIntent` while the activity is already alive, and the background service path (`FlamesBackgroundService` action `ACTION_VOICE_INPUT`, reachable from the ongoing notification and the Quick Settings tile) performs the same Dart injection. Injected microphone capture is the demonstrated consequence; no audio exfiltration to an attacker-controlled destination is demonstrated, so the confidentiality rating below reflects initiation of capture of the user's audio, not demonstrated attacker retrieval of it.\n\nTreat the action extra as untrusted input at the Android boundary, because any co-resident app can supply it.\n\n1. Validate the incoming action against an explicit allowlist at the point of extraction — the three values the app actually implements (`voice_input`, `cancel_voice`, `quick_status`) — and ignore anything else. An allowlist is the correct primitive here: the set is closed and known, and a denylist would silently accept future additions.\n2. Do not rely on the intent action or on the `MAIN`/`LAUNCHER` filter for trust. The extra is what selects the behaviour, so the check must be on the extra value itself, not on how the activity was started.\n3. If third-party automation of these actions is a product requirement, move it behind a dedicated exported component protected by a signature-level permission, so only apps signed with the same developer certificate can trigger it. The launcher activity should never be a privileged control surface.\n4. Keep the extraction function as the single choke point for validation so that both the `onCreate` and `onNewIntent` paths are covered by one check.\n5. Add a regression test asserting that an unknown action value is rejected and never reaches the method channel, and that a valid value still works, so the allowlist cannot silently regress."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "name": "Screen text read from any app is injected into the agent prompt as a tool result with no untrusted-data framing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Screen text read from any app is injected into the agent prompt as a tool result with no untrusted-data framing"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Android agent can read the accessibility tree of whatever app is in the foreground and hand that text to the LLM as a tool result (`ui_find_elements`). The tree is rendered from the window of *another* app, so its text is authored by that app, not by Flames or by the user — yet nothing marks it as untrusted, and the result is placed verbatim in the model's context. The codebase already ships a prompt-injection scanner (`SkillSecurityScanner`) but applies it only to imported skill text, so screen content reaches the model unframed and unscanned. The same model can then call Tier-1 tools that need no user approval (`save_memory`, `clipboard_write`) and read Tier-1/2 sensitive data (clipboard, location, files, notifications, OTP, health, contacts). Memory entries are re-injected on later turns as a `role: \"system\"` message, which converts transient screen text into persistent, highest-authority instructions. **Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — this is a source-only audit; there is no emulator, no device and no running app, so no payload was executed against a live model."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Android agent can read the accessibility tree of whatever app is in the foreground and hand that text to the LLM as a tool result (`ui_find_elements`). The tree is rendered from the window of *another* app, so its text is authored by that app, not by Flames or by the user — yet nothing marks it as untrusted, and the result is placed verbatim in the model's context. The codebase already ships a prompt-injection scanner (`SkillSecurityScanner`) but applies it only to imported skill text, so screen content reaches the model unframed and unscanned. The same model can then call Tier-1 tools that need no user approval (`save_memory`, `clipboard_write`) and read Tier-1/2 sensitive data (clipboard, location, files, notifications, OTP, health, contacts). Memory entries are re-injected on later turns as a `role: \"system\"` message, which converts transient screen text into persistent, highest-authority instructions. **Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — this is a source-only audit; there is no emulator, no device and no running app, so no payload was executed against a live model.\n\nAttacker-authored text on screen gains instruction authority inside a tool-calling agent. A malicious app that renders a crafted window (or a hostile web page / message rendered in any app) and waits for the user to invoke the agent can attempt to steer the model into data-collecting tool calls, and to persist the payload into the memory vault where it is re-injected as a system message on every subsequent turn. Because the injected text can invoke `save_memory` (Tier 1, auto-executed, no approval) and `clipboard_write` (Tier 1), the payload can be made persistent and can be staged for collection by a co-resident app. Read tools that need no approval include `list_device_files`, `read_web_page`, `calendar_list_events`, `external_camera_list`, `search_memories` and `list_memories`. The concrete end-to-end result is model- and payload-dependent and was not demonstrated: the attacker cannot directly observe the agent's replies, so exfiltration requires the model to also follow a second injected step that writes the data to an observable sink (clipboard, an external URL, or a WhatsApp message).\n\nTreat every accessibility-tree result as untrusted data before it becomes a tool result, and stop relying on the platform to redact secure fields.\n\n1. Frame the tool result explicitly at the single point where the tree is turned into text (the data-layer `findElementsFormatted`). Prefix and suffix the serialized tree with a short, unambiguous statement that the block is screen content captured from another app, that it must be treated strictly as data, and that nothing inside it may change the agent's task, tool policy, or disclosures. Leave the empty-tree message unframed so existing behaviour is preserved.\n2. Reuse the existing prompt-injection scanner rather than writing a new one. Screen text is the same untrusted class as an imported skill body, so run `SkillSecurityScanner` over the serialized tree and, when patterns match, prepend the matched pattern identifiers so the model and the user can see that the screen contains a suspected payload. Because the scanner is pattern-based and evadable by paraphrase, keep the explicit framing as the primary control and treat the scan as defence in depth.\n3. Exclude secure fields on the device side. In `captureTree`, skip nodes whose `isPassword` flag is set, and skip their subtrees, so password and other secure-field values are never collected even if a given device does not redact them.\n4. State the data class in the tool description so the model's own tool contract carries the warning, not just the result payload.\n5. Apply the same framing to the other untrusted text channels that feed the prompt — notification text, web-page text, and recalled memory entries — and stop injecting recalled memory as a `role: \"system\"` message, which currently gives attacker-influenced text the highest available authority. This is outside the code changed here and remains open.",
                "markdown": "The Android agent can read the accessibility tree of whatever app is in the foreground and hand that text to the LLM as a tool result (`ui_find_elements`). The tree is rendered from the window of *another* app, so its text is authored by that app, not by Flames or by the user — yet nothing marks it as untrusted, and the result is placed verbatim in the model's context. The codebase already ships a prompt-injection scanner (`SkillSecurityScanner`) but applies it only to imported skill text, so screen content reaches the model unframed and unscanned. The same model can then call Tier-1 tools that need no user approval (`save_memory`, `clipboard_write`) and read Tier-1/2 sensitive data (clipboard, location, files, notifications, OTP, health, contacts). Memory entries are re-injected on later turns as a `role: \"system\"` message, which converts transient screen text into persistent, highest-authority instructions. **Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — this is a source-only audit; there is no emulator, no device and no running app, so no payload was executed against a live model.\n\nAttacker-authored text on screen gains instruction authority inside a tool-calling agent. A malicious app that renders a crafted window (or a hostile web page / message rendered in any app) and waits for the user to invoke the agent can attempt to steer the model into data-collecting tool calls, and to persist the payload into the memory vault where it is re-injected as a system message on every subsequent turn. Because the injected text can invoke `save_memory` (Tier 1, auto-executed, no approval) and `clipboard_write` (Tier 1), the payload can be made persistent and can be staged for collection by a co-resident app. Read tools that need no approval include `list_device_files`, `read_web_page`, `calendar_list_events`, `external_camera_list`, `search_memories` and `list_memories`. The concrete end-to-end result is model- and payload-dependent and was not demonstrated: the attacker cannot directly observe the agent's replies, so exfiltration requires the model to also follow a second injected step that writes the data to an observable sink (clipboard, an external URL, or a WhatsApp message).\n\nTreat every accessibility-tree result as untrusted data before it becomes a tool result, and stop relying on the platform to redact secure fields.\n\n1. Frame the tool result explicitly at the single point where the tree is turned into text (the data-layer `findElementsFormatted`). Prefix and suffix the serialized tree with a short, unambiguous statement that the block is screen content captured from another app, that it must be treated strictly as data, and that nothing inside it may change the agent's task, tool policy, or disclosures. Leave the empty-tree message unframed so existing behaviour is preserved.\n2. Reuse the existing prompt-injection scanner rather than writing a new one. Screen text is the same untrusted class as an imported skill body, so run `SkillSecurityScanner` over the serialized tree and, when patterns match, prepend the matched pattern identifiers so the model and the user can see that the screen contains a suspected payload. Because the scanner is pattern-based and evadable by paraphrase, keep the explicit framing as the primary control and treat the scan as defence in depth.\n3. Exclude secure fields on the device side. In `captureTree`, skip nodes whose `isPassword` flag is set, and skip their subtrees, so password and other secure-field values are never collected even if a given device does not redact them.\n4. State the data class in the tool description so the model's own tool contract carries the warning, not just the result payload.\n5. Apply the same framing to the other untrusted text channels that feed the prompt — notification text, web-page text, and recalled memory entries — and stop injecting recalled memory as a `role: \"system\"` message, which currently gives attacker-influenced text the highest available authority. This is outside the code changed here and remains open."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-863",
              "name": "Tools with device-state side effects are classified Tier 1 \"Read-Only / Ambient\" and auto-execute with no authorization gate",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Tools with device-state side effects are classified Tier 1 \"Read-Only / Ambient\" and auto-execute with no authorization gate"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The agent tool registry documents Tier 1 as **\"Read-Only / Ambient (auto-execute)\"**, but six registered tools that modify device state, send user-visible output, or destroy user data are assigned to Tier 1 and therefore execute with no permission check and no approval prompt. Two of them (`send_local_notification`, `save_memory`) additionally give an injected instruction a user-visible spoofing primitive and a persistence foothold, because saved memories are re-injected into every subsequent model request as a `role: system` message. The misclassification is provably a defect rather than policy: `send_local_notification` (Tier 1, auto-executes) and `schedule_reminder` (Tier 3, requires a user tap) are registered against the same notification service in the same method, so sending a notification immediately is ungated while scheduling one for later is gated."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The agent tool registry documents Tier 1 as **\"Read-Only / Ambient (auto-execute)\"**, but six registered tools that modify device state, send user-visible output, or destroy user data are assigned to Tier 1 and therefore execute with no permission check and no approval prompt. Two of them (`send_local_notification`, `save_memory`) additionally give an injected instruction a user-visible spoofing primitive and a persistence foothold, because saved memories are re-injected into every subsequent model request as a `role: system` message. The misclassification is provably a defect rather than policy: `send_local_notification` (Tier 1, auto-executes) and `schedule_reminder` (Tier 3, requires a user tap) are registered against the same notification service in the same method, so sending a notification immediately is ungated while scheduling one for later is gated.\n\nOnce the on-device agent has decided to call one of these tools, the action executes with no permission evaluation and no user confirmation. The demonstrated (static) consequences are: arbitrary attacker-influenced text written to the system clipboard; arbitrary user-visible local notifications presented under the application's identity (a spoofing primitive that can impersonate a bank, security, or system alert); the ability to start and stop the foreground service, acquire and release wake locks, and set the Quick Settings tile state; registration of persistent geofence monitoring; and destruction of the user's stored memory vault by id or key with no confirmation. Because `save_memory` writes to a SQLite vault whose contents are re-injected into every subsequent model request as a `role: system` message, an instruction that reaches the prompt can also persist attacker-authored text inside the system-role portion of the conversation, surviving the turn, the session, and application restarts. The affected scope is the local device state and local application data of the user who runs the agent; no disclosure of restricted data was demonstrated.\n\nReclassify every tool whose execution modifies device state, produces user-visible output, or destroys stored data so that it is no longer Tier 1. Because the registry only evaluates a permission callback when one is present, and none of these six services defines one, assigning Tier 2 would not by itself introduce any enforcement; the immediate and reliable correction is to move these tools to the human-in-the-loop tier, and to add permission callbacks only where an automatic, non-interactive path is genuinely required.\n\nSpecifically: move the immediate local-notification tool, the clipboard write tool, the memory write and delete tools, the geofence registration tool, and the background-service control tool from the automatic tier to the confirmation tier, and update the tier documentation in the security policy so that Tier 1 is explicitly reserved for tools with no device-visible or persistent side effect.\n\nIndependently of the tier assignment, stop presenting stored memory content to the model as if it were trusted system context. Wrap the memory block in an explicit untrusted-data directive and escape the stored keys and content so that embedded line breaks cannot impersonate the surrounding conversation structure.\n\nFinally, update the test suite in the same change: several existing tests assert the current tiers and several integration tests exercise these tools through the agent loop without an approval step, so they will need to expect the approval gate instead. Consider adding a regression test that fails if any tool whose executor performs a write, a notification, a clipboard operation, or an external launch is registered at the automatic tier.",
                "markdown": "The agent tool registry documents Tier 1 as **\"Read-Only / Ambient (auto-execute)\"**, but six registered tools that modify device state, send user-visible output, or destroy user data are assigned to Tier 1 and therefore execute with no permission check and no approval prompt. Two of them (`send_local_notification`, `save_memory`) additionally give an injected instruction a user-visible spoofing primitive and a persistence foothold, because saved memories are re-injected into every subsequent model request as a `role: system` message. The misclassification is provably a defect rather than policy: `send_local_notification` (Tier 1, auto-executes) and `schedule_reminder` (Tier 3, requires a user tap) are registered against the same notification service in the same method, so sending a notification immediately is ungated while scheduling one for later is gated.\n\nOnce the on-device agent has decided to call one of these tools, the action executes with no permission evaluation and no user confirmation. The demonstrated (static) consequences are: arbitrary attacker-influenced text written to the system clipboard; arbitrary user-visible local notifications presented under the application's identity (a spoofing primitive that can impersonate a bank, security, or system alert); the ability to start and stop the foreground service, acquire and release wake locks, and set the Quick Settings tile state; registration of persistent geofence monitoring; and destruction of the user's stored memory vault by id or key with no confirmation. Because `save_memory` writes to a SQLite vault whose contents are re-injected into every subsequent model request as a `role: system` message, an instruction that reaches the prompt can also persist attacker-authored text inside the system-role portion of the conversation, surviving the turn, the session, and application restarts. The affected scope is the local device state and local application data of the user who runs the agent; no disclosure of restricted data was demonstrated.\n\nReclassify every tool whose execution modifies device state, produces user-visible output, or destroys stored data so that it is no longer Tier 1. Because the registry only evaluates a permission callback when one is present, and none of these six services defines one, assigning Tier 2 would not by itself introduce any enforcement; the immediate and reliable correction is to move these tools to the human-in-the-loop tier, and to add permission callbacks only where an automatic, non-interactive path is genuinely required.\n\nSpecifically: move the immediate local-notification tool, the clipboard write tool, the memory write and delete tools, the geofence registration tool, and the background-service control tool from the automatic tier to the confirmation tier, and update the tier documentation in the security policy so that Tier 1 is explicitly reserved for tools with no device-visible or persistent side effect.\n\nIndependently of the tier assignment, stop presenting stored memory content to the model as if it were trusted system context. Wrap the memory block in an explicit untrusted-data directive and escape the stored keys and content so that embedded line breaks cannot impersonate the surrounding conversation structure.\n\nFinally, update the test suite in the same change: several existing tests assert the current tiers and several integration tests exercise these tools through the agent loop without an approval step, so they will need to expect the approval gate instead. Consider adding a regression test that fails if any tool whose executor performs a write, a notification, a clipboard operation, or an external launch is registered at the automatic tier."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-863",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/863.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-22",
              "name": "Unrestricted file read and unbounded directory enumeration via read_device_file / list_device_files agent tools",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unrestricted file read and unbounded directory enumeration via read_device_file / list_device_files agent tools"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The agent tools **`read_device_file`** and **`list_device_files`** accept an absolute filesystem path supplied by the language model and pass it to `File(path).readAsBytes()` / `Directory(path).list()` without any containment check against a workspace root. The declared \"device workspace\" boundary exists only in the tools' prose descriptions and is never enforced in code.\n\nThe one gate that was supposed to constrain `read_device_file` is inert: its Tier-2 permission check resolves to a hardcoded `true`. **`list_device_files` is declared Tier-1 (auto-execute) and therefore has no gate at all**, and it accepts `recursive: true`.\n\nBoth paths are reachable from remote model output — tool-call arguments are decoded from the model's JSON with no validation and auto-executed. Because untrusted text (notification bodies, remote skill packages, tool results) reaches the model with no data/instruction separation, a prompt-injection payload can direct the agent to read or enumerate files. **Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — this is a source-only audit with no emulator, no device and no running app."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The agent tools **`read_device_file`** and **`list_device_files`** accept an absolute filesystem path supplied by the language model and pass it to `File(path).readAsBytes()` / `Directory(path).list()` without any containment check against a workspace root. The declared \"device workspace\" boundary exists only in the tools' prose descriptions and is never enforced in code.\n\nThe one gate that was supposed to constrain `read_device_file` is inert: its Tier-2 permission check resolves to a hardcoded `true`. **`list_device_files` is declared Tier-1 (auto-execute) and therefore has no gate at all**, and it accepts `recursive: true`.\n\nBoth paths are reachable from remote model output — tool-call arguments are decoded from the model's JSON with no validation and auto-executed. Because untrusted text (notification bodies, remote skill packages, tool results) reaches the model with no data/instruction separation, a prompt-injection payload can direct the agent to read or enumerate files. **Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — this is a source-only audit with no emulator, no device and no running app.\n\nAn attacker who influences the model's tool-call arguments (a remote or compromised model endpoint, a remote skill package, a crafted notification body, or an indirect injection via any tool result) can direct the agent to read and enumerate files as the application's own UID, with no workspace boundary enforced.\n\n**Demonstrated by code path (not runtime-verified):**\n\n- **Unbounded directory enumeration — fully effective.** `list_device_files` is Tier 1 (auto-execute, no gate) and accepts `recursive: true`. It returns names, absolute paths, sizes, modification times and extensions for the whole subtree. Directory listing applies no content filter of any kind, so it enumerates the app's entire private storage — including the `databases/`, `shared_prefs/`, `files/` and `app_flutter/` trees. This discloses the storage layout and reveals which features hold data, which is meaningful reconnaissance for follow-on attacks and is not blocked by any control.\n- **Read of NUL-free text files within the app's UID.** `read_device_file` returns any text file the app can open, including application-private XML and JSON. This includes the FlutterSecureStorage backing file under the app's `shared_prefs/` directory (the app constructs `const FlutterSecureStorage()` with no options override at `lib/common/injection/modules.dart:22-23`, so plugin defaults determine the filename; the exact name was not observed on a device). That file holds the **encrypted** credential blobs for the API key, gateway token and server profiles. Because these are ciphertext, this does not by itself disclose the credentials — it discloses the encrypted material, the stored key names, and the existence and structure of the credential store.\n- **Filter bypass via the PDF branch.** A path ending in `.pdf` is routed to PDF text extraction *before* the extension denylist and null-byte checks run, and `extractPdfText` applies neither filter. Any file addressed under a `.pdf` name is therefore read with no binary screening at all.\n\n**Explicitly NOT demonstrated, contrary to an earlier draft of this finding:** the plaintext SQLite databases (`flames_memory.db`, `flames_skills.db`, `flames_chat.db`) are **not** readable through `read_device_file`. They are blocked twice over — `db`/`sqlite` appear in the extension denylist, and a real SQLite file contains a NUL byte at offset 15 of its header, which the null-byte sample rejects regardless of filename. Memory-vault contents are also already available to the model through the Tier-1 `list_memories` / `search_memories` tools, so reading the database file would add little.\n\n**Why this is still a finding.** The app intends a workspace boundary — its own bundled skill descriptor states the invariant \"read file contents within allowed directories\" — and no code enforces it. The absence of containment is the root defect: any future text file the app writes into its private storage (a token cache, a log, an export) becomes immediately readable by model output, and enumeration of the private tree is ungated today. Scope limits, stated honestly: no `READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE` / `MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE` is declared, so arbitrary `/sdcard` reads fail on modern Android; impact is confined to the app's own private storage.\n\nEnforce a workspace boundary on every agent file tool, at the point where the model-supplied argument enters.\n\n1. Introduce a single containment primitive on the file-workspace service, and use it in the `execute` closure of both `read_device_file` and `list_device_files` before any filesystem access. Do not rely on the tool description to communicate the boundary.\n2. Resolve the requested path against the application documents directory, canonicalise it (collapse dot segments and normalise separators), and reject anything that is not a strict descendant of that root. Compare with a trailing-separator-aware prefix test so a sibling directory sharing the root's name prefix is not accepted.\n3. Resolve symbolic links on both the candidate path and the root before the final comparison, so a symlink created inside the workspace cannot be used to reach outside it.\n4. Replace the hardcoded permission grant in the default filesystem wrapper with a real evaluation. If the intended gate is a user-facing one, wire it to an actual platform permission or approval rather than returning a constant.\n5. Raise `list_device_files` from Tier 1 (auto-execute) to at least Tier 2 so enumeration is no longer performed without evaluation; promote it to Tier 3 if enumeration of the private data directory should require explicit user approval. Note that the HITL sheet already renders the real argument JSON, so a Tier-3 promotion would show the user the exact directory being listed.\n6. Add regression tests that assert an absolute path outside the workspace, a traversal sequence, and a symlink escape are each rejected, and that an in-workspace path still succeeds.",
                "markdown": "The agent tools **`read_device_file`** and **`list_device_files`** accept an absolute filesystem path supplied by the language model and pass it to `File(path).readAsBytes()` / `Directory(path).list()` without any containment check against a workspace root. The declared \"device workspace\" boundary exists only in the tools' prose descriptions and is never enforced in code.\n\nThe one gate that was supposed to constrain `read_device_file` is inert: its Tier-2 permission check resolves to a hardcoded `true`. **`list_device_files` is declared Tier-1 (auto-execute) and therefore has no gate at all**, and it accepts `recursive: true`.\n\nBoth paths are reachable from remote model output — tool-call arguments are decoded from the model's JSON with no validation and auto-executed. Because untrusted text (notification bodies, remote skill packages, tool results) reaches the model with no data/instruction separation, a prompt-injection payload can direct the agent to read or enumerate files. **Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — this is a source-only audit with no emulator, no device and no running app.\n\nAn attacker who influences the model's tool-call arguments (a remote or compromised model endpoint, a remote skill package, a crafted notification body, or an indirect injection via any tool result) can direct the agent to read and enumerate files as the application's own UID, with no workspace boundary enforced.\n\n**Demonstrated by code path (not runtime-verified):**\n\n- **Unbounded directory enumeration — fully effective.** `list_device_files` is Tier 1 (auto-execute, no gate) and accepts `recursive: true`. It returns names, absolute paths, sizes, modification times and extensions for the whole subtree. Directory listing applies no content filter of any kind, so it enumerates the app's entire private storage — including the `databases/`, `shared_prefs/`, `files/` and `app_flutter/` trees. This discloses the storage layout and reveals which features hold data, which is meaningful reconnaissance for follow-on attacks and is not blocked by any control.\n- **Read of NUL-free text files within the app's UID.** `read_device_file` returns any text file the app can open, including application-private XML and JSON. This includes the FlutterSecureStorage backing file under the app's `shared_prefs/` directory (the app constructs `const FlutterSecureStorage()` with no options override at `lib/common/injection/modules.dart:22-23`, so plugin defaults determine the filename; the exact name was not observed on a device). That file holds the **encrypted** credential blobs for the API key, gateway token and server profiles. Because these are ciphertext, this does not by itself disclose the credentials — it discloses the encrypted material, the stored key names, and the existence and structure of the credential store.\n- **Filter bypass via the PDF branch.** A path ending in `.pdf` is routed to PDF text extraction *before* the extension denylist and null-byte checks run, and `extractPdfText` applies neither filter. Any file addressed under a `.pdf` name is therefore read with no binary screening at all.\n\n**Explicitly NOT demonstrated, contrary to an earlier draft of this finding:** the plaintext SQLite databases (`flames_memory.db`, `flames_skills.db`, `flames_chat.db`) are **not** readable through `read_device_file`. They are blocked twice over — `db`/`sqlite` appear in the extension denylist, and a real SQLite file contains a NUL byte at offset 15 of its header, which the null-byte sample rejects regardless of filename. Memory-vault contents are also already available to the model through the Tier-1 `list_memories` / `search_memories` tools, so reading the database file would add little.\n\n**Why this is still a finding.** The app intends a workspace boundary — its own bundled skill descriptor states the invariant \"read file contents within allowed directories\" — and no code enforces it. The absence of containment is the root defect: any future text file the app writes into its private storage (a token cache, a log, an export) becomes immediately readable by model output, and enumeration of the private tree is ungated today. Scope limits, stated honestly: no `READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE` / `MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE` is declared, so arbitrary `/sdcard` reads fail on modern Android; impact is confined to the app's own private storage.\n\nEnforce a workspace boundary on every agent file tool, at the point where the model-supplied argument enters.\n\n1. Introduce a single containment primitive on the file-workspace service, and use it in the `execute` closure of both `read_device_file` and `list_device_files` before any filesystem access. Do not rely on the tool description to communicate the boundary.\n2. Resolve the requested path against the application documents directory, canonicalise it (collapse dot segments and normalise separators), and reject anything that is not a strict descendant of that root. Compare with a trailing-separator-aware prefix test so a sibling directory sharing the root's name prefix is not accepted.\n3. Resolve symbolic links on both the candidate path and the root before the final comparison, so a symlink created inside the workspace cannot be used to reach outside it.\n4. Replace the hardcoded permission grant in the default filesystem wrapper with a real evaluation. If the intended gate is a user-facing one, wire it to an actual platform permission or approval rather than returning a constant.\n5. Raise `list_device_files` from Tier 1 (auto-execute) to at least Tier 2 so enumeration is no longer performed without evaluation; promote it to Tier 3 if enumeration of the private data directory should require explicit user approval. Note that the HITL sheet already renders the real argument JSON, so a Tier-3 promotion would show the user the exact directory being listed.\n6. Add regression tests that assert an absolute path outside the workspace, a traversal sequence, and a symlink escape are each rejected, and that an in-workspace path still succeeds."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-319",
              "name": "Credentials can be transmitted over cleartext HTTP/WS because endpoint validators accept http:// and ws:// URLs",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Credentials can be transmitted over cleartext HTTP/WS because endpoint validators accept http:// and ws:// URLs"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The app's endpoint validators accept cleartext `http://` and `ws://` URLs, and the stored credentials are then attached to whatever endpoint results: the LLM API key is sent as `Authorization: Bearer` to `$baseUrl/chat/completions` and to the TTS endpoint, and the gateway token is transmitted as the first WebSocket frame. The project's own deployment guide instructs users to configure plain-http LAN endpoints, so the credential traverses the local network unencrypted and is recoverable by anyone who can observe that segment. Critically, Android's cleartext policy does not prevent this: Flutter's HTTP/WebSocket stack is implemented in the Dart VM and never consults it, and Flutter's own equivalent gate is permissive by default on Android."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The app's endpoint validators accept cleartext `http://` and `ws://` URLs, and the stored credentials are then attached to whatever endpoint results: the LLM API key is sent as `Authorization: Bearer` to `$baseUrl/chat/completions` and to the TTS endpoint, and the gateway token is transmitted as the first WebSocket frame. The project's own deployment guide instructs users to configure plain-http LAN endpoints, so the credential traverses the local network unencrypted and is recoverable by anyone who can observe that segment. Critically, Android's cleartext policy does not prevent this: Flutter's HTTP/WebSocket stack is implemented in the Dart VM and never consults it, and Flutter's own equivalent gate is permissive by default on Android.\n\nA passive attacker positioned on the same network segment as the device — the home or office LAN that the project's own deployment guide targets with `http://192.168.1.100:8642/v1` — can read the bearer credential directly from the wire: the LLM API key from the `Authorization: Bearer` header of `/chat/completions` (and of the TTS request), or the Hermes gateway token from the first WebSocket frame. Either credential grants the holder the user's authority on that infrastructure: API spend and conversation access on the LLM endpoint, and device-tool invocation through the operator's gateway. Because the token is provisioned by scanning a QR code, an attacker who can present a QR code can also supply the cleartext `http://` gateway URL that produces the exposure. An on-path attacker able to modify the cleartext stream can additionally alter request and response content (integrity impact), though that requires active interception rather than passive observation. The exposure applies to every Android version the build supports, because the platform's cleartext restriction is not consulted by this application's network stack.\n\n1. Restrict the accepted gateway schemes to the TLS variants. `acceptedGatewayUrlSchemes` (lib/domain/services/gateway/gateway_url.dart:15) is the shared contract consulted by the profile form, the settings form and the gateway client, so narrowing it to `https` and `wss` closes those entry points in one change and `isAcceptableGatewayUrl` then rejects cleartext URLs before they can be stored.\n2. Reject the `http` scheme explicitly in the pairing parser (lib/domain/tools/pairing/pairing_parser.dart:53-56) so a scanned QR code can never select a cleartext transport, and update the parser's error message so it no longer advertises HTTP as acceptable.\n3. Apply the same TLS requirement to the two duplicated validators (lib/presentation/screen/settings/cubit/settings_cubit.dart:118-121, lib/presentation/screen/server_profiles/cubit/server_profile_cubit.dart:97-100) and to the settings form field validator (lib/presentation/screen/settings/settings_screen.dart:417-420), so the contract cannot drift between entry points.\n4. Migrate or reject already-persisted cleartext configuration. The validators only govern new input; a device that already stored an `http://` base URL will keep using it. Reject a non-TLS base URL when settings are read from secure storage (or clear the stored value once), so the credential can never be attached to a legacy cleartext endpoint.\n5. Do not rely on the Android network security policy for this issue. Declaring `android:networkSecurityConfig` with `cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"false\"` is worthwhile defence-in-depth for any Java-side plugin traffic, but it does not govern this application's HTTP or WebSocket stack, which does not consult it. The effective control is the scheme validation in the application code. (If the platform policy is added, it must be accompanied by the code change, or the credential path remains open.)\n6. Update the deployment documentation (docs/connecting-to-remote-hermes.md:161 and :176) to stop presenting `http://` endpoints as the standard option, and instead point at the TLS options already documented in the same guide (Cloudflare Tunnel, or an Nginx/Caddy TLS reverse proxy).\n7. Update the test suite to assert the new contract: the cases in test/domain/services/gateway/gateway_url_test.dart that accept `http://`/`ws://` become rejection cases, and add a case asserting that a cleartext profile is refused before any socket is opened.",
                "markdown": "The app's endpoint validators accept cleartext `http://` and `ws://` URLs, and the stored credentials are then attached to whatever endpoint results: the LLM API key is sent as `Authorization: Bearer` to `$baseUrl/chat/completions` and to the TTS endpoint, and the gateway token is transmitted as the first WebSocket frame. The project's own deployment guide instructs users to configure plain-http LAN endpoints, so the credential traverses the local network unencrypted and is recoverable by anyone who can observe that segment. Critically, Android's cleartext policy does not prevent this: Flutter's HTTP/WebSocket stack is implemented in the Dart VM and never consults it, and Flutter's own equivalent gate is permissive by default on Android.\n\nA passive attacker positioned on the same network segment as the device — the home or office LAN that the project's own deployment guide targets with `http://192.168.1.100:8642/v1` — can read the bearer credential directly from the wire: the LLM API key from the `Authorization: Bearer` header of `/chat/completions` (and of the TTS request), or the Hermes gateway token from the first WebSocket frame. Either credential grants the holder the user's authority on that infrastructure: API spend and conversation access on the LLM endpoint, and device-tool invocation through the operator's gateway. Because the token is provisioned by scanning a QR code, an attacker who can present a QR code can also supply the cleartext `http://` gateway URL that produces the exposure. An on-path attacker able to modify the cleartext stream can additionally alter request and response content (integrity impact), though that requires active interception rather than passive observation. The exposure applies to every Android version the build supports, because the platform's cleartext restriction is not consulted by this application's network stack.\n\n1. Restrict the accepted gateway schemes to the TLS variants. `acceptedGatewayUrlSchemes` (lib/domain/services/gateway/gateway_url.dart:15) is the shared contract consulted by the profile form, the settings form and the gateway client, so narrowing it to `https` and `wss` closes those entry points in one change and `isAcceptableGatewayUrl` then rejects cleartext URLs before they can be stored.\n2. Reject the `http` scheme explicitly in the pairing parser (lib/domain/tools/pairing/pairing_parser.dart:53-56) so a scanned QR code can never select a cleartext transport, and update the parser's error message so it no longer advertises HTTP as acceptable.\n3. Apply the same TLS requirement to the two duplicated validators (lib/presentation/screen/settings/cubit/settings_cubit.dart:118-121, lib/presentation/screen/server_profiles/cubit/server_profile_cubit.dart:97-100) and to the settings form field validator (lib/presentation/screen/settings/settings_screen.dart:417-420), so the contract cannot drift between entry points.\n4. Migrate or reject already-persisted cleartext configuration. The validators only govern new input; a device that already stored an `http://` base URL will keep using it. Reject a non-TLS base URL when settings are read from secure storage (or clear the stored value once), so the credential can never be attached to a legacy cleartext endpoint.\n5. Do not rely on the Android network security policy for this issue. Declaring `android:networkSecurityConfig` with `cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"false\"` is worthwhile defence-in-depth for any Java-side plugin traffic, but it does not govern this application's HTTP or WebSocket stack, which does not consult it. The effective control is the scheme validation in the application code. (If the platform policy is added, it must be accompanied by the code change, or the credential path remains open.)\n6. Update the deployment documentation (docs/connecting-to-remote-hermes.md:161 and :176) to stop presenting `http://` endpoints as the standard option, and instead point at the TLS options already documented in the same guide (Cloudflare Tunnel, or an Nginx/Caddy TLS reverse proxy).\n7. Update the test suite to assert the new contract: the cases in test/domain/services/gateway/gateway_url_test.dart that accept `http://`/`ws://` become rejection cases, and add a case asserting that a cleartext profile is refused before any socket is opened."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-319",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "name": "Notification text from any installed app reaches the agent prompt with no data/instruction separation and can drive auto-executing Tier-1 device tools",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Notification text from any installed app reaches the agent prompt with no data/instruction separation and can drive auto-executing Tier-1 device tools"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Flames reads the notification title and body of every app on the device through its notification-listener service and returns them to the language model as a tool result (read_recent_notifications, extract_latest_otp). Those strings are authored by whichever app posted the notification, not by Flames and not by the user, yet nothing on the path marks them as untrusted, delimits them, caps their length, or scans them. The app already ships a prompt-injection scanner but applies it only to imported skill text. The same model can then call tools that execute with no user confirmation, including clipboard_write, send_local_notification, save_memory and speak_text, and, because read_web_page is declared permissioned but registered without a permission checker, read_web_page, which can carry on-device data off the device in a URL. Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated — this is a source-only audit with no emulator, no device and no running app, so no payload was executed and no tool call was induced."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Flames reads the notification title and body of every app on the device through its notification-listener service and returns them to the language model as a tool result (read_recent_notifications, extract_latest_otp). Those strings are authored by whichever app posted the notification, not by Flames and not by the user, yet nothing on the path marks them as untrusted, delimits them, caps their length, or scans them. The app already ships a prompt-injection scanner but applies it only to imported skill text. The same model can then call tools that execute with no user confirmation, including clipboard_write, send_local_notification, save_memory and speak_text, and, because read_web_page is declared permissioned but registered without a permission checker, read_web_page, which can carry on-device data off the device in a URL. Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated — this is a source-only audit with no emulator, no device and no running app, so no payload was executed and no tool call was induced.\n\nA malicious or merely compromised app installed on the same device can post a notification whose title/body contains instructions, and those instructions enter the agent's context with the same standing as the user's own request. Because the reachable tools need no approval, the injected text can cause the device to copy attacker-chosen content to the clipboard (clipboard_write), display a forged notification (send_local_notification), speak text aloud (speak_text), write an entry into the memory vault (save_memory) — which is later re-injected as a role:\"system\" message on any turn whose prompt matches — or register a geofence (register_geofence). Combined with clipboard_read (Tier 1) and read_web_page (declared Tier 2 but registered without a permission checker, so it auto-executes), on-device content can be staged and then transmitted to an attacker-controlled URL in a query string, with no permission prompt and no human-in-the-loop step. The concrete end-to-end result is model- and payload-dependent and was not demonstrated. Tier-3 actions (make_phone_call, open_whatsapp_chat, calendar_create_event, schedule_reminder) remain behind the approval modal and were not bypassed.\n\nSeparate data from instructions on every channel that carries externally-authored text into the prompt, and do it at the point where the request is assembled rather than per tool.\n\n1. Envelope untrusted tool output before it is sent. When the message list is serialized for the provider, wrap the string content of every message whose role is \"tool\" in a fixed, unambiguous delimiter, and cap its length so a single hostile notification cannot flood the context window. Keying on the role rather than the tool name covers notification text, fetched web pages, contacts, the screen-reader tree, clipboard text and file contents in one place, including channels that are currently unframed.\n2. State the rule in every request. Add a short statement to the system prompt that text inside the envelope is external data, never an instruction, and must not change the task, the tool policy, or what is disclosed. Because the prompt builder currently returns an empty string when no skill is active, the rule must be added before that early return, or it will be absent in the default configuration.\n3. Treat the envelope as framing, not provenance. A payload can contain the literal delimiter text, so the prompt rule is the load-bearing control and the two changes should ship together.\n4. Do not rely on the existing content-topic filter. It matches banking and credential vocabulary, not instruction-like text, and provides no protection against this class. Reusing the existing prompt-injection scanner as an additional signal is reasonable defence in depth, but its patterns are literal and evadable by paraphrase, so it must not be the primary control.\n5. Apply the same treatment to recalled memory. Active memories are currently injected as a role \"system\" message, which gives attacker-influenced text the highest available authority; they should be framed the same way and injected as untrusted data rather than as a system instruction.\n6. Close the exfiltration path separately. Tools declared Tier 2 but registered without a permission checker (read_web_page, open_external_url, open_email_composer, launch_system_setting, camera_take_photo, nfc_read_tag, nfc_read_emoney_balance) currently skip permission evaluation entirely because the guard also tests that the checker is non-null. Require every Tier-2 registration to supply a checker, or fail closed when one is missing.\n7. Update the two tests that encode the pre-fix expectation of an empty system prompt in the no-skill case, since they assert the insecure behaviour.",
                "markdown": "Flames reads the notification title and body of every app on the device through its notification-listener service and returns them to the language model as a tool result (read_recent_notifications, extract_latest_otp). Those strings are authored by whichever app posted the notification, not by Flames and not by the user, yet nothing on the path marks them as untrusted, delimits them, caps their length, or scans them. The app already ships a prompt-injection scanner but applies it only to imported skill text. The same model can then call tools that execute with no user confirmation, including clipboard_write, send_local_notification, save_memory and speak_text, and, because read_web_page is declared permissioned but registered without a permission checker, read_web_page, which can carry on-device data off the device in a URL. Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated — this is a source-only audit with no emulator, no device and no running app, so no payload was executed and no tool call was induced.\n\nA malicious or merely compromised app installed on the same device can post a notification whose title/body contains instructions, and those instructions enter the agent's context with the same standing as the user's own request. Because the reachable tools need no approval, the injected text can cause the device to copy attacker-chosen content to the clipboard (clipboard_write), display a forged notification (send_local_notification), speak text aloud (speak_text), write an entry into the memory vault (save_memory) — which is later re-injected as a role:\"system\" message on any turn whose prompt matches — or register a geofence (register_geofence). Combined with clipboard_read (Tier 1) and read_web_page (declared Tier 2 but registered without a permission checker, so it auto-executes), on-device content can be staged and then transmitted to an attacker-controlled URL in a query string, with no permission prompt and no human-in-the-loop step. The concrete end-to-end result is model- and payload-dependent and was not demonstrated. Tier-3 actions (make_phone_call, open_whatsapp_chat, calendar_create_event, schedule_reminder) remain behind the approval modal and were not bypassed.\n\nSeparate data from instructions on every channel that carries externally-authored text into the prompt, and do it at the point where the request is assembled rather than per tool.\n\n1. Envelope untrusted tool output before it is sent. When the message list is serialized for the provider, wrap the string content of every message whose role is \"tool\" in a fixed, unambiguous delimiter, and cap its length so a single hostile notification cannot flood the context window. Keying on the role rather than the tool name covers notification text, fetched web pages, contacts, the screen-reader tree, clipboard text and file contents in one place, including channels that are currently unframed.\n2. State the rule in every request. Add a short statement to the system prompt that text inside the envelope is external data, never an instruction, and must not change the task, the tool policy, or what is disclosed. Because the prompt builder currently returns an empty string when no skill is active, the rule must be added before that early return, or it will be absent in the default configuration.\n3. Treat the envelope as framing, not provenance. A payload can contain the literal delimiter text, so the prompt rule is the load-bearing control and the two changes should ship together.\n4. Do not rely on the existing content-topic filter. It matches banking and credential vocabulary, not instruction-like text, and provides no protection against this class. Reusing the existing prompt-injection scanner as an additional signal is reasonable defence in depth, but its patterns are literal and evadable by paraphrase, so it must not be the primary control.\n5. Apply the same treatment to recalled memory. Active memories are currently injected as a role \"system\" message, which gives attacker-influenced text the highest available authority; they should be framed the same way and injected as untrusted data rather than as a system instruction.\n6. Close the exfiltration path separately. Tools declared Tier 2 but registered without a permission checker (read_web_page, open_external_url, open_email_composer, launch_system_setting, camera_take_photo, nfc_read_tag, nfc_read_emoney_balance) currently skip permission evaluation entirely because the guard also tests that the checker is non-null. Require every Tier-2 registration to supply a checker, or fail closed when one is missing.\n7. Update the two tests that encode the pre-fix expectation of an empty system prompt in the no-skill case, since they assert the insecure behaviour."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-940",
              "name": "Any co-resident app can auto-submit an agent turn through the exported share-target activity, with no user confirmation and an attacker-chosen URL fetch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Any co-resident app can auto-submit an agent turn through the exported share-target activity, with no user confirmation and an attacker-chosen URL fetch"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The exported, non-permission-guarded `MainActivity` of the Flames Android app declares `ACTION_SEND` / `ACTION_SEND_MULTIPLE` share-target filters. Text delivered through that channel is consumed by the `receive_sharing_intent` plugin and routed into the chat pipeline, which **submits it to the on-device agent as a user turn with no confirmation step of any kind**. Any installed app — requiring no Android permissions — can therefore drive a full agent turn (a request to the configured LLM endpoint plus the on-device ReAct tool loop). When the shared text contains an `http`/`https` URL, the app also issues that request from the victim's device before submitting, and the URL host is not validated beyond scheme and non-empty host, so loopback, RFC1918 and link-local addresses are reachable. **This finding is unconfirmed and not dynamically validated: the assessment was source-only, with no emulator, device or `adb` available.**"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The exported, non-permission-guarded `MainActivity` of the Flames Android app declares `ACTION_SEND` / `ACTION_SEND_MULTIPLE` share-target filters. Text delivered through that channel is consumed by the `receive_sharing_intent` plugin and routed into the chat pipeline, which **submits it to the on-device agent as a user turn with no confirmation step of any kind**. Any installed app — requiring no Android permissions — can therefore drive a full agent turn (a request to the configured LLM endpoint plus the on-device ReAct tool loop). When the shared text contains an `http`/`https` URL, the app also issues that request from the victim's device before submitting, and the URL host is not validated beyond scheme and non-empty host, so loopback, RFC1918 and link-local addresses are reachable. **This finding is unconfirmed and not dynamically validated: the assessment was source-only, with no emulator, device or `adb` available.**\n\nA co-resident app with no permissions and no special access can cause the Flames agent to run a turn that the user never initiated, and can choose a URL that the device then fetches.\n\n1. **Unattended agent turn.** `AgentCubit.sendMessage` is invoked with attacker-authored text (`chat_screen.dart:1505`, `:1522`). The turn is persisted to the chat session, sent to the configured remote LLM endpoint, and drives the ReAct tool loop (`agent_cubit.dart:502-536`, `:864`). The attacker's text arrives as an ordinary user turn with no provenance marking, so the model has no basis to treat it as untrusted. Consequences are bounded by the existing tier gates: Tier-3 tools still stop at the human-in-the-loop modal, and the agent's reply is rendered in the app's own chat view. The demonstrable consequence is unauthorized consumption of the user's paid LLM quota and an unattended tool loop operating on the user's behalf, with the attacker's text as the instruction source.\n\n2. **Attacker-chosen outbound request.** When the shared text contains a URL, the app fetches it from the device before submitting (`chat_screen.dart:541-542`). The only validation is scheme (`http`/`https`) plus non-empty host (`lib/domain/tools/chat/url_reader_service.dart:59-70`); there is no loopback, RFC1918, link-local or hostname restriction, and `followRedirects: true` means a redirect can move the target after the check. `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/`, `http://192.168.x.x/`, `http://10.x.x.x/` and `http://169.254.169.254/` all pass. The response body is reduced to text and embedded in the prompt that is sent to the LLM (`shared_payload.dart:74-81`), so content from an internal endpoint that the attacker cannot reach directly can be pulled into the model context. This is a blind primitive — the attacker does not receive the response — and it is a client-side fetch, not a server-side one, so it is reported as a secondary aspect of this finding rather than as a standalone high-severity issue.\n\n3. **Reachability and visibility.** `MainActivity` is `launchMode=\"singleTop\"`, so when Flames is already running the payload is delivered to the live instance via the plugin's `NewIntentListener` without a restart. When Flames is not in the foreground, starting the activity brings the Flames window to the front, so the launch itself is visible; the turn that follows is not announced. The attacker cannot hide the window, and the reported severity reflects that.\n\nTreat content arriving through a share intent as untrusted input that requires explicit user action, rather than as a message the user sent.\n\n1. Remove the automatic submission. When a share payload arrives, stage the generated prompt in the chat composer and let the user review and send it themselves; do not call the submit path from the share handler. This single change removes both the unattended agent turn and the unattended fetch, because the fetch happens inside the same handler.\n2. Keep the user-visible notification that content was staged, so the feature remains discoverable and the user understands what was added to the composer.\n3. Apply the same treatment to the cold-start path and the live path, since both reach the same handler.\n4. Independently of the share flow, restrict the URL reader's destination policy: reject loopback, link-local, and private-range addresses, resolve the host and re-check after each redirect, and disable automatic redirect following so that the destination cannot change after validation. This protects the `read_web_page` agent tool as well, which shares the same reader and the same gap.\n5. Where a shared payload is later shown to the model, mark it as externally supplied content so the model can distinguish it from user instructions; do not rely on the surrounding prose for that signal.\n6. Add a regression test asserting that delivering a share payload does not reach the message-submission path without a user action.",
                "markdown": "The exported, non-permission-guarded `MainActivity` of the Flames Android app declares `ACTION_SEND` / `ACTION_SEND_MULTIPLE` share-target filters. Text delivered through that channel is consumed by the `receive_sharing_intent` plugin and routed into the chat pipeline, which **submits it to the on-device agent as a user turn with no confirmation step of any kind**. Any installed app — requiring no Android permissions — can therefore drive a full agent turn (a request to the configured LLM endpoint plus the on-device ReAct tool loop). When the shared text contains an `http`/`https` URL, the app also issues that request from the victim's device before submitting, and the URL host is not validated beyond scheme and non-empty host, so loopback, RFC1918 and link-local addresses are reachable. **This finding is unconfirmed and not dynamically validated: the assessment was source-only, with no emulator, device or `adb` available.**\n\nA co-resident app with no permissions and no special access can cause the Flames agent to run a turn that the user never initiated, and can choose a URL that the device then fetches.\n\n1. **Unattended agent turn.** `AgentCubit.sendMessage` is invoked with attacker-authored text (`chat_screen.dart:1505`, `:1522`). The turn is persisted to the chat session, sent to the configured remote LLM endpoint, and drives the ReAct tool loop (`agent_cubit.dart:502-536`, `:864`). The attacker's text arrives as an ordinary user turn with no provenance marking, so the model has no basis to treat it as untrusted. Consequences are bounded by the existing tier gates: Tier-3 tools still stop at the human-in-the-loop modal, and the agent's reply is rendered in the app's own chat view. The demonstrable consequence is unauthorized consumption of the user's paid LLM quota and an unattended tool loop operating on the user's behalf, with the attacker's text as the instruction source.\n\n2. **Attacker-chosen outbound request.** When the shared text contains a URL, the app fetches it from the device before submitting (`chat_screen.dart:541-542`). The only validation is scheme (`http`/`https`) plus non-empty host (`lib/domain/tools/chat/url_reader_service.dart:59-70`); there is no loopback, RFC1918, link-local or hostname restriction, and `followRedirects: true` means a redirect can move the target after the check. `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/`, `http://192.168.x.x/`, `http://10.x.x.x/` and `http://169.254.169.254/` all pass. The response body is reduced to text and embedded in the prompt that is sent to the LLM (`shared_payload.dart:74-81`), so content from an internal endpoint that the attacker cannot reach directly can be pulled into the model context. This is a blind primitive — the attacker does not receive the response — and it is a client-side fetch, not a server-side one, so it is reported as a secondary aspect of this finding rather than as a standalone high-severity issue.\n\n3. **Reachability and visibility.** `MainActivity` is `launchMode=\"singleTop\"`, so when Flames is already running the payload is delivered to the live instance via the plugin's `NewIntentListener` without a restart. When Flames is not in the foreground, starting the activity brings the Flames window to the front, so the launch itself is visible; the turn that follows is not announced. The attacker cannot hide the window, and the reported severity reflects that.\n\nTreat content arriving through a share intent as untrusted input that requires explicit user action, rather than as a message the user sent.\n\n1. Remove the automatic submission. When a share payload arrives, stage the generated prompt in the chat composer and let the user review and send it themselves; do not call the submit path from the share handler. This single change removes both the unattended agent turn and the unattended fetch, because the fetch happens inside the same handler.\n2. Keep the user-visible notification that content was staged, so the feature remains discoverable and the user understands what was added to the composer.\n3. Apply the same treatment to the cold-start path and the live path, since both reach the same handler.\n4. Independently of the share flow, restrict the URL reader's destination policy: reject loopback, link-local, and private-range addresses, resolve the host and re-check after each redirect, and disable automatic redirect following so that the destination cannot change after validation. This protects the `read_web_page` agent tool as well, which shares the same reader and the same gap.\n5. Where a shared payload is later shown to the model, mark it as externally supplied content so the model can distinguish it from user instructions; do not rely on the surrounding prose for that signal.\n6. Add a regression test asserting that delivering a share payload does not reach the message-submission path without a user action."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-940",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/940.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-346",
              "name": "Attacker-controlled QR pairing payload becomes the device's active gateway with no destination validation or origin confirmation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Attacker-controlled QR pairing payload becomes the device's active gateway with no destination validation or origin confirmation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Flames Android app pairs with a gateway by scanning a QR code (or pasting the same JSON into a manual-entry dialog). The payload is entirely attacker-controlled and its `gatewayUrl` field becomes the device's active gateway destination with only a scheme check — any `http`/`https` host is accepted, no allowlist, no same-origin check and no rejection of URL user-info. The app then persists the destination and immediately connects and authenticates to it, showing the user only a success message containing attacker-chosen text. The chosen origin gains the device's remote tool-execution channel (arbitrary readable file access, clipboard, notifications/OTP, screen tree) and can invoke ungated side-effecting tools."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Flames Android app pairs with a gateway by scanning a QR code (or pasting the same JSON into a manual-entry dialog). The payload is entirely attacker-controlled and its `gatewayUrl` field becomes the device's active gateway destination with only a scheme check — any `http`/`https` host is accepted, no allowlist, no same-origin check and no rejection of URL user-info. The app then persists the destination and immediately connects and authenticates to it, showing the user only a success message containing attacker-chosen text. The chosen origin gains the device's remote tool-execution channel (arbitrary readable file access, clipboard, notifications/OTP, screen tree) and can invoke ungated side-effecting tools.\n\nA hostile QR code (printed, pasted into a chat, or handed to the user as \"pair with your gateway\") silently makes an attacker-controlled origin the device's active gateway. After pairing, that origin receives the device's full agent tool manifest and can issue tool-call frames that the app executes: `read_device_file` reads any path the app can read (its permission check is hardcoded to allow), `clipboard_read` reads the clipboard, `read_recent_notifications` and `extract_latest_otp` read notification content and one-time passcodes, `ui_find_elements` reads the foreground screen's accessibility tree, and several Tier-1 tools perform ungated device side effects (clipboard write, memory write/delete, local notifications, geofence, background service). Pairing also overwrites the stored `llm_base_url`/`llm_api_key` pair, so the app's own LLM and text-to-speech traffic is repointed to the attacker origin. The user is never shown which origin they paired with. The user's previously stored LLM API key is not itself transmitted to the attacker origin, because the base URL and key are always rewritten together from the same payload; a leak of a key the user re-enters afterwards is possible but requires that later user action.\n\n1. Require explicit user confirmation of the destination origin before a pairing payload is persisted or connected. Before calling the profile cubit, display the resolved origin (scheme, host and port as they will actually be contacted) and require an affirmative action; abort pairing on cancel. The confirmation must render the host that the socket will use, not the raw payload string, so user-info confusion is visible.\n\n2. Reject payloads whose `gatewayUrl` carries user-info. In `PairingParser.parse`, after parsing the URI, fail when the user-info component is non-empty. No legitimate gateway URL in this codebase uses user-info — the credential travels in the authentication frame — and rejecting it removes the destination-confusion class entirely.\n\n3. Decide and enforce the transport policy for pairing deliberately. Pairing currently accepts `http` by design, which is exercised by the existing tests that treat `http://192.168.1.50:18789` as valid; a blanket HTTPS-only rule would break the intended LAN pairing flow and must therefore be a product decision rather than a silent parser change. If plaintext LAN pairing is retained, restrict it explicitly to private-range hosts and require HTTPS/WSS for any other destination, so a hostile public origin cannot be paired over cleartext.\n\n4. Apply the same validation to the manual paste entry point and to the Settings entry point, so a pasted payload cannot bypass the confirmation added for the camera flow.\n\n5. Treat the gateway destination as a security-sensitive setting: when an incoming payload would change an already-configured active destination, require re-confirmation and surface a prominent notice identifying the previous and new origins.\n\n6. Consider constraining the authority granted to a newly paired gateway. The remote tool channel currently exposes arbitrary readable file access, clipboard content, notification/OTP content and the foreground screen tree to whichever origin authenticated; scoping those tools per-profile, or requiring in-app approval for the highest-impact tools regardless of tier, limits the blast radius of any future pairing compromise.",
                "markdown": "The Flames Android app pairs with a gateway by scanning a QR code (or pasting the same JSON into a manual-entry dialog). The payload is entirely attacker-controlled and its `gatewayUrl` field becomes the device's active gateway destination with only a scheme check — any `http`/`https` host is accepted, no allowlist, no same-origin check and no rejection of URL user-info. The app then persists the destination and immediately connects and authenticates to it, showing the user only a success message containing attacker-chosen text. The chosen origin gains the device's remote tool-execution channel (arbitrary readable file access, clipboard, notifications/OTP, screen tree) and can invoke ungated side-effecting tools.\n\nA hostile QR code (printed, pasted into a chat, or handed to the user as \"pair with your gateway\") silently makes an attacker-controlled origin the device's active gateway. After pairing, that origin receives the device's full agent tool manifest and can issue tool-call frames that the app executes: `read_device_file` reads any path the app can read (its permission check is hardcoded to allow), `clipboard_read` reads the clipboard, `read_recent_notifications` and `extract_latest_otp` read notification content and one-time passcodes, `ui_find_elements` reads the foreground screen's accessibility tree, and several Tier-1 tools perform ungated device side effects (clipboard write, memory write/delete, local notifications, geofence, background service). Pairing also overwrites the stored `llm_base_url`/`llm_api_key` pair, so the app's own LLM and text-to-speech traffic is repointed to the attacker origin. The user is never shown which origin they paired with. The user's previously stored LLM API key is not itself transmitted to the attacker origin, because the base URL and key are always rewritten together from the same payload; a leak of a key the user re-enters afterwards is possible but requires that later user action.\n\n1. Require explicit user confirmation of the destination origin before a pairing payload is persisted or connected. Before calling the profile cubit, display the resolved origin (scheme, host and port as they will actually be contacted) and require an affirmative action; abort pairing on cancel. The confirmation must render the host that the socket will use, not the raw payload string, so user-info confusion is visible.\n\n2. Reject payloads whose `gatewayUrl` carries user-info. In `PairingParser.parse`, after parsing the URI, fail when the user-info component is non-empty. No legitimate gateway URL in this codebase uses user-info — the credential travels in the authentication frame — and rejecting it removes the destination-confusion class entirely.\n\n3. Decide and enforce the transport policy for pairing deliberately. Pairing currently accepts `http` by design, which is exercised by the existing tests that treat `http://192.168.1.50:18789` as valid; a blanket HTTPS-only rule would break the intended LAN pairing flow and must therefore be a product decision rather than a silent parser change. If plaintext LAN pairing is retained, restrict it explicitly to private-range hosts and require HTTPS/WSS for any other destination, so a hostile public origin cannot be paired over cleartext.\n\n4. Apply the same validation to the manual paste entry point and to the Settings entry point, so a pasted payload cannot bypass the confirmation added for the camera flow.\n\n5. Treat the gateway destination as a security-sensitive setting: when an incoming payload would change an already-configured active destination, require re-confirmation and surface a prominent notice identifying the previous and new origins.\n\n6. Consider constraining the authority granted to a newly paired gateway. The remote tool channel currently exposes arbitrary readable file access, clipboard content, notification/OTP content and the foreground screen tree to whichever origin authenticated; scoping those tools per-profile, or requiring in-app approval for the highest-impact tools regardless of tier, limits the blast radius of any future pairing compromise."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-346",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/346.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-918",
              "name": "SSRF via the `read_web_page` agent tool: unrestricted URL fetch reachable from model output and from an inbound gateway RPC frame, with no permission gate and no host validation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SSRF via the `read_web_page` agent tool: unrestricted URL fetch reachable from model output and from an inbound gateway RPC frame, with no permission gate and no host validation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The agent tool `read_web_page` fetches any `http`/`https` URL chosen by the language model **or supplied in an inbound gateway RPC frame** and returns the page body to the caller, but it is registered as a permissioned tool without a permission check and its URL validator accepts loopback, private, link-local, and cloud-metadata addresses. Because the ReAct loop auto-executes every non-Tier-3 tool and the gateway RPC receiver gates only Tier 3, a URL that reaches the device as data — a poisoned web page, a notification body, a shared link, a compromised gateway, or a directly dispatched frame — can steer the device into fetching internal services that are not reachable from the public internet. On the gateway path the response is returned to the frame's sender over the same socket; on the ReAct path it is forwarded to the configured LLM API.\n\n**Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated.** This assessment is source-only: no emulator, no `adb`, and no running application instance were available. Every claim below is supported by code at a named location and, where noted, by executing the exact validator logic in a standalone Dart runtime; the end-to-end request was never issued from the application."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The agent tool `read_web_page` fetches any `http`/`https` URL chosen by the language model **or supplied in an inbound gateway RPC frame** and returns the page body to the caller, but it is registered as a permissioned tool without a permission check and its URL validator accepts loopback, private, link-local, and cloud-metadata addresses. Because the ReAct loop auto-executes every non-Tier-3 tool and the gateway RPC receiver gates only Tier 3, a URL that reaches the device as data — a poisoned web page, a notification body, a shared link, a compromised gateway, or a directly dispatched frame — can steer the device into fetching internal services that are not reachable from the public internet. On the gateway path the response is returned to the frame's sender over the same socket; on the ReAct path it is forwarded to the configured LLM API.\n\n**Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated.** This assessment is source-only: no emulator, no `adb`, and no running application instance were available. Every claim below is supported by code at a named location and, where noted, by executing the exact validator logic in a standalone Dart runtime; the end-to-end request was never issued from the application.\n\nA party who can influence model input — by publishing a page the agent is asked to summarise, sending a notification the user has granted the app access to read, or controlling the configured model endpoint — can make the device issue HTTP(S) requests to addresses of their choosing and have the responses returned into the conversation, where they are forwarded to the configured LLM API.\n\nA party who can send frames on the paired gateway connection obtains a stronger version of the same primitive. The frame names the tool and carries the destination URL, no approval is requested, and the fetched body is written back to the sender over the same socket. That removes the model from the loop entirely: the attacker does not have to induce a tool call and does not depend on a third-party model endpoint to observe the result. The device is additionally told to advertise the capability — the tool manifest sent after a successful handshake lists `read_web_page` because the manifest provider applies an enablement filter and no pruning.\n\nThe concrete targets that become reachable from the device are the ones the threat model treats as internal: the on-device Hermes gateway and API server (`http://localhost:8642`, `http://127.0.0.1:8644/rpc`), any RFC1918 host on the user's LAN, and link-local cloud metadata services. The Hermes gateway is the component that holds the operator's LLM API key, so an SSRF that can read it is a credential-access primitive, not just a reachability bug. On the ReAct path, fetched content is additionally disclosed to whichever LLM provider the app is configured against, which is an unanticipated egress path for internal response bodies.\n\nThe demonstrated consequence is unauthorized outbound request capability and information flow, with the response returned to the caller on the gateway path. The specific contents readable from each internal service were not observed, so the confidentiality impact is assessed as limited rather than high.\n\nEnforce the destination policy in the reader itself, so that every caller — the ReAct loop, the gateway RPC receiver, and the share-intent prefetch — is covered by one control rather than by per-caller checks.\n\n1. Add a non-public-address policy to the URL validator: reject loopback, RFC1918, carrier-grade NAT, link-local, multicast, unspecified, and IPv6 unique-local and IPv4-mapped addresses, together with `localhost`, `.local`, `.internal`, `.home.arpa`, and trailing-dot variants, plus decimal, hexadecimal, and octal spellings of IPv4 addresses. Apply it after host lower-casing and dot-trimming so the comparison cannot be evaded by case or a trailing dot.\n2. Stop letting the HTTP client follow redirects. Follow them manually with a small hop limit and re-run the full validator on every hop, resolving each `Location` against the current URL before checking it. This closes the redirect-to-internal bypass and bounds redirect loops.\n3. Declare the tool as Tier 3 rather than Tier 2. A network fetch of an arbitrary destination is a state-changing egress action, and the current Tier-2 classification is only nominal because no runtime permission exists to check; routing it through the existing human-in-the-loop modal makes the destination visible to the user before the request is issued.\n4. Where a permission check is genuinely appropriate, treat a null `checkPermission` on a permissioned tool as a failure rather than a pass, so that a tier declaration cannot be silently neutralized by omitting the callback.\n5. Add regression tests covering the private-range, metadata, IPv4-mapped IPv6, alternative-notation, and redirect cases, alongside the existing scheme and empty-input tests.\n\nNote that restricting the tool to public destinations changes behaviour for users who legitimately point the agent at a LAN service; if that use case must be preserved, gate it behind an explicit opt-in allowlist rather than leaving the destination unrestricted.",
                "markdown": "The agent tool `read_web_page` fetches any `http`/`https` URL chosen by the language model **or supplied in an inbound gateway RPC frame** and returns the page body to the caller, but it is registered as a permissioned tool without a permission check and its URL validator accepts loopback, private, link-local, and cloud-metadata addresses. Because the ReAct loop auto-executes every non-Tier-3 tool and the gateway RPC receiver gates only Tier 3, a URL that reaches the device as data — a poisoned web page, a notification body, a shared link, a compromised gateway, or a directly dispatched frame — can steer the device into fetching internal services that are not reachable from the public internet. On the gateway path the response is returned to the frame's sender over the same socket; on the ReAct path it is forwarded to the configured LLM API.\n\n**Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated.** This assessment is source-only: no emulator, no `adb`, and no running application instance were available. Every claim below is supported by code at a named location and, where noted, by executing the exact validator logic in a standalone Dart runtime; the end-to-end request was never issued from the application.\n\nA party who can influence model input — by publishing a page the agent is asked to summarise, sending a notification the user has granted the app access to read, or controlling the configured model endpoint — can make the device issue HTTP(S) requests to addresses of their choosing and have the responses returned into the conversation, where they are forwarded to the configured LLM API.\n\nA party who can send frames on the paired gateway connection obtains a stronger version of the same primitive. The frame names the tool and carries the destination URL, no approval is requested, and the fetched body is written back to the sender over the same socket. That removes the model from the loop entirely: the attacker does not have to induce a tool call and does not depend on a third-party model endpoint to observe the result. The device is additionally told to advertise the capability — the tool manifest sent after a successful handshake lists `read_web_page` because the manifest provider applies an enablement filter and no pruning.\n\nThe concrete targets that become reachable from the device are the ones the threat model treats as internal: the on-device Hermes gateway and API server (`http://localhost:8642`, `http://127.0.0.1:8644/rpc`), any RFC1918 host on the user's LAN, and link-local cloud metadata services. The Hermes gateway is the component that holds the operator's LLM API key, so an SSRF that can read it is a credential-access primitive, not just a reachability bug. On the ReAct path, fetched content is additionally disclosed to whichever LLM provider the app is configured against, which is an unanticipated egress path for internal response bodies.\n\nThe demonstrated consequence is unauthorized outbound request capability and information flow, with the response returned to the caller on the gateway path. The specific contents readable from each internal service were not observed, so the confidentiality impact is assessed as limited rather than high.\n\nEnforce the destination policy in the reader itself, so that every caller — the ReAct loop, the gateway RPC receiver, and the share-intent prefetch — is covered by one control rather than by per-caller checks.\n\n1. Add a non-public-address policy to the URL validator: reject loopback, RFC1918, carrier-grade NAT, link-local, multicast, unspecified, and IPv6 unique-local and IPv4-mapped addresses, together with `localhost`, `.local`, `.internal`, `.home.arpa`, and trailing-dot variants, plus decimal, hexadecimal, and octal spellings of IPv4 addresses. Apply it after host lower-casing and dot-trimming so the comparison cannot be evaded by case or a trailing dot.\n2. Stop letting the HTTP client follow redirects. Follow them manually with a small hop limit and re-run the full validator on every hop, resolving each `Location` against the current URL before checking it. This closes the redirect-to-internal bypass and bounds redirect loops.\n3. Declare the tool as Tier 3 rather than Tier 2. A network fetch of an arbitrary destination is a state-changing egress action, and the current Tier-2 classification is only nominal because no runtime permission exists to check; routing it through the existing human-in-the-loop modal makes the destination visible to the user before the request is issued.\n4. Where a permission check is genuinely appropriate, treat a null `checkPermission` on a permissioned tool as a failure rather than a pass, so that a tier declaration cannot be silently neutralized by omitting the callback.\n5. Add regression tests covering the private-range, metadata, IPv4-mapped IPv6, alternative-notation, and redirect cases, alongside the existing scheme and empty-input tests.\n\nNote that restricting the tool to public destinations changes behaviour for users who legitimately point the agent at a LAN service; if that use case must be preserved, gate it behind an explicit opt-in allowlist rather than leaving the destination unrestricted."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918",
                  "stride:T",
