            {
              "id": "CWE-306",
              "name": "Gateway WebSocket dispatches privileged tool-call frames before authentication completes",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Gateway WebSocket dispatches privileged tool-call frames before authentication completes"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Hermes gateway WebSocket client dispatches action-bearing frames to the device RPC receiver regardless of whether the authentication handshake has completed. `_handleIncomingData` intercepts only the auth success/failure frames while the connection is in the `connecting` state; every other frame type falls through to a dispatcher that contains no state check. A peer that answers the configured gateway origin can therefore drive device tool execution for the duration of the authentication timeout without ever presenting the gateway token. The client's own \"authenticated\" state is likewise asserted by the peer, not proven, because the success frame carries no nonce, signature, or channel binding. Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated: no emulator, device, or running gateway was available, so no frame was delivered to a running application."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Hermes gateway WebSocket client dispatches action-bearing frames to the device RPC receiver regardless of whether the authentication handshake has completed. `_handleIncomingData` intercepts only the auth success/failure frames while the connection is in the `connecting` state; every other frame type falls through to a dispatcher that contains no state check. A peer that answers the configured gateway origin can therefore drive device tool execution for the duration of the authentication timeout without ever presenting the gateway token. The client's own \"authenticated\" state is likewise asserted by the peer, not proven, because the success frame carries no nonce, signature, or channel binding. Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated: no emulator, device, or running gateway was available, so no frame was delivered to a running application.\n\nA peer positioned to answer the configured gateway endpoint — an on-path attacker, or the operator of an attacker-chosen gateway destination obtained through the QR pairing path — can issue `tool_call_request` frames that reach `GatewayDeviceRpcReceiver.handleRequest` and execute device tools without ever supplying the bearer token. Because only Tier 3 tools are gated by human approval on this path, every Tier 1 and Tier 2 tool is reachable: the demonstrated consequences in this repository include arbitrary-path file reads (`read_device_file`, `list_device_files`), notification and OTP extraction, clipboard reads, and an unrestricted outbound URL fetch (`read_web_page`). The window is not one-shot: the authentication timer is 10 seconds by default, automatic reconnection is enabled with up to ten attempts, and each reconnect re-opens the same window on the same stored endpoint. The net effect is that the token check is advisory rather than a gate — it determines when the client considers itself connected, not what the peer is permitted to make the device do.\n\n1. Make the inbound dispatcher state-aware. Add an explicit authenticated-state guard to the frame dispatcher in `GatewayClient._handleIncomingData` so that action-bearing frames are only dispatched once the connection has reached the authenticated state. The existing \"If connected:\" comment describes the intended precondition; enforce it in code rather than in a comment.\n2. Fail closed for the whole pre-authentication period. While the connection is in the connecting state, drop (or reply to with an error) every frame other than the authentication result and the liveness frames the handshake requires. Do not rely on the authentication timeout to bound the exposure, because the window re-opens on every reconnect.\n3. Bind the authentication result to the request. Have the gateway echo a client-generated nonce (or sign the authentication result with the shared secret) so that a peer answering the socket cannot assert a successful authentication it did not perform. At minimum, treat the success frame as advisory and keep the tool-call path closed until a challenge has been answered.\n4. Keep the RPC listener aligned with the authenticated state. The listener is currently started for every non-disconnected state; it should not accept requests before authentication, so that the transport-level guard is not the only control.\n5. Add a regression test that delivers a `tool_call_request` frame before `auth_success` and asserts that no tool is executed and no response frame is sent.",
                "markdown": "The Hermes gateway WebSocket client dispatches action-bearing frames to the device RPC receiver regardless of whether the authentication handshake has completed. `_handleIncomingData` intercepts only the auth success/failure frames while the connection is in the `connecting` state; every other frame type falls through to a dispatcher that contains no state check. A peer that answers the configured gateway origin can therefore drive device tool execution for the duration of the authentication timeout without ever presenting the gateway token. The client's own \"authenticated\" state is likewise asserted by the peer, not proven, because the success frame carries no nonce, signature, or channel binding. Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated: no emulator, device, or running gateway was available, so no frame was delivered to a running application.\n\nA peer positioned to answer the configured gateway endpoint — an on-path attacker, or the operator of an attacker-chosen gateway destination obtained through the QR pairing path — can issue `tool_call_request` frames that reach `GatewayDeviceRpcReceiver.handleRequest` and execute device tools without ever supplying the bearer token. Because only Tier 3 tools are gated by human approval on this path, every Tier 1 and Tier 2 tool is reachable: the demonstrated consequences in this repository include arbitrary-path file reads (`read_device_file`, `list_device_files`), notification and OTP extraction, clipboard reads, and an unrestricted outbound URL fetch (`read_web_page`). The window is not one-shot: the authentication timer is 10 seconds by default, automatic reconnection is enabled with up to ten attempts, and each reconnect re-opens the same window on the same stored endpoint. The net effect is that the token check is advisory rather than a gate — it determines when the client considers itself connected, not what the peer is permitted to make the device do.\n\n1. Make the inbound dispatcher state-aware. Add an explicit authenticated-state guard to the frame dispatcher in `GatewayClient._handleIncomingData` so that action-bearing frames are only dispatched once the connection has reached the authenticated state. The existing \"If connected:\" comment describes the intended precondition; enforce it in code rather than in a comment.\n2. Fail closed for the whole pre-authentication period. While the connection is in the connecting state, drop (or reply to with an error) every frame other than the authentication result and the liveness frames the handshake requires. Do not rely on the authentication timeout to bound the exposure, because the window re-opens on every reconnect.\n3. Bind the authentication result to the request. Have the gateway echo a client-generated nonce (or sign the authentication result with the shared secret) so that a peer answering the socket cannot assert a successful authentication it did not perform. At minimum, treat the success frame as advisory and keep the tool-call path closed until a challenge has been answered.\n4. Keep the RPC listener aligned with the authenticated state. The listener is currently started for every non-disconnected state; it should not accept requests before authentication, so that the transport-level guard is not the only control.\n5. Add a regression test that delivers a `tool_call_request` frame before `auth_success` and asserts that no tool is executed and no response frame is sent."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
