            {
              "id": "CWE-522",
              "name": "Gateway bearer token disclosed to an arbitrary origin via WebSocket handshake redirect",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Gateway bearer token disclosed to an arbitrary origin via WebSocket handshake redirect"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The app connects to the Hermes Gateway with `WebSocket.connect()` and immediately writes an `auth` frame containing the stored gateway bearer token. The dart:io WebSocket handshake is an ordinary `GET` issued by a shared `HttpClient` whose request objects default to `followRedirects = true` with up to 5 redirects. A `301`/`302`/`303`/`307`/`308` response to the handshake is therefore followed automatically, the upgrade is completed against the redirect target, and the credential frame is written to that socket. The client never checks that the origin it finally connected to is the origin it was configured with. Reproduced against a local redirect server: the token frame arrived at the redirect target, including a `wss://` to `http://` cleartext downgrade."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The app connects to the Hermes Gateway with `WebSocket.connect()` and immediately writes an `auth` frame containing the stored gateway bearer token. The dart:io WebSocket handshake is an ordinary `GET` issued by a shared `HttpClient` whose request objects default to `followRedirects = true` with up to 5 redirects. A `301`/`302`/`303`/`307`/`308` response to the handshake is therefore followed automatically, the upgrade is completed against the redirect target, and the credential frame is written to that socket. The client never checks that the origin it finally connected to is the origin it was configured with. Reproduced against a local redirect server: the token frame arrived at the redirect target, including a `wss://` to `http://` cleartext downgrade.\n\nThe stored gateway bearer token is disclosed to whoever controls the HTTP response to the handshake for the configured origin. Because the same value is reused as the LLM API key, the disclosed credential also authenticates the OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint. The disclosure is silent (no user-visible error, the socket reports as connected) and is independent of TLS: a `wss://` profile can be redirected to a cleartext `http://` listener, so the token crosses the network in plaintext. The attacker needs the ability to answer for the configured host - a MITM on the path, a hostile Wi-Fi/DNS/proxy, or control of the endpoint itself.\n\nDisable HTTP redirect following on the WebSocket handshake so the socket can only ever reach the origin the user configured, and treat a redirecting origin as a connection failure. The concrete change is in `lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart`: pass a `customClient:` to `WebSocket.connect` whose requests set `followRedirects = false` (see the suggested patch).\n\nAs defence in depth, consider verifying the origin actually reached before the authentication frame is written, and re-checking it on every reconnect. If the deployment expects redirects (for example an HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrade on the gateway), resolve them explicitly with an allowlist rather than letting the transport follow them implicitly.",
                "markdown": "The app connects to the Hermes Gateway with `WebSocket.connect()` and immediately writes an `auth` frame containing the stored gateway bearer token. The dart:io WebSocket handshake is an ordinary `GET` issued by a shared `HttpClient` whose request objects default to `followRedirects = true` with up to 5 redirects. A `301`/`302`/`303`/`307`/`308` response to the handshake is therefore followed automatically, the upgrade is completed against the redirect target, and the credential frame is written to that socket. The client never checks that the origin it finally connected to is the origin it was configured with. Reproduced against a local redirect server: the token frame arrived at the redirect target, including a `wss://` to `http://` cleartext downgrade.\n\nThe stored gateway bearer token is disclosed to whoever controls the HTTP response to the handshake for the configured origin. Because the same value is reused as the LLM API key, the disclosed credential also authenticates the OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint. The disclosure is silent (no user-visible error, the socket reports as connected) and is independent of TLS: a `wss://` profile can be redirected to a cleartext `http://` listener, so the token crosses the network in plaintext. The attacker needs the ability to answer for the configured host - a MITM on the path, a hostile Wi-Fi/DNS/proxy, or control of the endpoint itself.\n\nDisable HTTP redirect following on the WebSocket handshake so the socket can only ever reach the origin the user configured, and treat a redirecting origin as a connection failure. The concrete change is in `lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart`: pass a `customClient:` to `WebSocket.connect` whose requests set `followRedirects = false` (see the suggested patch).\n\nAs defence in depth, consider verifying the origin actually reached before the authentication frame is written, and re-checking it on every reconnect. If the deployment expects redirects (for example an HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrade on the gateway), resolve them explicitly with an allowlist rather than letting the transport follow them implicitly."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-522",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/522.html"
