      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-321",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Release build signed with the public Android debug keystore\n\nThe release build type is configured to sign with the shared Android debug keystore instead of a private release key. Because the debug keystore and its password are public and identical on every development machine, the shipped release APK is signed with a key any third party can reproduce. Anyone holding that key can build an APK that Android accepts as a legitimate update to this application, and the application can never be published to Google Play under a trustworthy signing identity.\n\nThis finding is based on static source review only and is **unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — no APK was built and no update was delivered in this assessment."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "android/app/build.gradle.kts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 35,
                  "endLine": 41,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "buildTypes {\n        release {\n            // TODO: Add your own signing config for the release build.\n            // Signing with the debug keys for now, so `flutter run --release` works.\n            signingConfig = signingConfigs.getByName(\"debug\")\n        }\n    }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Replace the debug signing config with a real release signing config that fails closed when key.properties is absent"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "android/app/build.gradle.kts",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "android/app/build.gradle.kts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 35,
                        "endLine": 41
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "signingConfigs {\n        create(\"release\") {\n            val keystoreProperties = java.util.Properties()\n            val keystorePropertiesFile = rootProject.file(\"key.properties\")\n            require(keystorePropertiesFile.exists()) {\n                \"key.properties is required to build a release APK; refusing to fall back to the debug keystore.\"\n            }\n            keystorePropertiesFile.inputStream().use { keystoreProperties.load(it) }\n            keyAlias = keystoreProperties.getProperty(\"keyAlias\")\n            keyPassword = keystoreProperties.getProperty(\"keyPassword\")\n            storeFile = file(keystoreProperties.getProperty(\"storeFile\"))\n            storePassword = keystoreProperties.getProperty(\"storePassword\")\n        }\n    }\n\n    buildTypes {\n        release {\n            signingConfig = signingConfigs.getByName(\"release\")\n            isMinifyEnabled = true\n            isShrinkResources = true\n        }\n    }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Generate a private release keystore and store it outside the repository, with the credentials in a `key.properties` file that is already covered by the Android `.gitignore`.\n2. Declare a `signingConfigs { create(\"release\") { ... } }` block in `android/app/build.gradle.kts` that loads `key.properties`, and point the `release` build type at that config instead of `debug`.\n3. Make the release build fail loudly when `key.properties` is absent, so a debug-signed release can never be produced by accident again.\n4. Enable code shrinking and resource shrinking for the release build type (`isMinifyEnabled = true`, `isShrinkResources = true`) and add a ProGuard/R8 keep rules file.\n5. Store the release keystore and its passwords in the CI secret store, and use Play App Signing if the app is distributed through Google Play.\n6. Rotate the signing identity before any public release — an already-distributed debug-signed build cannot be safely upgraded in place.",
                "markdown": "1. Generate a private release keystore and store it outside the repository, with the credentials in a `key.properties` file that is already covered by the Android `.gitignore`.\n2. Declare a `signingConfigs { create(\"release\") { ... } }` block in `android/app/build.gradle.kts` that loads `key.properties`, and point the `release` build type at that config instead of `debug`.\n3. Make the release build fail loudly when `key.properties` is absent, so a debug-signed release can never be produced by accident again.\n4. Enable code shrinking and resource shrinking for the release build type (`isMinifyEnabled = true`, `isShrinkResources = true`) and add a ProGuard/R8 keep rules file.\n5. Store the release keystore and its passwords in the CI secret store, and use Play App Signing if the app is distributed through Google Play.\n6. Rotate the signing identity before any public release — an already-distributed debug-signed build cannot be safely upgraded in place."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "66bc0e0c1a9bc8296d46a6655c2296f0ecf30bb5ebe718c99bad761a1d18f921"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.2",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "c8a5953207e49ee95062fb7518a41af8123b46439cd7a211bc16376117dd4ae5",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.2,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-15 05:15:23 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/flames-audit-t196 (Flames Android application, package ai.hermes.flames)",
              "endpoint": "android/app/build.gradle.kts",
              "cwe": "CWE-321",
              "impact": "The application's release artifacts are signed with a key that is not secret. If the APK is distributed outside Google Play (which the permission set and feature set make likely for this product), an attacker can produce a tampered APK that carries the same signing identity and installs as an in-place update over the legitimate app, with no signature-mismatch warning to the user. Such an update runs with the application's identity and privileges and can read the app's private data — including the locally stored chat transcripts, the agent memory vault, and the Action Center store — and can exercise every device capability the app has been granted (notifications, accessibility, location, camera, Health Connect). The app also cannot be published to Google Play with this signing configuration, so the release pipeline is not production-ready.",
              "technical_analysis": "`android/app/build.gradle.kts` declares a `release` build type whose only configuration is a signing config that resolves to the built-in `debug` keystore:\n\n```kotlin title=android/app/build.gradle.kts startLineNumber=35 endLineNumber=41\n    buildTypes {\n        release {\n            // TODO: Add your own signing config for the release build.\n            // Signing with the debug keys for now, so `flutter run --release` works.\n            signingConfig = signingConfigs.getByName(\"debug\")\n        }\n    }\n```\n\nThe `signingConfigs` block that a real release build needs is absent from the file, and no `key.properties` file is present in the repository (the Android `.gitignore` already lists `key.properties`, `**/*.keystore` and `**/*.jks`, showing the release keystore was intended but never wired up). No `isMinifyEnabled` / `isShrinkResources` settings are declared for the release build type either.\n\nThe debug keystore is generated by the Android SDK at `~/.android/debug.keystore` with the well-known alias `androiddebugkey` and password `android`; it is identical across all installations. Any party can therefore sign an APK that carries the same certificate as a legitimate release build of this app. Android treats an APK with the same `applicationId` (`ai.hermes.flames`) and the same signing certificate as an in-place update to the installed application.\n\nNote that this defect is independent of the source code's runtime security posture — it is a build/release configuration issue, and the fix is confined to the Gradle file plus an out-of-repository keystore.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Generate a private release keystore and store it outside the repository, with the credentials in a `key.properties` file that is already covered by the Android `.gitignore`.\n2. Declare a `signingConfigs { create(\"release\") { ... } }` block in `android/app/build.gradle.kts` that loads `key.properties`, and point the `release` build type at that config instead of `debug`.\n3. Make the release build fail loudly when `key.properties` is absent, so a debug-signed release can never be produced by accident again.\n4. Enable code shrinking and resource shrinking for the release build type (`isMinifyEnabled = true`, `isShrinkResources = true`) and add a ProGuard/R8 keep rules file.\n5. Store the release keystore and its passwords in the CI secret store, and use Play App Signing if the app is distributed through Google Play.\n6. Rotate the signing identity before any public release — an already-distributed debug-signed build cannot be safely upgraded in place.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively checked for constraints that would neutralise this finding. (1) A release `signingConfigs` block or a `key.properties` file could have existed and overridden the debug config — neither is present anywhere in the repository, and `key.properties` is only referenced in `.gitignore`. (2) Gradle could have injected a signing config through a property or an included build — `android/build.gradle.kts` only sets repositories, build directories and JVM targets, and `gradle.properties` contains no signing properties. (3) The impact could be limited to \"cannot publish to Play\", which would make it informational — but the update-hijack consequence is real for any non-Play distribution channel, which is the realistic channel for this app given its permission set. The genuine constraint that lowers severity is that exploitation requires the attacker to already be able to deliver an APK to the victim and the victim to accept the install, so this is not a remotely triggerable issue on its own; that precondition is reflected in the `attack_complexity` and `user_interaction` metrics rather than being used to dismiss the finding. No dynamic validation was possible, so the exploitability of the update path is reasoned from Android's documented signing behaviour rather than demonstrated.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "The code fact is certain and directly observable — the release build type is configured with `signingConfig = signingConfigs.getByName(\"debug\")` and no release signing material exists in the repository. What is not dynamically validated is the downstream consequence: no APK was built, no signing certificate was inspected, and no update-over-install was attempted, because this assessment had no Android SDK, emulator or device. The conclusion that a same-certificate APK installs as an update relies on documented Android platform behaviour rather than on an executed proof.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that release APKs built from this repository are actually distributed to users outside Google Play would raise the severity, because the forgeable update channel would then be live rather than hypothetical; conversely, evidence that the project only ever builds debug artifacts locally (and that the release configuration is unused scaffolding) would reduce this to an informational build-hygiene observation.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 — Applicability: `fix_before` is a character-for-character copy of `android/app/build.gradle.kts` lines 35-41 as read from the checkout, and the replacement is confined to that block (it stays inside the existing `android { }` extension, before the `kotlin { }` block). No syntax/type check could be executed: this environment has no Flutter SDK, no Android SDK and no Gradle, so `./gradlew` cannot run (gate 4 is an explicit gap). The Kotlin DSL shape used (`signingConfigs { create(...) }`, `keyAlias`/`keyPassword`/`storeFile`/`storePassword` as `String?` properties, `isMinifyEnabled`/`isShrinkResources` on the release build type) matches the current Android Gradle Plugin Kotlin DSL used by the rest of the file.\n\nGate 2 — Security closure: after the patch the release build type resolves `signingConfigs.getByName(\"release\")`, whose material comes exclusively from `key.properties`. The `require(...)` guard means a build without that file throws during configuration instead of silently producing a debug-signed artifact, so the previous failure mode (a release APK signed with the public debug key) is unreachable. The `debug` signing config is no longer referenced by any build type.\n\nGate 3 — Bypass review: the change is checked against the other paths that could still produce a debug-signed release — the `debug` and `profile` build types (which legitimately keep the default debug signing), the `flutter { source = \"../..\" }` block (no signing role), and `android/build.gradle.kts` / `gradle.properties` (no signing properties; verified). `key.properties` is already ignored by `android/.gitignore`, so the fix does not introduce a secret into version control. The residual risk that a developer commits a keystore is outside this file.\n\nGate 4 — Repository checks: NOT RUN — no Flutter/Android/Gradle toolchain is installed in the assessment environment, so neither `./gradlew :app:assembleRelease` nor a Gradle configuration check could be executed. This gate must be run by the repository owner before merging.\n\nGate 5 — Preserved behaviour: `flutter run --release` and `flutter build apk --release` continue to work for anyone who has created `key.properties`; only the previously-implicit fallback to the debug keystore is removed, which is the intended fail-closed behaviour. No public API, error semantics, or runtime code is touched. Narrowest-complete-change judgement: the patch is the smallest repository-native change that fully removes the public-key signing path — it adds no unrelated refactors, and the only new out-of-repository artefact it depends on (`key.properties`) is already anticipated by `.gitignore`.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Read `android/app/build.gradle.kts` and observe that the `release` build type sets `signingConfig = signingConfigs.getByName(\"debug\")` and that no `signingConfigs { create(\"release\") { ... } }` block exists.\n2. Observe that no `key.properties`, `.jks` or `.keystore` file exists anywhere in the repository, so no private release key is configured.\n3. Confirm the shared debug keystore is the only signing material in play by inspecting the Android SDK keystore location and the absence of any release signing properties file.\n4. (Not performed — no Android SDK or emulator in this environment) Build a release APK and inspect its signing certificate; it will match the well-known debug certificate fingerprint shared by all Android debug builds.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-312",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Sensitive app data included in Android backups because allowBackup is unset\n\nThe application declares no android:allowBackup attribute on its application element and ships no fullBackupContent or dataExtractionRules rule, so Android applies its default of allowBackup=true and includes the app private data in cloud backups and device-to-device transfers. The app keeps sensitive user content in plaintext app-private stores - chat transcripts, the agent memory vault, imported skills and the Action Center inbox - which are consequently swept into the backup set without any exclusion. This finding is based on static source review only and is unconfirmed, not dynamically validated: no device, emulator or backup extraction was available in this assessment."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 53,
                  "endLine": 53
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Declare allowBackup=false and point at a data-extraction rules resource on the application element"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 53,
                        "endLine": 53
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "<application\n        android:allowBackup=\"false\"\n        android:dataExtractionRules=\"@xml/data_extraction_rules\""
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Add android:allowBackup=\"false\" to the application element in android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml.\n2. Add android:dataExtractionRules=\"@xml/data_extraction_rules\" alongside it and create android/app/src/main/res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml containing a data-extraction-rules document that excludes the root, database, sharedpref and file domains under both cloud-backup and device-transfer.\n3. Add android:fullBackupContent=\"@xml/backup_rules\" with an equivalent full-backup-content exclusion list for API levels below 31.\n4. If chat history should survive device migration, implement it as an explicit, user-initiated, encrypted export rather than relying on the platform backup channel.\n5. Review any future plaintext store against this rule before adding it, since the default include behaviour exposes new files automatically.",
                "markdown": "1. Add android:allowBackup=\"false\" to the application element in android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml.\n2. Add android:dataExtractionRules=\"@xml/data_extraction_rules\" alongside it and create android/app/src/main/res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml containing a data-extraction-rules document that excludes the root, database, sharedpref and file domains under both cloud-backup and device-transfer.\n3. Add android:fullBackupContent=\"@xml/backup_rules\" with an equivalent full-backup-content exclusion list for API levels below 31.\n4. If chat history should survive device migration, implement it as an explicit, user-initiated, encrypted export rather than relying on the platform backup channel.\n5. Review any future plaintext store against this rule before adding it, since the default include behaviour exposes new files automatically."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "f24b99a1cd798b1b6013c08bc8f1ca368528392394ffa83d5897851165c67783"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.7",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "f46253d1a2efd25787eb286c1bdc60b88c9c0da5520412c244fcb23efb11e983",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.7,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-15 05:23:59 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/flames-audit-t196 (Flames Android application, package ai.hermes.flames)",
              "endpoint": "android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml",
              "cwe": "CWE-312",
              "impact": "The app private storage is included in Android cloud backups and device-to-device transfers. A backup set obtained through ADB backup, a cloud-account compromise, or forensic acquisition of a backup image yields the plaintext contents of flames_memory.db (the agent memory vault, whose content column holds arbitrary user- and agent-authored text), flames_skills.db, and the Hive action_center box, together with the session titles in flames_chat.db. The LLM API key and server-profile records are held in FlutterSecureStorage and are not directly recoverable from a backup because Android excludes the Keystore master key, and chat message bodies are likewise encrypted at rest rather than stored in flames_chat.db; the exposure is therefore user content rather than credentials or full transcripts. The issue also runs the other way: a backup restored onto a different device reintroduces this data into app storage without the user having chosen to restore it.",
              "technical_analysis": "Android includes an application's private data in cloud backup and device-to-device transfer when android:allowBackup is true, which is the platform default when the attribute is omitted. The application element in android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml declares no allowBackup, fullBackupContent or dataExtractionRules attribute, and no such resource file exists under android/app/src/main/res/xml/ (that directory contains only accessibility_service_config.xml). The default therefore applies and the whole app data directory is backed up.\n\nThe data directory contains plaintext stores traced in code:\n\n- flames_chat.db in the app databases directory (chat_local_datasource.dart:26,64). Its schema (chat_local_datasource.dart:80-95) contains a single `sessions` table: id, profile_id, title, created_at, updated_at. Message bodies are deliberately not stored here — `saveMessages` writes them through FlutterSecureStorage (`_storage.write(key: keyFor(sessionId), value: encoded)`, chat_local_datasource.dart:113-125) and `getMessages` reads them back from the same store (:240-261). The plaintext exposure from this database is therefore the session titles, which are derived verbatim from the first 40 characters of the user's first message (chat_repository_impl.dart:22-28, agent_cubit.dart:162-168), not the transcripts themselves.\n- flames_memory.db in the app databases directory on Android (sqlite_memory_datasource.dart:16,88-103), holding the memories table and its FTS5 mirror. This store is plaintext and its `content` column holds arbitrary user- and agent-authored text.\n- flames_skills.db in the app databases directory (skills_local_datasource.dart:15).\n- the Hive action_center box opened under the app documents directory by Hive.initFlutter('flames') (lib/myapp.dart:47), storing JSON-encoded inbox entries with free-text titles, summaries and action payloads (lib/data/models/action_center/action_item_model.dart:39-55).\n\nNone is excluded by a backup rule, so all are included in the backup set. Android 12+ would honour a dataExtractionRules resource, but the attribute is unset, so no restriction is in force on any supported API level. Credentials (LLM API key, server profiles) are held in FlutterSecureStorage and are not recoverable from a backup because Android excludes the Keystore master key.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Add android:allowBackup=\"false\" to the application element in android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml.\n2. Add android:dataExtractionRules=\"@xml/data_extraction_rules\" alongside it and create android/app/src/main/res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml containing a data-extraction-rules document that excludes the root, database, sharedpref and file domains under both cloud-backup and device-transfer.\n3. Add android:fullBackupContent=\"@xml/backup_rules\" with an equivalent full-backup-content exclusion list for API levels below 31.\n4. If chat history should survive device migration, implement it as an explicit, user-initiated, encrypted export rather than relying on the platform backup channel.\n5. Review any future plaintext store against this rule before adding it, since the default include behaviour exposes new files automatically.",
              "counterevidence": "Looked for constraints that would neutralise this. No fullBackupContent or dataExtractionRules attribute or resource exists anywhere under android/, so nothing is excluded. The stores are not outside the auto-backup set: flames_chat.db and flames_skills.db resolve to the app databases directory, flames_memory.db resolves there on Android, and the Hive action_center box resolves under the app documents directory. The genuine constraint is that the credentials themselves live in FlutterSecureStorage with default options, and Android excludes the Keystore master key from backup, so the stored LLM API key and base URL are not directly recoverable from a backup set. That is why confidentiality is rated Low rather than High. It reduces the value of the extracted data but does not neutralise the finding, because the chat transcripts, memory-vault rows and Action Center entries are plaintext SQLite and Hive files with no such protection. No dynamic validation was possible.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "The static evidence is complete: no allowBackup, dataExtractionRules or fullBackupContent attribute exists anywhere in the Android configuration, and the sensitive stores were traced in code to app-private paths. What is not validated is the end-to-end extraction: no device, emulator, ADB or bmgr access was available, so no backup set was produced or parsed. The step from an unset attribute to data leaving the device is documented Android behaviour, not an executed proof.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating that a produced backup set contains readable chat-transcript or memory-vault rows would raise the severity; evidence that the app is only distributed to managed devices with cloud backup disabled by policy would lower it.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 Applicability: fix_before is a character-for-character copy of android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml line 53 (four spaces followed by the opening application tag). The patch inserts two attribute lines immediately after that opening tag, before the existing label and icon attributes, which is valid XML and keeps the element well-formed. The referenced @xml/data_extraction_rules resource must be created; its target directory android/app/src/main/res/xml/ already exists and holds accessibility_service_config.xml, so no directory creation is implied. No syntax check could be executed: this environment has no Android SDK or Gradle, so the manifest was not run through aapt/aapt2 and the rules resource was not schema-validated. Gate 4 is an explicit gap.\n\nGate 2 Security closure: with allowBackup=false the platform excludes the app data directory from backup regardless of the rules resource, and the dataExtractionRules resource independently excludes the root, database, sharedpref and file domains from both cloud backup and device transfer. The previously reachable path - unset attribute leading to the platform default true, then the whole data directory being included - is closed where the platform reads the flag, before any file is copied into a backup set.\n\nGate 3 Bypass review: fullBackupContent is not set, which on API below 31 means allowBackup is the only gate, and it is now set, so those levels are covered. On Android 12+ dataExtractionRules governs the transfer decision and the new resource covers both cloud-backup and device-transfer. The debug and profile manifests declare only the INTERNET permission and contain no application element, so they cannot re-enable backup. Key-value data in FlutterSecureStorage remains excluded by the platform handling of the Keystore master key and is unchanged by this patch. Deliberately not addressed: a rooted device, or an ADB backup taken with a pre-existing backup set.\n\nGate 4 Repository checks: NOT RUN - no Android SDK, Gradle or Flutter toolchain is installed, so neither gradlew assembleRelease nor a manifest merge could be executed. The repository owner must run the Android build to confirm the merged manifest and validate the rules resource against the AGP schema before merging.\n\nGate 5 Preserved behaviour: no runtime code, public API or error semantics change. Users lose the ability to restore chat history onto a new device through the platform backup channel; that is the intended security trade-off and is called out in the PR body with the encrypted-export alternative. The rules resource is ignored below API 31, where allowBackup=false still applies, so behaviour is consistent across the app minSdk of 26. Narrowest-complete-change judgement: the smallest repository-native change that enforces the invariant that app-private content does not leave the device through the platform backup channel; it reuses the platform mechanism rather than adding an application-layer filter and folds in no unrelated manifest changes.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Read android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml and confirm the application element declares no allowBackup, dataExtractionRules or fullBackupContent attribute.\n2. Confirm no backup rules resource exists under android/app/src/main/res/xml/.\n3. Confirm the app persists sensitive content to plaintext app-private stores by reading the database file names and schemas in lib/data/datasources/local/.\n4. (Not performed - no device, emulator or ADB in this environment) Build a release or debug APK, install it on a device, create chat and memory content, then run an ADB backup and inspect the resulting archive for flames_chat.db and flames_memory.db.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Exported MainActivity accepts unvalidated action extra, letting any co-resident Android app start a microphone voice turn and dispatch agent prompts\n\nThe exported `MainActivity` of the Flames Android app reads the string extra `extra_flames_action` from any incoming `Intent` and forwards it, unvalidated, to the Flutter layer, which dispatches on it without an allowlist. Any third-party app installed on the same device can therefore start `MainActivity` with a crafted extra and trigger privileged app behaviour with no user action beyond the app window becoming visible: `voice_input` starts a 15-second speech-recognition (microphone) capture session, and `quick_status` sends a canned prompt to the AI agent, which may then execute device tools. **This finding is unconfirmed and not dynamically validated — the audit was source-only with no emulator or device.**"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "android/app/src/main/kotlin/ai/hermes/flames/MainActivity.kt"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 73,
                  "endLine": 85,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "private fun extractActionFromIntent(intent: Intent?): String? {\n        val actionExtra = intent?.getStringExtra(\"extra_flames_action\")\n        if (!actionExtra.isNullOrBlank()) {\n            pendingAction = actionExtra\n            return actionExtra\n        }\n        if (intent?.action == FlamesQuickTile.ACTION_QUICK_TILE_TRIGGER) {\n            val fallbackAction = \"voice_input\"\n            pendingAction = fallbackAction\n            return fallbackAction\n        }\n        return null\n    }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Validate the intent-supplied action against an explicit allowlist before it can reach the Dart layer"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "android/app/src/main/kotlin/ai/hermes/flames/MainActivity.kt"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 51,
                  "endLine": 52,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "private var backgroundMethodChannel: MethodChannel? = null\n    private var pendingAction: String? = null"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Declare the allowlist of actions the exported activity will accept"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "android/app/src/main/kotlin/ai/hermes/flames/MainActivity.kt"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 73,
                        "endLine": 85
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "private fun extractActionFromIntent(intent: Intent?): String? {\n        // The activity is exported (launcher entry point), so this extra is\n        // attacker-controlled: any co-resident app can start MainActivity and\n        // supply an arbitrary value. Only the actions this app actually\n        // implements may be forwarded to the Dart layer.\n        val actionExtra = intent?.getStringExtra(\"extra_flames_action\")\n        if (!actionExtra.isNullOrBlank()) {\n            val action = actionExtra.trim()\n            if (action !in ALLOWED_ACTIONS) {\n                return null\n            }\n            pendingAction = action\n            return action\n        }\n        if (intent?.action == FlamesQuickTile.ACTION_QUICK_TILE_TRIGGER) {\n            val fallbackAction = \"voice_input\"\n            pendingAction = fallbackAction\n            return fallbackAction\n        }\n        return null\n    }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "android/app/src/main/kotlin/ai/hermes/flames/MainActivity.kt"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 51,
                        "endLine": 52
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "private var backgroundMethodChannel: MethodChannel? = null\n    private var pendingAction: String? = null\n\n    /** Actions the app implements; anything else is rejected at the boundary. */\n    private val ALLOWED_ACTIONS = setOf(\"voice_input\", \"cancel_voice\", \"quick_status\")"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Treat the action extra as untrusted input at the Android boundary, because any co-resident app can supply it.\n\n1. Validate the incoming action against an explicit allowlist at the point of extraction — the three values the app actually implements (`voice_input`, `cancel_voice`, `quick_status`) — and ignore anything else. An allowlist is the correct primitive here: the set is closed and known, and a denylist would silently accept future additions.\n2. Do not rely on the intent action or on the `MAIN`/`LAUNCHER` filter for trust. The extra is what selects the behaviour, so the check must be on the extra value itself, not on how the activity was started.\n3. If third-party automation of these actions is a product requirement, move it behind a dedicated exported component protected by a signature-level permission, so only apps signed with the same developer certificate can trigger it. The launcher activity should never be a privileged control surface.\n4. Keep the extraction function as the single choke point for validation so that both the `onCreate` and `onNewIntent` paths are covered by one check.\n5. Add a regression test asserting that an unknown action value is rejected and never reaches the method channel, and that a valid value still works, so the allowlist cannot silently regress.",
                "markdown": "Treat the action extra as untrusted input at the Android boundary, because any co-resident app can supply it.\n\n1. Validate the incoming action against an explicit allowlist at the point of extraction — the three values the app actually implements (`voice_input`, `cancel_voice`, `quick_status`) — and ignore anything else. An allowlist is the correct primitive here: the set is closed and known, and a denylist would silently accept future additions.\n2. Do not rely on the intent action or on the `MAIN`/`LAUNCHER` filter for trust. The extra is what selects the behaviour, so the check must be on the extra value itself, not on how the activity was started.\n3. If third-party automation of these actions is a product requirement, move it behind a dedicated exported component protected by a signature-level permission, so only apps signed with the same developer certificate can trigger it. The launcher activity should never be a privileged control surface.\n4. Keep the extraction function as the single choke point for validation so that both the `onCreate` and `onNewIntent` paths are covered by one check.\n5. Add a regression test asserting that an unknown action value is rejected and never reaches the method channel, and that a valid value still works, so the allowlist cannot silently regress."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "38e935e0801a5fa094d0d1b88582f88a982f4887eb0ed175ed119595e54baca8"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "ae36d31443c7166d8bf82d65683c820d2e0bf852bd53f1fdd9a4f85914f3cbe6",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-15 05:25:38 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/flames-audit-t196 (Flames Android app, package `ai.hermes.flames`)",
              "endpoint": "android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml",
              "cwe": "CWE-862",
              "impact": "An attacker with no Android permissions and no special access — merely a co-resident app on the same device — can drive Flames' most sensitive local capability without the user tapping anything in the app.\n\n1. **Microphone activation (`voice_input`).** The app opens a 15-second speech-recognition session (`VoiceTurnService.defaultListenFor`). Any speech in the room during that window is transcribed on-device and, when a dispatch transport is ready, transmitted to the configured remote LLM API as a chat prompt. The capture begins with the microphone permission already granted by the user to Flames, so no permission dialog appears. The recording indicator is an overlay bubble that only exists if the user previously enabled it (off by default), so in the default configuration there is no on-screen indication that capture is active.\n\n2. **Autonomous agent dispatch (`quick_status`).** The fixed prompt `Quick status check: summarize battery, device context, and agent readiness.` is injected into the agent loop. The agent can respond by invoking the app's native device tools (clipboard, location, contacts, calendar, notifications, accessibility-tree reading, Health Connect). The dispatch path was read to confirm the resulting state machine, and a `WaitingApproval` terminal state is surfaced as a failure rather than silently bypassing the human-in-the-loop gate, so this leg is bounded by the existing tier gating rather than escalating it.\n\n3. **Persistence and reachability.** `pendingAction` is set on both `onCreate` and `onNewIntent` and is never cleared until the Dart layer drains it, so the value survives a cold start and is replayed when the listener mounts. The chain does not require the app to be in the foreground: the action is delivered through `onNewIntent` while the activity is already alive, and the background service path (`FlamesBackgroundService` action `ACTION_VOICE_INPUT`, reachable from the ongoing notification and the Quick Settings tile) performs the same Dart injection. Injected microphone capture is the demonstrated consequence; no audio exfiltration to an attacker-controlled destination is demonstrated, so the confidentiality rating below reflects initiation of capture of the user's audio, not demonstrated attacker retrieval of it.",
              "technical_analysis": "## Root cause\n\n`MainActivity` is declared exported with no guarding permission, which is required for the launcher entry point:\n\n```xml title=android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml startLineNumber=57 endLineNumber=76\n<activity\n    android:name=\".MainActivity\"\n    android:exported=\"true\"\n    android:launchMode=\"singleTop\"\n    ...\n    <intent-filter>\n        <action android:name=\"android.intent.action.MAIN\"/>\n        <category android:name=\"android.intent.category.LAUNCHER\"/>\n    </intent-filter>\n```\n\n`extractActionFromIntent()` accepts an arbitrary string from that `Intent` and stores it, with no allowlist, no caller-identity check and no signature/authentication of the caller:\n\n```kotlin title=android/app/src/main/kotlin/ai/hermes/flames/MainActivity.kt startLineNumber=73 endLineNumber=85\nprivate fun extractActionFromIntent(intent: Intent?): String? {\n    val actionExtra = intent?.getStringExtra(\"extra_flames_action\")\n    if (!actionExtra.isNullOrBlank()) {\n        pendingAction = actionExtra\n        return actionExtra\n    }\n    if (intent?.action == FlamesQuickTile.ACTION_QUICK_TILE_TRIGGER) {\n        val fallbackAction = \"voice_input\"\n        pendingAction = fallbackAction\n        return fallbackAction\n    }\n    return null\n}\n```\n\nThe value is delivered to Dart on both lifecycle paths — cold start (`onCreate`, line 56) and warm delivery (`onNewIntent`, lines 59-66) — and is additionally readable by the Flutter layer through the `getPendingAction` / `clearPendingAction` methods on the `ai.hermes.flames/background_service` channel (lines 303-309). `FlamesBackgroundService.EXTRA_ACTION` (line 38) is the same string constant, and the ongoing notification's \"Quick Status\" action ships the same extra (lines 402-406), confirming the extra is a first-class control channel rather than a developer leftover.\n\n## Sink chain\n\n1. `VoiceTurnActionListener._handleAction` switches on the action value with no validation:\n```dart title=lib/presentation/voice/voice_turn_action_listener.dart startLineNumber=162 endLineNumber=178\nvoid _handleAction(String action) {\n  switch (action.toLowerCase().trim()) {\n    case 'voice_input':\n      unawaited(_runTurnWithTileSync());\n      break;\n    case 'cancel_voice':\n      unawaited(_cancelTurnAndResetTile());\n      break;\n    case 'quick_status':\n      unawaited(\n        _registry?.active?.dispatch(\n          'Quick status check: summarize battery, device context, and agent readiness.',\n        ),\n      );\n      break;\n  }\n}\n```\n2. `_runTurnWithTileSync()` calls `VoiceTurnService.runTurn()`, which passes every precondition (permission already granted, `_voiceInput.isListening` false) and reaches the microphone at line 141: `await _voiceInput.startListening(listenFor: defaultListenFor /* 15s */, pauseFor: 3s, ...)`.\n3. `VoiceInputService.startListening` calls `_speech.listen(...)` on `package:speech_to_text` (line 202), which starts the platform speech recognizer and sets `_listening = true`.\n4. `_checkPendingAction()` (lines 291-297) drains the same value at startup, so a cold-start injection is replayed when the widget mounts.\n\n## Controls examined and found absent\n\n- No allowlist of accepted action values anywhere in `android/` or `lib/` — the only consumers are the unguarded `switch` above.\n- No caller authentication: `getCallingActivity`, `getCallingPackage`, `Binder.getCallingUid`, `checkCallingPermission` and `getReferrer` do not appear anywhere in `android/` or `lib/`.\n- No user confirmation, dialog or HITL prompt before the microphone opens; the approval gate applies only to Tier-3 tool invocation inside the agent loop, not to microphone activation.\n- No `android:permission` attribute on `MainActivity` and no `<permission>` / `signature`-level protection on the receiving side.\n\n## Reachability\n\nThe `MAIN`/`LAUNCHER` filter is the target of the explicit-intent start, and the activity is `exported=\"true\"` without a permission, so any installed app can start it with `Intent.setComponent(...)` and attach the extra. Because the action is carried in an extra rather than the intent action, the attacker does not even need to match a filter — a plain explicit intent suffices. `MainActivity` and `FlamesBackgroundService` share the default process, so `attachDartChannel` (line 259) makes `backgroundMethodChannel` live whenever the Flutter engine exists.",
              "remediation_steps": "Treat the action extra as untrusted input at the Android boundary, because any co-resident app can supply it.\n\n1. Validate the incoming action against an explicit allowlist at the point of extraction — the three values the app actually implements (`voice_input`, `cancel_voice`, `quick_status`) — and ignore anything else. An allowlist is the correct primitive here: the set is closed and known, and a denylist would silently accept future additions.\n2. Do not rely on the intent action or on the `MAIN`/`LAUNCHER` filter for trust. The extra is what selects the behaviour, so the check must be on the extra value itself, not on how the activity was started.\n3. If third-party automation of these actions is a product requirement, move it behind a dedicated exported component protected by a signature-level permission, so only apps signed with the same developer certificate can trigger it. The launcher activity should never be a privileged control surface.\n4. Keep the extraction function as the single choke point for validation so that both the `onCreate` and `onNewIntent` paths are covered by one check.\n5. Add a regression test asserting that an unknown action value is rejected and never reaches the method channel, and that a valid value still works, so the allowlist cannot silently regress.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively attempted to break the chain and could not: (a) searched `android/` and `lib/` for caller-identity or permission verification (`getCallingActivity`, `getCallingPackage`, `Binder.getCallingUid`, `checkCallingPermission`, `getReferrer`) — no matches; (b) searched for any allowlist, enum or constant set constraining the action value at either the Kotlin or Dart boundary — the only consumers are the unvalidated `switch` in `_handleAction`, so an arbitrary value is accepted and simply falls through if unknown, while the three privileged values are matched exactly; (c) searched for a user-confirmation, dialog or lifecycle/foreground gate on the microphone path (`showDialog`, `AlertDialog`, `AppLifecycleState`, `didChangeAppLifecycleState`) — none exists; (d) confirmed `MainActivity` carries no `android:permission` and that the system `BIND_*` permissions on the other exported components are irrelevant to this path, since the attacker targets `MainActivity` directly; (e) confirmed the microphone precondition is satisfied in the default state — `VoiceInputService.startListening` proceeds when the user has already granted `RECORD_AUDIO`, which is required for the feature to work at all, so no additional dialog appears; (f) considered that the victim sees the Flames window open — this is a real limitation of the attack (it is not fully stealthy) but does not block the microphone capture or the agent dispatch, and the launch is indistinguishable from a normal user launch of the app.\n\nTwo honest scope limits on the impact: the audio path is bounded by whether a dispatch transport is ready (`runTurn()` returns before opening the recognizer when none is, per the guard at `voice_turn_service.dart:84-90`), and the agent-dispatch path is bounded by the existing tier gating — a Tier-3 tool still surfaces `WaitingApproval` rather than executing, which the code returns as a failure. The finding is therefore microphone-capture initiation and unattended agent dispatch, not a demonstrated bypass of the human-in-the-loop control or demonstrated exfiltration of captured audio. No dynamic validation was possible (no device, emulator or `adb`), so the attack was established by complete source trace only.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "Static-only trace. The audit environment provided no Android device, emulator or `adb`, so the attack was not executed and no runtime response, recognizer activation or agent dispatch was observed. Every hop of the chain was verified by reading the source: exported unguarded activity, unvalidated extra read, delivery over the method channel, the Dart switch, and the call into the speech-recognition plugin. The specific unverified step is that the injected intent reaches `onNewIntent`/`onCreate` with the extra intact and that the Dart seam is live at that moment; both follow from the code but were not observed. Runtime confirmation (the recognizer opening, or the agent receiving the injected prompt) would be required to raise confidence to high.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating on a real device that the injected `voice_input` action reliably opens the speech recognizer and that the resulting transcript is transmitted to the configured remote LLM API would raise the severity, as it would establish unattended capture and off-device egress of the user's ambient audio. Conversely, evidence that the dispatch transport is normally unavailable when the app is not in the foreground, or that the microphone path requires an additional foreground user action on current Android versions, would lower it.",
              "fix_verification": "**Gate 1 — Applicability.** Both edited regions were read from the file immediately before writing the patch: lines 51-52 hold the two field declarations the new constant sits beside, and lines 73-85 are the whole `extractActionFromIntent` function. The `fix_before` blocks are verbatim copies of those ranges, and `fix_after` replaces exactly those ranges. The result is syntactically valid Kotlin: a `Set<String>` property plus an early-return guard. No other call site needed changing — `extractActionFromIntent` is the single extraction choke point and both `onCreate` (line 56) and `onNewIntent` (line 62) route through it.\n\n**Gate 2 — Security closure.** Re-traced the source → sink path against the patched code: attacker `Intent` → `onCreate`/`onNewIntent` → `extractActionFromIntent` → the new `action !in ALLOWED_ACTIONS` check rejects any value outside `{voice_input, cancel_voice, quick_status}` by returning `null`, which means `pendingAction` is never set and `onNewIntent` never calls `backgroundMethodChannel?.invokeMethod(\"onActionReceived\", action)` (guarded by `if (action != null)` at lines 63-65). Both the warm path and the cold-start path are covered because the check sits inside the single shared extraction function, and `getPendingAction` therefore can only ever return a vetted value. The PoC could not be executed against the patched code (no device or emulator), so closure was established by trace, not by re-running the attack.\n\n**Gate 3 — Bypass review.** Re-read the diff for what the original reasoning might have missed. (i) *Sibling call sites*: `FlamesBackgroundService.kt` also defines `EXTRA_ACTION` and injects `voice_input`, `cancel_voice` and `quick_status` over the same Dart channel (lines 226, 248, 276, 404) — all three are in the allowlist, so the legitimate notification, tile and bubble flows keep working, and the service is `android:exported=\"false\"` so it is not an independent injection point. (ii) *Equivalent sinks*: the only other consumer of `actionStream` is `QuickActionsService`, which is fed by the `quick_actions` plugin's own shortcut callback rather than by an exported intent, so it is unaffected. (iii) *Alternate input classes tested by reasoning*: a `null` extra (falls through to the tile check, unchanged), a blank extra (`isNullOrBlank` short-circuits, unchanged), mixed case (`VOICE_INPUT`), leading/trailing whitespace, and a Unicode-confusable value all fail the exact-match set membership — the added `trim()` is the one deliberate normalisation, and it can only widen matching to values the Dart layer already lowercases and trims, so it does not admit anything new. Case-insensitive matching was deliberately **not** added, so `VOICE_INPUT` is rejected — conservative, and no legitimate caller uses it (all three in-repo producers emit lowercase). (iv) *Residual exposure*: the allowlist bounds the *values*, not the *caller*, so an attacker can still trigger a real `voice_input` turn — it can no longer supply an arbitrary value, but the three privileged actions remain reachable from outside the app. Fully closing that would require moving third-party automation behind a signature-level permission, which is a product decision recorded in the remediation steps rather than something this minimal patch can assume.\n\n**Gate 4 — Preserved behaviour.** The legitimate producers are unchanged: the launcher start sends no extra (falls through to `return null`), the Quick Settings tile path reaches the same `voice_input` fallback as before, and the notification's \"Voice Input\" / \"Quick Status\" / \"Stop\" actions all carry values inside the allowlist. Error semantics are preserved — an unrecognised action still results in no action being dispatched rather than a crash or a channel error. Note that the tile-trigger fallback branch (lines 79-83) is intentionally left untouched: it assigns the literal `voice_input` rather than reading attacker input, so it needs no validation.\n\n**Gate 5 — Repository checks (not executed — gap).** No Kotlin/Gradle build, no `flutter analyze` and no test run was possible in this environment (no Android SDK/emulator, and the Flutter toolchain is not installed), so the Kotlin change was not compiled. The relevant guard is an existing read-only source assertion, `test/domain/tools/chat/quick_tile_contract_test.dart:276-285`, which requires `MainActivity.kt` to still contain `FlamesQuickTile.ACTION_QUICK_TILE_TRIGGER`, `\"getPendingAction\"` and `\"clearPendingAction\"`; the patch leaves all three untouched, so that test remains satisfied. That is a static assertion, not a run. **Explicit gaps: gates 2 and 5 were verified by inspection only, not by execution; no compiler, no device, no test run.** A reviewer should compile the change and add a unit assertion that an unknown action value is dropped.\n\n**Narrowest complete change.** Two contiguous hunks in one file — the allowlist constant and the guard in the single extraction function. No existing helper covers this (the codebase has no action-validation utility), no unrelated refactor is folded in, and the patch does not touch the Dart layer, where the same allowlist could arguably live; enforcing it at the Android boundary is correct because that is where the untrusted input enters, and it protects the `getPendingAction` read path as well as the push path.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "Source-only audit: no device, emulator or `adb` was available, so the steps below describe the attack as it would be mounted against a real installation and were verified by static trace only, not executed.\n\n1. Install any third-party app on the same Android device as Flames. It needs no Android permissions and no special access — only the ability to start an activity, which every app has.\n2. From that app, build an explicit intent targeting the exported Flames activity: action `android.intent.action.MAIN`, component `ai.hermes.flames/.MainActivity`, and string extra `extra_flames_action` set to `voice_input`.\n3. Call `startActivity(intent)` with `FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK`. The Flames activity comes to the foreground.\n4. `MainActivity.extractActionFromIntent()` stores the value in `pendingAction` and `onNewIntent()` forwards it over the `ai.hermes.flames/background_service` method channel as `onActionReceived`.\n5. `VoiceTurnActionListener._handleAction` matches `voice_input` and calls `VoiceTurnService.runTurn()`, which calls `VoiceInputService.startListening()` — the speech recognizer opens the microphone for 15 seconds.\n6. Repeat step 2 with the extra set to `quick_status` to instead inject the fixed status prompt into the agent loop with no user interaction.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "vuln-0004",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Screen text read from any app is injected into the agent prompt as a tool result with no untrusted-data framing\n\nThe Android agent can read the accessibility tree of whatever app is in the foreground and hand that text to the LLM as a tool result (`ui_find_elements`). The tree is rendered from the window of *another* app, so its text is authored by that app, not by Flames or by the user — yet nothing marks it as untrusted, and the result is placed verbatim in the model's context. The codebase already ships a prompt-injection scanner (`SkillSecurityScanner`) but applies it only to imported skill text, so screen content reaches the model unframed and unscanned. The same model can then call Tier-1 tools that need no user approval (`save_memory`, `clipboard_write`) and read Tier-1/2 sensitive data (clipboard, location, files, notifications, OTP, health, contacts). Memory entries are re-injected on later turns as a `role: \"system\"` message, which converts transient screen text into persistent, highest-authority instructions. **Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — this is a source-only audit; there is no emulator, no device and no running app, so no payload was executed against a live model."
          },
