          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 250,
                        "endLine": 256
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (SecurityPolicy.requiresPermission(tool.securityTier) &&\n          !tool.selfEnforcesPermission) {\n        final checkPermission = tool.checkPermission;\n        if (checkPermission == null) {\n          return Left(ServerFailure(\n              'Tool $name is Tier 2 but declares no permission check.'));\n        }\n        final granted = await checkPermission();\n        if (!granted) {\n          return Left(PermissionFailure('Permission denied for tool: $name'));\n        }\n      }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 51,
                        "endLine": 60
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "final PermissionChecker? checkPermission;\n\n  /// True when [execute] performs its own permission/authorization evaluation\n  /// and [checkPermission] is deliberately omitted. Every other non-Tier-1\n  /// tool must supply [checkPermission]; the registry gate fails closed.\n  final bool selfEnforcesPermission;\n\n  const RegisteredTool({\n    required this.name,\n    required this.description,\n    required this.parameters,\n    required this.securityTier,\n    required this.execute,\n    this.checkPermission,\n    this.selfEnforcesPermission = false,\n  });"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 1760,
                        "endLine": 1763
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "securityTier: SecurityTier.tier2Permission,\n        // No checkPermission: the registry gate would return a generic\n        // PermissionFailure, while the service returns the actionable\n        // [accessibility_not_enabled] message the model needs. The service\n        // self-checks via checkStatus() (AccessibilityInspectorService\n        // .findElementsFormatted), so the omission is explicit and deliberate.\n        selfEnforcesPermission: true,"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 371,
                        "endLine": 372
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "securityTier: SecurityTier.tier2Permission,\n        // No checkPermission: the OS capture Activity raised by image_picker is\n        // the runtime gate and performs the request itself; a check-only probe\n        // here would block first-use capture before any prompt could be shown.\n        selfEnforcesPermission: true,\n        execute: (args) => service.takePhoto("
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Make the Tier 2 gate fail closed in `ToolRegistry.executeTool`. A Tier 2 tool that declares no permission checker must be refused before its `execute` closure runs, rather than falling through to execution.\n2. Make the deliberate omission explicit rather than implicit. The gate currently cannot distinguish \"the author forgot\" from \"the author intended the service to self-enforce\". Introduce an explicit opt-in marker on `RegisteredTool` (for example a `selfEnforcesPermission` flag defaulting to false) and treat a Tier 2 tool with neither a checker nor the marker as a hard failure.\n3. Apply the marker only to the two registrations where another mechanism genuinely performs the evaluation, and record the reason in a comment at the registration site: `ui_find_elements`, where the accessibility inspector service already returns a status-specific failure before reading the tree, and `camera_take_photo`, where the OS capture Activity raised by the image picker performs the runtime request and enforcement itself.\n4. Resolve the tier of the remaining affected registrations, which currently have no runtime permission to evaluate and therefore cannot supply a meaningful checker: `launch_system_setting` and `open_email_composer` should require human approval before they run, because they land the user on privilege-granting settings pages or create an outbound message the user did not author; `nfc_read_tag`, `nfc_read_emoney_balance`, and `open_external_url` should be treated as non-permissioned, because NFC has no runtime permission and requires a physical tap, and the URL launcher already restricts schemes to http and https. This is a policy decision for the maintainers and should be ratified rather than applied silently.\n5. Add a registry-level invariant test asserting that every registered tool whose tier is not Tier 1 declares either a permission checker or the self-enforcement marker, so a future omission fails the build instead of silently bypassing the gate.\n6. Review the same gate for the analogous no-op case already present in the codebase, where a checker is supplied but unconditionally returns true; a present-but-vacuous checker is not distinguishable from a real one at the call site.",
                "markdown": "1. Make the Tier 2 gate fail closed in `ToolRegistry.executeTool`. A Tier 2 tool that declares no permission checker must be refused before its `execute` closure runs, rather than falling through to execution.\n2. Make the deliberate omission explicit rather than implicit. The gate currently cannot distinguish \"the author forgot\" from \"the author intended the service to self-enforce\". Introduce an explicit opt-in marker on `RegisteredTool` (for example a `selfEnforcesPermission` flag defaulting to false) and treat a Tier 2 tool with neither a checker nor the marker as a hard failure.\n3. Apply the marker only to the two registrations where another mechanism genuinely performs the evaluation, and record the reason in a comment at the registration site: `ui_find_elements`, where the accessibility inspector service already returns a status-specific failure before reading the tree, and `camera_take_photo`, where the OS capture Activity raised by the image picker performs the runtime request and enforcement itself.\n4. Resolve the tier of the remaining affected registrations, which currently have no runtime permission to evaluate and therefore cannot supply a meaningful checker: `launch_system_setting` and `open_email_composer` should require human approval before they run, because they land the user on privilege-granting settings pages or create an outbound message the user did not author; `nfc_read_tag`, `nfc_read_emoney_balance`, and `open_external_url` should be treated as non-permissioned, because NFC has no runtime permission and requires a physical tap, and the URL launcher already restricts schemes to http and https. This is a policy decision for the maintainers and should be ratified rather than applied silently.\n5. Add a registry-level invariant test asserting that every registered tool whose tier is not Tier 1 declares either a permission checker or the self-enforcement marker, so a future omission fails the build instead of silently bypassing the gate.\n6. Review the same gate for the analogous no-op case already present in the codebase, where a checker is supplied but unconditionally returns true; a present-but-vacuous checker is not distinguishable from a real one at the call site."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "8e5a698c0b045285f5d3f20aae9ee1f43e35a2960de61ef18b1366ea475229e8"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6c1f6564d477eecb3b764ee0f85127f08ae0ca9c6480b864746c9f416d702556",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0006",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-15 06:25:47 UTC",
              "target": "Flames Android app (Flutter package `flutter_ai_agent`) — `lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart`",
              "endpoint": "lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart",
              "cwe": "CWE-863",
              "impact": "The application's declared authorization invariant — that a Tier 2 capability runs only after its runtime permission is evaluated — is not enforced for tools registered without a checker. The two most consequential affected tools are `launch_system_setting`, which can navigate the user to the notification-listener, accessibility, and overlay-apps settings pages (the exact pages that grant this app additional device authority), and `open_email_composer`, which can prefill an outbound message. Both can be driven by untrusted text reaching the model, with no in-app confirmation. The demonstrated effect is bounded: the operating system toggle and the mail client's send button still require a user action, so no permission is granted and no message is sent by the tool call itself. The remaining affected tools are neutralised by other controls (scheme allow-list, OS capture UI, physical NFC tap). The durable risk is that the gate silently degrades any future Tier 2 tool that omits the field.",
              "technical_analysis": "`SecurityTier` documents the intended contract as *\"Tier 2: Sensitive / Permissioned (auto-execute only if runtime permission is granted)\"* (`lib/domain/tools/chat/security_policy.dart:1-10`), and `SecurityPolicy.requiresPermission` returns true for that tier (`security_policy.dart:26-28`). The registry is the single place that contract is enforced:\n\n```dart title=lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart startLineNumber=250 endLineNumber=256\n      if (SecurityPolicy.requiresPermission(tool.securityTier) &&\n          tool.checkPermission != null) {\n        final granted = await tool.checkPermission!();\n        if (!granted) {\n          return Left(PermissionFailure('Permission denied for tool: $name'));\n        }\n      }\n```\n\nThe second conjunct is the defect. When `checkPermission` is null the whole block is skipped and control falls through to `return await tool.execute(arguments);` (`tool_registry.dart:257`), so the tool behaves as Tier 1. `checkPermission` is an optional constructor parameter (`tool_registry.dart:51`, `:59`), so nothing prevents the omission.\n\n`SecurityPolicy.requiresPermission` is referenced in exactly one place in the application source — this gate — so there is no secondary Tier 2 enforcement point. `executeTool` is defined at `tool_registry.dart:238` and has three call sites: `lib/presentation/screen/chat/cubit/agent_cubit.dart:611` (resume after approval), `agent_cubit.dart:766` (ReAct loop), and `lib/domain/services/gateway/gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:148` (remote gateway frame). The first two gate only Tier 3 (`agent_cubit.dart:753`, with the comment *\"Tier 1 & Tier 2: Auto-execute.\"* at `:764`); the gateway path gates only Tier 3 (`gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:89`). Tier 2 therefore depends entirely on the fail-open gate.\n\nThe tool name and arguments are unvalidated model output on the ReAct path (`agent_cubit.dart:717` reads `toolCall.name`, `:723` `jsonDecode`s the model-supplied argument fragment) and server-supplied data on the gateway path (`gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:148-151` passes `request.tool` and `request.arguments`).\n\nAffected registrations, all declared `SecurityTier.tier2Permission` with no `checkPermission` and no equivalent check inside the called service:\n\n- `camera_take_photo` — tier at `tool_registry.dart:371`\n- `open_external_url` — tier at `tool_registry.dart:1432`\n- `open_email_composer` — tier at `tool_registry.dart:1493`\n- `launch_system_setting` — tier at `tool_registry.dart:1524`\n- `nfc_read_tag` — tier at `tool_registry.dart:1568`\n- `nfc_read_emoney_balance` — tier at `tool_registry.dart:1621`\n- `ui_find_elements` — tier at `tool_registry.dart:1760` (documented deliberate omission; the service self-checks — see counterevidence)\n\nFive of these carry no explanatory comment, which distinguishes them from the deliberate case. The omission is a registration defect, not remote tampering: `securityTier` is a `final` field (`tool_registry.dart:49`) assigned only from literals, there are no assignment sites for `securityTier`/`checkPermission` anywhere in the source, and `RegisteredTool` is constructed in only two files (`tool_registry.dart` and `lib/data/datasources/skills/doc_builder_skill.dart:39`). `toOpenAiTool()` (`tool_registry.dart:62-71`) exposes neither the tier nor the checker to the model or the gateway manifest. Imported skills are force-disabled on import (`lib/domain/services/skills/skill_security_scanner.dart:189-190`), and the skill registry only toggles enablement (`lib/domain/services/skills/skills_registry_service.dart:386-405`).",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Make the Tier 2 gate fail closed in `ToolRegistry.executeTool`. A Tier 2 tool that declares no permission checker must be refused before its `execute` closure runs, rather than falling through to execution.\n2. Make the deliberate omission explicit rather than implicit. The gate currently cannot distinguish \"the author forgot\" from \"the author intended the service to self-enforce\". Introduce an explicit opt-in marker on `RegisteredTool` (for example a `selfEnforcesPermission` flag defaulting to false) and treat a Tier 2 tool with neither a checker nor the marker as a hard failure.\n3. Apply the marker only to the two registrations where another mechanism genuinely performs the evaluation, and record the reason in a comment at the registration site: `ui_find_elements`, where the accessibility inspector service already returns a status-specific failure before reading the tree, and `camera_take_photo`, where the OS capture Activity raised by the image picker performs the runtime request and enforcement itself.\n4. Resolve the tier of the remaining affected registrations, which currently have no runtime permission to evaluate and therefore cannot supply a meaningful checker: `launch_system_setting` and `open_email_composer` should require human approval before they run, because they land the user on privilege-granting settings pages or create an outbound message the user did not author; `nfc_read_tag`, `nfc_read_emoney_balance`, and `open_external_url` should be treated as non-permissioned, because NFC has no runtime permission and requires a physical tap, and the URL launcher already restricts schemes to http and https. This is a policy decision for the maintainers and should be ratified rather than applied silently.\n5. Add a registry-level invariant test asserting that every registered tool whose tier is not Tier 1 declares either a permission checker or the self-enforcement marker, so a future omission fails the build instead of silently bypassing the gate.\n6. Review the same gate for the analogous no-op case already present in the codebase, where a checker is supplied but unconditionally returns true; a present-but-vacuous checker is not distinguishable from a real one at the call site.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest case against this finding is that for most of the affected tools there is no runtime permission for the gate to evaluate, so \"the gate did not run\" may have no consequence beyond a misleading tier label. That argument holds for part of the list and was tested tool by tool.\n\n- `camera_take_photo` — neutralised. The `execute` closure reaches `image_picker`'s OS capture Activity (`lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/camera_vision_service.dart:73-87`), which is a foreground UI the user must operate and which raises the runtime permission request itself; a denial surfaces as `PermissionFailure` at `camera_vision_service.dart:100-107`. The `retrieveLostData()` pre-path (`:50-67`) only recovers a capture the user already took. Adding a check-only probe here would in fact regress first use, because a read-only probe cannot raise the prompt.\n- `open_external_url` — neutralised. `lib/domain/tools/chat/contact_launcher_tools.dart:159-163` rejects any URI that is not `http`/`https` with a non-empty host, so `intent://`, `javascript:`, `file:`, and `market://` are refused, and the launch uses `LaunchMode.externalApplication` (`:170`). No runtime permission exists for opening a browser URL, so the omitted check skips no evaluation.\n- `open_email_composer` — partially neutralised. It only builds a `mailto:` URI and launches the composer (`lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/intent_launcher_service_impl.dart:186-205`); the recipient goes to the URI path and subject/body to percent-encoded query parameters, so there is no header or query injection, and no message is sent. No runtime permission exists for composing mail. What remains is a model-driven prefill of an outbound message the user did not author.\n- `launch_system_setting` — partially neutralised. The allow-list is enforced on both sides (`intent_launcher_service_impl.dart:223`, `MainActivity.kt:431-448`), so no arbitrary settings action can be injected, and no runtime permission exists for launching a settings page. However the allow-list deliberately includes `notification_listener`, `accessibility`, and `overlay`, the pages that grant this app further authority, and the tool description itself instructs the model to use them (`tool_registry.dart:1505-1512`). The consequence is a privilege-chaining primer; the user must still operate the OS toggle, and nothing in the source shows the tool can flip it.\n- `nfc_read_tag`, `nfc_read_emoney_balance` — neutralised and mislabelled. `android.permission.NFC` is an install-time permission for which Android shows no runtime dialog, so there is no runtime permission to evaluate; the real gates are physical (a card must be tapped within a twenty-second window — `lib/data/datasources/tools/device/nfc_service.dart:70-80`, `:260-277`) and both tools are read-only.\n- `ui_find_elements` — deliberate, documented omission, not a defect. The registration carries the comment *\"No checkPermission: the registry gate would return a generic PermissionFailure, while the service returns the actionable [accessibility_not_enabled] message the model needs\"* (`tool_registry.dart:1761-1763`) and the service independently refuses to read the tree unless the service is active (`lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/accessibility_inspector_service.dart:109-112`, via `checkStatus()` at `:72-86`). A naive fail-closed patch would silently change this documented behaviour, which is why the recommended fix uses an explicit marker.\n\nChecked and found absent: no remote path can influence a tool's tier or checker (only literal assignments, two construction sites, and `toOpenAiTool()` exposes neither); no skill enablement requirement gates these tools (`register()` defaults to enabled at `tool_registry.dart:210`, and a tool listed in no skill is never disabled — `open_email_composer`, `launch_system_setting`, and both NFC tools appear in no bundled skill); no direct call to a tool's `execute` closure exists anywhere outside the registry (`tool.execute` is invoked at exactly one place, `tool_registry.dart:257`). Nothing here demonstrates confidentiality or availability impact, and no permission is actually granted or message sent by a tool call alone — the impact metrics below reflect only the limited, user-mediated effect that could be evidenced. No runtime confirmation was possible: there is no emulator, device, or Flutter/Dart toolchain in the assessment environment, so the defect was never executed.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "Static trace only. The defect mechanism and its reachability are fully evidenced in source — the fail-open condition, the single enforcement point, the two auto-execute paths, the model-supplied tool name and arguments, and the seven registrations — and the claim was independently reviewed and could not be refuted. The gap is that no tool call was ever dispatched: there is no emulator, no device, and no Flutter/Dart toolchain available, so neither the vulnerable nor the patched behaviour was observed at runtime, and the severity of the downstream effect (a settings page opening, a mail draft being prefilled) rests on source reading rather than observation.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that the settings navigation or the email prefill can be converted into an actual privilege grant or message send without a distinct user action — for example an automated tapping path reachable from the same ungated tool invocation — would raise this to High; conversely, a runtime demonstration that the tool call alone produces no state change at all would lower it to informational.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 — Security closure: REASONED, NOT EXECUTED. Tracing the patched source, a Tier 2 tool with a null checker and `selfEnforcesPermission == false` now returns `Left(ServerFailure(...))` at `tool_registry.dart:250-256` before reaching `tool.execute(arguments)` at `:257`. Because `tool.execute` is invoked at exactly one place in the application source and all three `executeTool` call sites (`agent_cubit.dart:611`, `agent_cubit.dart:766`, `gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:148`) route through that method, no path reaches an affected tool's closure. The previously-silent omission now fails closed. This was not executed: no Dart/Flutter toolchain is available, so neither the vulnerable nor the patched behaviour was observed.\n\nGate 2 — Bypass review: reviewed without relying on the rationale that produced the patch. Sibling call sites of the same helper were enumerated (`tool.execute` at one site only); equivalent sinks were checked (`executeTool` has three callers, all through the gate); and the alternative input class — remote influence over the tier or checker — was tested and refuted (`securityTier` is `final`, assigned only from literals; `RegisteredTool` is constructed in two files; `toOpenAiTool()` exposes neither the tier nor the checker). One deliberate direction change was identified and accepted: five registrations that currently auto-execute will now be refused until their tier is resolved, which is fail-closed rather than fail-open.\n\nGate 3 — Preserved behaviour: PARTIAL. `ui_find_elements` keeps its documented actionable error, and `camera_take_photo` keeps first-use capture, because both are marked as self-enforcing; a check-only probe on the camera tool would otherwise have blocked the first permission prompt. The legitimate inputs and error semantics for the remaining five registrations change: `open_external_url`, `open_email_composer`, `launch_system_setting`, `nfc_read_tag`, and `nfc_read_emoney_balance` will return a failure instead of executing. That is intentional — their Tier 2 label has no runtime permission behind it — but it is a behaviour change that must be applied together with the tier decision in remediation step 4, and it will require the existing execution tests for those tools to be updated (for example `test/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry_test.dart:916` and `:1027`, `test/domain/tools/nfc_tools_test.dart:42` and `:100`). This is the gate that could not be fully cleared, and it is a product decision rather than a code gap.\n\nGate 4 — Repository checks: NOT RUN, and this is an explicit gap. `dart analyze` and `flutter test` are unavailable in the assessment environment; there is no Dart or Flutter SDK. The patch was written against the file's existing style and imports (`ServerFailure` and `PermissionFailure` are already imported and used in the same method at `:245` and `:254`), and the line ranges were verified against the file, but no formatter, analyzer, or test suite was executed. A reviewer must run the package's own checks before merging.\n\nNarrowest-complete-change judgement: the smallest change that fully enforces the invariant is the fail-closed gate plus the explicit marker; the marker is required rather than optional, because without it the patch cannot distinguish a forgotten checker from a deliberate one and would silently break the accessibility tool's documented error semantics.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Read the enforcement point at `lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart:250-256` and observe that the Tier 2 branch requires both a Tier 2 tier and a non-null `checkPermission`.\n2. Read the `RegisteredTool` declaration at `tool_registry.dart:45-60` and confirm `checkPermission` is optional.\n3. Read each affected registration (`camera_take_photo` :371, `open_external_url` :1432, `open_email_composer` :1493, `launch_system_setting` :1524, `nfc_read_tag` :1568, `nfc_read_emoney_balance` :1621) and confirm the field is absent and that its `execute` closure delegates directly to the service.\n4. Read each target service and confirm it performs no permission evaluation of its own on that path.\n5. Compare against a sibling Tier 2 tool that does declare a checker, for example `get_location` at `tool_registry.dart:317` (`checkPermission: () => locationService.checkPermission()`), whose implementation calls the runtime location permission API at `lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/location_service.dart:26-35` — this establishes that the field is load-bearing rather than decorative.\n6. Trace the two invocation paths that treat non-Tier-3 tools as auto-executable: `lib/presentation/screen/chat/cubit/agent_cubit.dart:753-768` and `lib/domain/services/gateway/gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:89-151`.\n7. Confirm reachability of the model-supplied tool name and arguments at `agent_cubit.dart:717` and `:720-728`.\n8. Optionally execute the accompanying unit test to demonstrate the behaviour. This step was NOT performed: no Flutter/Dart toolchain, emulator, or device was available in the assessment environment, so the finding is static-only and unconfirmed at runtime.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-22",
          "ruleIndex": 5,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unrestricted file read and unbounded directory enumeration via read_device_file / list_device_files agent tools\n\nThe agent tools **`read_device_file`** and **`list_device_files`** accept an absolute filesystem path supplied by the language model and pass it to `File(path).readAsBytes()` / `Directory(path).list()` without any containment check against a workspace root. The declared \"device workspace\" boundary exists only in the tools' prose descriptions and is never enforced in code.\n\nThe one gate that was supposed to constrain `read_device_file` is inert: its Tier-2 permission check resolves to a hardcoded `true`. **`list_device_files` is declared Tier-1 (auto-execute) and therefore has no gate at all**, and it accepts `recursive: true`.\n\nBoth paths are reachable from remote model output — tool-call arguments are decoded from the model's JSON with no validation and auto-executed. Because untrusted text (notification bodies, remote skill packages, tool results) reaches the model with no data/instruction separation, a prompt-injection payload can direct the agent to read or enumerate files. **Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated** — this is a source-only audit with no emulator, no device and no running app."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "agent tool: read_device_file / list_device_files",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "46234e7eca309aff68c3f0fa89728fb20717ccfac065b721c466230133e6399b"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "b0ac9c15dfb816d1a7d191b5f1bca60f8aa5f3441699a4baad8373c8c2d2ba90",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0007",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-15 06:29:20 UTC",
              "target": "Flames Android app (ai.hermes.flames) — repository at /workspace/flames-audit-t196",
              "endpoint": "agent tool: read_device_file / list_device_files",
              "method": "N/A (in-app agent tool invocation, not HTTP)",
              "cwe": "CWE-22",
              "impact": "An attacker who influences the model's tool-call arguments (a remote or compromised model endpoint, a remote skill package, a crafted notification body, or an indirect injection via any tool result) can direct the agent to read and enumerate files as the application's own UID, with no workspace boundary enforced.\n\n**Demonstrated by code path (not runtime-verified):**\n\n- **Unbounded directory enumeration — fully effective.** `list_device_files` is Tier 1 (auto-execute, no gate) and accepts `recursive: true`. It returns names, absolute paths, sizes, modification times and extensions for the whole subtree. Directory listing applies no content filter of any kind, so it enumerates the app's entire private storage — including the `databases/`, `shared_prefs/`, `files/` and `app_flutter/` trees. This discloses the storage layout and reveals which features hold data, which is meaningful reconnaissance for follow-on attacks and is not blocked by any control.\n- **Read of NUL-free text files within the app's UID.** `read_device_file` returns any text file the app can open, including application-private XML and JSON. This includes the FlutterSecureStorage backing file under the app's `shared_prefs/` directory (the app constructs `const FlutterSecureStorage()` with no options override at `lib/common/injection/modules.dart:22-23`, so plugin defaults determine the filename; the exact name was not observed on a device). That file holds the **encrypted** credential blobs for the API key, gateway token and server profiles. Because these are ciphertext, this does not by itself disclose the credentials — it discloses the encrypted material, the stored key names, and the existence and structure of the credential store.\n- **Filter bypass via the PDF branch.** A path ending in `.pdf` is routed to PDF text extraction *before* the extension denylist and null-byte checks run, and `extractPdfText` applies neither filter. Any file addressed under a `.pdf` name is therefore read with no binary screening at all.\n\n**Explicitly NOT demonstrated, contrary to an earlier draft of this finding:** the plaintext SQLite databases (`flames_memory.db`, `flames_skills.db`, `flames_chat.db`) are **not** readable through `read_device_file`. They are blocked twice over — `db`/`sqlite` appear in the extension denylist, and a real SQLite file contains a NUL byte at offset 15 of its header, which the null-byte sample rejects regardless of filename. Memory-vault contents are also already available to the model through the Tier-1 `list_memories` / `search_memories` tools, so reading the database file would add little.\n\n**Why this is still a finding.** The app intends a workspace boundary — its own bundled skill descriptor states the invariant \"read file contents within allowed directories\" — and no code enforces it. The absence of containment is the root defect: any future text file the app writes into its private storage (a token cache, a log, an export) becomes immediately readable by model output, and enumeration of the private tree is ungated today. Scope limits, stated honestly: no `READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE` / `MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE` is declared, so arbitrary `/sdcard` reads fail on modern Android; impact is confined to the app's own private storage.",
              "technical_analysis": "**1. `read_device_file` — declared Tier 2, gate is a no-op**\n\nRegistration at `lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart:759-790` declares `securityTier: SecurityTier.tier2Permission` and wires `checkPermission: () => service.checkPermission()`. The parameter is documented as an **absolute file path**. The implementation at `lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/file_workspace_service.dart:77-81` is:\n\n```dart title=lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/file_workspace_service.dart startLineNumber=77 endLineNumber=81\n  @override\n  Future<bool> checkPermission() async => true;\n\n  @override\n  Future<bool> requestPermission() async => true;\n```\n\nThe registry's only permission gate (`tool_registry.dart:250-255`) therefore always passes:\n\n```dart title=lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart startLineNumber=250 endLineNumber=256\n      if (SecurityPolicy.requiresPermission(tool.securityTier) &&\n          tool.checkPermission != null) {\n        final granted = await tool.checkPermission!();\n        if (!granted) {\n          return Left(PermissionFailure('Permission denied for tool: $name'));\n        }\n      }\n```\n\n**2. `readFile` performs no containment, and the PDF branch skips both filters**\n\n`file_workspace_service.dart:149-175` reads the raw caller-supplied path. There is **no** `startsWith` check, **no** canonicalisation, **no** base-directory join and **no** symlink resolution. `getDocumentsDirectory()` — the only workspace-root accessor — is used solely for PDF *writes* (`lib/data/datasources/skills/doc_builder_skill.dart:86`), never to bound reads. A whole-tree search of `lib/` for containment vocabulary returns no hit on this path.\n\nThe two filters that do exist are applied only on the non-PDF branch, and only after the PDF dispatch:\n\n```dart title=lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/file_workspace_service.dart startLineNumber=159 endLineNumber=169\n      final ext = _extensionOf(path).toLowerCase();\n      if (ext == 'pdf') {\n        final result = await extractPdfText(path);\n        return result.map((text) => _paginate(text, maxLines: maxLines, offset: offset));\n      }\n      final bytes = await _fileSystem.readFileAsBytes(path);\n      if (_binaryExtensions.contains(ext) || _containsNullByte(bytes)) {\n        return Left(CacheFailure('Binary file not readable: $path'));\n      }\n      final content = await _fileSystem.readFileAsString(path);\n```\n\n`extractPdfText` (`:178-211`) reads the file with `readAsBytes` and applies neither `_binaryExtensions` nor `_containsNullByte`. Any path ending in `.pdf` is therefore read with no binary screening.\n\n**3. `list_device_files` — declared Tier 1, no gate at all**\n\n`tool_registry.dart:791-815` registers it at `securityTier: SecurityTier.tier1Auto` with no `checkPermission`, accepting an arbitrary `directoryPath` and `recursive`. `listFiles` (`file_workspace_service.dart:224-281`) calls `Directory(path).list(recursive: recursive)` unbounded, and directory listing applies no content filter.\n\n**4. The \"workspace\" boundary is prose, not a control**\n\nThe tool description says \"device workspace directory\", and the bundled skill descriptor states the intended invariant — `lib/domain/services/skills/skills_registry_service.dart:643`: \"Inspect file paths safely and read file contents within allowed directories.\" No code implements \"allowed directories\".\n\n**5. What the filters do and do not block**\n\n`db` and `sqlite` are present in `_binaryExtensions`, and a real SQLite file carries a NUL byte at offset 15 (`SQLite format 3\\0`), which `_containsNullByte` over the first 8000 bytes rejects independently. The plaintext databases are therefore blocked by both filters. They do not protect text files (XML/JSON/logs), and they do not apply to the `.pdf` branch.\n\n**6. Attacker reachability**\n\nTool-call arguments originate from the model and are decoded without validation in `lib/presentation/screen/chat/cubit/agent_cubit.dart:720-726` (`arguments = jsonDecode(toolCall.argumentsFragment)`), then passed straight to the registry at `:766-768`. Only `tier3Hitl` tools pause for approval (`:760-765`); Tier 1 and Tier 2 auto-execute. No layer validates, normalises or confines `path` or `directoryPath`. The HITL sheet does render the real argument JSON (`lib/presentation/screen/chat/widgets/action_approval_sheet.dart:44-45`), so raising a tier is an effective control where it applies — but neither tool reaches it.",
              "remediation_steps": "Enforce a workspace boundary on every agent file tool, at the point where the model-supplied argument enters.\n\n1. Introduce a single containment primitive on the file-workspace service, and use it in the `execute` closure of both `read_device_file` and `list_device_files` before any filesystem access. Do not rely on the tool description to communicate the boundary.\n2. Resolve the requested path against the application documents directory, canonicalise it (collapse dot segments and normalise separators), and reject anything that is not a strict descendant of that root. Compare with a trailing-separator-aware prefix test so a sibling directory sharing the root's name prefix is not accepted.\n3. Resolve symbolic links on both the candidate path and the root before the final comparison, so a symlink created inside the workspace cannot be used to reach outside it.\n4. Replace the hardcoded permission grant in the default filesystem wrapper with a real evaluation. If the intended gate is a user-facing one, wire it to an actual platform permission or approval rather than returning a constant.\n5. Raise `list_device_files` from Tier 1 (auto-execute) to at least Tier 2 so enumeration is no longer performed without evaluation; promote it to Tier 3 if enumeration of the private data directory should require explicit user approval. Note that the HITL sheet already renders the real argument JSON, so a Tier-3 promotion would show the user the exact directory being listed.\n6. Add regression tests that assert an absolute path outside the workspace, a traversal sequence, and a symlink escape are each rejected, and that an in-workspace path still succeeds.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively sought constraints, and **two of them defeated part of my own initial claim** — recorded here rather than hidden.\n\n**Constraints that genuinely limit impact.** (1) The plaintext databases are NOT readable: `db` and `sqlite` are in `_binaryExtensions` (`file_workspace_service.dart:98-124`) *and* any real SQLite file has a NUL byte at offset 15, which `_containsNullByte` (`:133-140`) rejects independently of the filename. My first draft asserted these were readable; that was wrong and has been corrected. (2) Memory-vault content is already reachable by the model through the Tier-1 `list_memories` / `search_memories` tools (`tool_registry.dart:905-945`), so reading the vault file would add nothing. (3) Chat message bodies live in `FlutterSecureStorage`, not in `flames_chat.db`, so the database holds only session titles in any case. (4) No `READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE` / `MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE` is declared anywhere under `android/`, so `/sdcard` reads fail on modern Android — broad external-storage access is not claimed. (5) The data belongs to the same user who installed the app; this is not cross-tenant exposure. (6) The attacker must first land influence on the model's arguments; this is not unauthenticated access to a listening service.\n\n**What is NOT a control.** The binary-extension denylist and the null-byte sample do not cover the `.pdf` branch: `readFile` dispatches to `extractPdfText` *before* either check runs (`:159-163`), and `extractPdfText` (`:178-211`) applies neither. The denylist also inspects only the filename extension, so it constrains naming rather than access. Directory enumeration passes through no filter at all. I verified there is no canonicalisation, no base-directory join and no symlink resolution anywhere on either call path, and that `getDocumentsDirectory()` is used only for PDF writes.\n\n**Checked and not reachable as a bypass.** Argument mutation between approval and execution: the Tier-3 HITL sheet renders the real `request.parameters` JSON (`action_approval_sheet.dart:44-45`) and `approveAction()` executes the stored object, so there is no TOCTOU window — moot here, since neither tool reaches HITL. Tier downgrade by remote input: the tier is a local `const` on `RegisteredTool` with no setter, so remote input cannot change it; the defect is the tiers as declared in source.\n\n**Residual.** A symlink planted inside the workspace would defeat a lexical-only fix; my verification exercised that case and confirmed the symlink check is required. A check-to-open TOCTOU swap is theoretically possible but no model-reachable primitive creates symlinks (`export_document_pdf` writes regular files only), so I do not treat it as exploitable.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "Static-only trace. No emulator, adb or running app instance was available, so no step was executed: I could not confirm at runtime which absolute paths the app process can actually open, nor observe a real model-emitted tool call reaching the sink. The source-to-sink path is complete, every control on it was inspected individually, and the two filters that limit impact were analysed against real SQLite header bytes. The remediation's containment logic was executed in isolation (16/16 cases, including a symlink escape). The specific gap is runtime confirmation of the reachable file set and of the actual contents of `shared_prefs/FlutterSecureStorage.xml` on a real device.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that a NUL-free text file containing a plaintext secret (for example an unencrypted token or key cache) exists in the app's private storage and is returned by `read_device_file` would raise this to high; conversely, if device testing shows `shared_prefs/FlutterSecureStorage.xml` is not readable or contains only opaque ciphertext with no useful metadata, the confidentiality impact would fall to low and the finding would reduce to the ungated enumeration plus the missing boundary.",
              "fix_verification": "Gates worked in order. **Gate 1 (applicability)** and **Gate 4 (repository checks)** could only be partially executed — the Flutter package has no `.dart_tool/package_config.json`, no pub cache and no network, so a package-wide `dart analyze`/`dart test` was impossible. What I did run: `dart format --output=none` over all three patched files reported no parse errors; the patch was applied to the working tree, diffed byte-identical against an independently prepared copy, then reverted so the `fix_before` blocks remain verbatim against the current checkout.\n\n**Gate 2 (security closure)** — re-traced source-to-sink through the patched code. `read_device_file` and `list_device_files` now evaluate `service.isWithinWorkspace(path)` before reaching `readFile`/`listFilesFormattedJson` and return `ValidationFailure` when it is false. `isWithinWorkspace` returns false when the path is empty, when the workspace root cannot be resolved, or when the canonicalised path is not under the canonicalised root; it then re-checks containment after `resolveSymbolicLinks()` on both the candidate and the root. Because the guard sits in the `execute` closure — the single point where the model-supplied argument enters — every caller that passes model input is covered. I could not re-run the original PoC (no device), so closure rests on the isolated execution below plus the trace.\n\n**Executed:** the containment logic was run against a real filesystem in a standalone Dart program, 16/16 cases correct — traversal (`../../../databases/flames_memory.db`), sibling-database paths, `/etc/hosts`, `/sdcard/Download/secret.txt`, doubled separators, dot-segments, and prefix confusion against a sibling directory named `app_flutter_backup` (which a naive `startsWith(root)` would wrongly allow). A symlink created inside the workspace pointing at a file outside it was correctly **rejected after resolution** while the lexical check alone allowed it — that case is why the symlink step is required.\n\n**Gate 3 (bypass review)** — re-read the diff against the sibling call sites. Two were checked: (a) `chat_screen.dart:880` `_pickDocument()` also calls `readFile`, but with a path chosen by the user through the platform picker, which may legitimately be outside the workspace; this is why containment is enforced at the tool boundary rather than inside `readFile` — confining the service would have broken the file-attachment feature and eight existing unit tests that use paths such as `/docs/note.txt` against a fake root of `/fake/docs`. (b) `export_document_pdf` writes through the same wrapper but creates regular files only, so it offers no symlink-creation primitive to defeat the check; a check-to-open TOCTOU swap is therefore theoretical here. Alternate input classes tried: doubled separators, dot-segments, and symlink escape (all rejected).\n\n**Gate 4 (preserved behaviour)** — legitimate inputs verified as still accepted: a path inside the workspace, the workspace root itself, and `dir/..` resolving back inside. Error semantics are preserved — `CacheFailure`/`ValidationFailure` as before, and the existing missing-file path still returns `Left`. The single new interface member (`isWithinWorkspace`) is satisfied by the real implementation; the only other implementers of the interface are Mockito mocks (`test/helpers/mocks.dart:135`, `test/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry_test.dart:27`), which auto-satisfy added members, so no test file needed changing.\n\n**Deliberate design note for the reviewer:** moving `list_device_files` from Tier 1 to Tier 2 raises it off auto-execute and gives it a permission evaluation, consistent with the codebase's own tier definitions (`security_policy.dart:3-5`: Tier 1 = read-only/ambient, Tier 3 = state-modifying/destructive). It is the *containment check*, not the tier, that enforces the invariant for both tools — a reviewer wanting an explicit user tap for enumeration could instead promote it to Tier 3, and the HITL sheet would then display the real `directoryPath` argument (`action_approval_sheet.dart:44-45`).",
              "poc": {
                "description": "No device or emulator was available, so the end-to-end exploit was not executed. The following is the source-level attack path, with a static harness that proves the control is absent.\n\n1. Establish the attacker-controlled input. Any of: a remote or compromised OpenAI-compatible endpoint returning a crafted tool call, an imported remote skill package, a notification body read by the listener service, or an indirect injection carried in a tool result. All reach these paths with no data/instruction separation.\n2. For enumeration, cause the model to emit a `list_device_files` tool call with `directoryPath` set to the app's private data directory and `recursive: true`. It is declared Tier 1 and executes with no gate at all, returning names, absolute paths, sizes, timestamps and extensions for the whole subtree — the app's entire private storage layout.\n3. For a read, cause the model to emit a `read_device_file` call whose `path` targets a text file under the app's private storage. The Tier-2 `checkPermission` returns hardcoded `true`, so the registry gate passes, and `readFile` opens the caller-supplied absolute path directly.\n4. Note the filter ordering: a path ending in `.pdf` is dispatched to `extractPdfText` before the extension denylist and null-byte checks run, and `extractPdfText` applies neither, so a `.pdf`-named path is read with no binary screening.\n5. The result is returned as the tool result, appended to the conversation as a `role: tool` message, and transmitted to the configured remote LLM endpoint.\n6. Note what is NOT reachable: the plaintext SQLite databases are blocked both by the `db`/`sqlite` extension denylist and by the NUL byte at offset 15 of any real SQLite header, which the null-byte sample rejects independently.\n\nStatic confirmation (no app execution): the harness reproduces the conditions above against the repository source and asserts the absence of the control — declared tiers, the hardcoded permission grant, the absence of any containment primitive in `readFile`/`listFiles`, the unused workspace-root getter, and the unvalidated `jsonDecode` of model arguments.\n\nThe remediation's containment logic was additionally executed in isolation against a real filesystem (16 cases: traversal, double-separator, dot-segment, sibling-prefix confusion, and a symlink inside the workspace pointing outside it). All 16 behaved correctly, including rejecting the symlink escape that a purely lexical check would have allowed.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Tier-2 OTP extraction tool bypasses the app's own sensitive-notification filter and transmits a live second-factor code off-device without per-use confirmation\n\nThe Flames agent tool `extract_latest_otp` extracts a live one-time passcode from another application's notification and returns the code to the configured large-language-model endpoint, while the application's own privacy filter deliberately excludes those same OTP and banking notifications from its sibling notification-reading tool. The tool is classified Tier 2 \"permissioned\", which means it executes automatically once Notification Listener access has been granted a single time, with no per-use user confirmation — so the one control the application built to keep second-factor codes away from the model is bypassed by a path that then transmits the code off the device."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 871,
                  "endLine": 871,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "securityTier: SecurityTier.tier2Permission,"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Reclassify extract_latest_otp from Tier 2 (auto-execute) to Tier 3 (per-use HITL approval) — the single chokepoint all three executeTool call sites consult"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/notification_triage_service.dart"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 283,
                  "endLine": 287,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (gate != null) return Left(gate);\n      final entries = await _loadEntries(limit: limit);\n      for (final entry in entries) {\n        if (!matchesAppFilter(entry, appFilter)) continue;\n        final otp = extractOtp('${entry.title} ${entry.body}');"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Informational — the bypass itself: this path loads the unfiltered notification list and never consults isSensitive, unlike the sibling getRecentNotifications at :249-252. Do NOT patch by adding an isSensitive check here (every OTP notification matches the keyword list, so the tool would permanently return found:false); the tier change above is the correct fix."
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "agent tool: extract_latest_otp",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 871,
                        "endLine": 871
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "securityTier: SecurityTier.tier3Hitl,"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Reclassify `extract_latest_otp` from Tier 2 (permissioned, auto-execute) to Tier 3 (HITL) in `lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart`, so every extraction requires an explicit per-use user confirmation showing the tool name and arguments. This is the minimal change that closes both the local-model and gateway transmission paths, because all three `executeTool` call sites consult the same registration-time tier constant.\n2. Update the tier expectation in `test/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry_test.dart`, which currently asserts the Tier-2 classification, and add a regression test asserting that the tool cannot execute without approval.\n3. Prefer, as a follow-up, never transmitting the passcode to the model at all: have `extract_latest_otp` return only a non-secret acknowledgement (for example, that a code was received and from which application) and surface the actual code in a local user interface element, so the secret never enters a prompt, a request body, or a gateway response.\n4. Do not attempt to close this by applying the `isSensitive` keyword filter inside `extractLatestOtp`: every OTP notification matches those keywords, so the tool would permanently report that no code was found. The keyword list is a topic filter for the general notification reader, not a redaction primitive for the extraction tool.\n5. Treat the notification-listener grant as a coarse capability rather than consent for any individual secret: audit the other notification-derived tools (`read_recent_notifications`) for the same assumption, and consider an explicit, revocable in-app toggle specifically for second-factor extraction.\n6. Consider whether the long-term memory vault should accept secret-shaped content at all, given that the auto-executing `save_memory` tool can persist an extracted code into an unencrypted SQLite database; at minimum, exclude OTP-shaped values from memory writes or encrypt the vault.",
                "markdown": "1. Reclassify `extract_latest_otp` from Tier 2 (permissioned, auto-execute) to Tier 3 (HITL) in `lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart`, so every extraction requires an explicit per-use user confirmation showing the tool name and arguments. This is the minimal change that closes both the local-model and gateway transmission paths, because all three `executeTool` call sites consult the same registration-time tier constant.\n2. Update the tier expectation in `test/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry_test.dart`, which currently asserts the Tier-2 classification, and add a regression test asserting that the tool cannot execute without approval.\n3. Prefer, as a follow-up, never transmitting the passcode to the model at all: have `extract_latest_otp` return only a non-secret acknowledgement (for example, that a code was received and from which application) and surface the actual code in a local user interface element, so the secret never enters a prompt, a request body, or a gateway response.\n4. Do not attempt to close this by applying the `isSensitive` keyword filter inside `extractLatestOtp`: every OTP notification matches those keywords, so the tool would permanently report that no code was found. The keyword list is a topic filter for the general notification reader, not a redaction primitive for the extraction tool.\n5. Treat the notification-listener grant as a coarse capability rather than consent for any individual secret: audit the other notification-derived tools (`read_recent_notifications`) for the same assumption, and consider an explicit, revocable in-app toggle specifically for second-factor extraction.\n6. Consider whether the long-term memory vault should accept secret-shaped content at all, given that the auto-executing `save_memory` tool can persist an extracted code into an unencrypted SQLite database; at minimum, exclude OTP-shaped values from memory writes or encrypt the vault."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "b7f9f94f5723fad816c6bf17164bc231c52ea7129f79f0580ded078be852fcfc"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.8",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "8698ed347c627fd509dad4e059c160d65866346baabb4361f0d3ca4c1e9f140f",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0008",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.8,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-15 06:36:26 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/flames-audit-t196 (Flames Android application, package ai.hermes.flames)",
              "endpoint": "agent tool: extract_latest_otp",
              "method": "N/A (in-app agent tool invocation, not HTTP)",
              "cwe": "CWE-863",
              "impact": "A live second-factor code — together with the sending application's name, package identifier, and a body snippet — is extracted from another application's notification and transmitted off the device to the configured large-language-model endpoint, and on the gateway path to the remote Hermes server, without any per-use user confirmation. Because the extraction path never consults `isSensitive`, the application's own exclusion of OTP, banking, and password notifications from the model is defeated: the one notification class the product deliberately withholds from the model is the exact class this tool hands over. Nothing binds the extracted code to a transaction the user initiated, and no confirmation step exists, so the code is disclosed to a third party that has no functional need for it. The repository's own end-to-end test further has the model persist the extracted code into the long-term memory vault via the auto-executing Tier-1 `save_memory` tool, and that vault is a plain, unencrypted SQLite database (`flames_memory.db`), which extends the window in which the code is readable at rest. On a device that receives OTPs for banking, email, or account recovery, this converts a short-lived authentication secret into a durable third-party disclosure.",
              "technical_analysis": "**The two notification paths disagree.** `NotificationTriageService` exposes one private loader and two public readers. `getRecentNotifications` (`lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/notification_triage_service.dart:242-262`) filters the loaded entries with `.where((e) => matchesAppFilter(e, appFilter) && !isSensitive(e))` at `:249-252`. `extractLatestOtp` (`:277-310`) calls the same loader directly at `:284` — `final entries = await _loadEntries(limit: limit);` — and never invokes `isSensitive`. Both therefore read the identical in-memory notification buffer, but only the first applies the application's sensitive-content exclusion. The `_sensitiveKeywords` list at `:87-118` includes `otp`, `one-time`, `verification code`, `password`, `passcode`, `pin`, `cvv`, `cvc`, `bank`, `transaction`, `payment`, `balance`, `account number`, and `credit card`, and `isSensitive` at `:119-122` matches them against the lowercased `title` and `body`. By construction, every notification this tool is designed to find is a notification the sibling tool refuses to return.\n\n**The extracted secret is returned in full.** `extractOtp` (`:137-151`) applies a contextual pattern `(?:code|otp|pin|passcode|verification)[^\\d]{0,24}(\\d{4,8})` and falls back to any bare four-to-eight digit group, so the match is not limited to notifications that literally say \"OTP\". On a hit, the service returns an `OtpExtractionResult` (`:290-297`) carrying the live code plus the sending application's display name, package name, timestamp, and a 120-character body snippet. `OtpExtractionResult.toJson` (`lib/domain/tools/chat/notification_triage_service.dart:79-89`) emits `'otp': otp` unconditionally, so the secret is present in the serialized tool result. There is no redaction, masking, or truncation of the code at any point.\n\n**No binding check ties the code to the user's own action.** `extractLatestOtp` accepts only `appFilter` and `limit` (`:277-280`); there is no transaction identifier, no correlation with a login the user initiated, and no verification that the user is the party the code is meant for. The tool returns whatever the newest matching notification contains, so an attacker-crafted notification can be used to have the agent surface the attacker's own code, and a code received on the victim's device for an attacker-initiated login can be harvested without any check that the user requested it.\n\n**The gate is auto-executing.** The tool is registered at `lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart:851` with `securityTier: SecurityTier.tier2Permission` at `:871` and `checkPermission: () => service.checkPermission()` at `:872`. The enforcement point at `:250-255` evaluates that permission once and never re-prompts; `checkPermission` in turn merely confirms the OS-level Notification Listener grant (`notification_triage_service.dart:196-207`), which is a single coarse toggle the user sets once. The agent loop confirms the tier semantics: `lib/presentation/screen/chat/cubit/agent_cubit.dart:759-765` pauses for human approval only when `registered.securityTier == SecurityTier.tier3Hitl`, and Tier 1 and Tier 2 fall through to `emit(ExecutingTool(...))` followed by `executeTool` at `:766`. The tier is read from a local constant fixed at registration and is never derived from model output, a gateway frame, a skill package, or a tool result, so the classification itself cannot be downgraded remotely — but the classification as written is the defect.\n\n**Two independent off-device sinks consume the result.** In the local ReAct loop the result string is appended as `ChatMessage(role: 'tool', toolCallId: callId, name: toolName, content: resultStr)` at `agent_cubit.dart:820-825`; `_messages.map((m) => m.toWireJson())` at `:906` serializes it, and `toWireJson` (`lib/domain/entities/chat/chat_message.dart:116-127`) writes `content` verbatim into the map; the resulting `messagesToSend` is passed to `_llmRepository.streamCompletion(...)` at `:923-930`. The endpoint is the user's configured model base URL, defaulting to `https://api.openai.com/v1` at `:121` and overridden from profile settings at `:482-483` and `:491-492`. Separately, when the call originates from a gateway `tool_call_request`, `lib/domain/services/gateway/gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:148` executes the same tool and the success branch at `:166-171` returns the full parsed result map — including the `otp` field — to the remote Hermes server via `gatewayClient.sendToolCallResponse`. Both sinks are governed by the same single tier constant, which is why reclassification closes both.\n\n**The value is also persisted.** `agent_cubit.dart:826` calls `_persistMessages()`, which writes the session history through `lib/data/datasources/local/chat/chat_local_datasource.dart:113-121` into FlutterSecureStorage — so the chat-history copy is encrypted at rest and is not itself a plaintext exposure. The stronger persistence path is the one the project's own integration test encodes: `test/integration/e2e_fase3_intelligence_test.dart:280-293` has the model follow an `extract_latest_otp` result with a `save_memory` call whose content is the extracted code. `save_memory` is Tier 1 and auto-executes (`tool_registry.dart:884`), writing into the memory vault, which is a plain SQLite database (`lib/data/datasources/local/memory/sqlite_memory_datasource.dart:16`, `databaseFileName = 'flames_memory.db'`) with no application-level encryption, so the code would be readable at rest beyond its normal validity window.\n\n**Intent.** The repository is explicit that this is a deliberate capability — the tool description says it \"Extracts the latest OTP code from captured notifications\", the `notification_triage` skill is documented as reading notifications and extracting security OTP verification codes (`lib/domain/services/skills/skills_registry_service.dart:610-625`), and the deployment documentation states that Flames \"requires Notification Listener permission to capture 2FA OTP codes\" (`docs/connecting-to-remote-hermes.md:270`). The finding is therefore not that the feature exists, but that the control around it is inconsistent with the application's own privacy design: the product deliberately withholds these notifications from the model on one path while handing the extracted secret to the model on another, and the path that hands it over is the one that requires no per-use confirmation.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Reclassify `extract_latest_otp` from Tier 2 (permissioned, auto-execute) to Tier 3 (HITL) in `lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart`, so every extraction requires an explicit per-use user confirmation showing the tool name and arguments. This is the minimal change that closes both the local-model and gateway transmission paths, because all three `executeTool` call sites consult the same registration-time tier constant.\n2. Update the tier expectation in `test/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry_test.dart`, which currently asserts the Tier-2 classification, and add a regression test asserting that the tool cannot execute without approval.\n3. Prefer, as a follow-up, never transmitting the passcode to the model at all: have `extract_latest_otp` return only a non-secret acknowledgement (for example, that a code was received and from which application) and surface the actual code in a local user interface element, so the secret never enters a prompt, a request body, or a gateway response.\n4. Do not attempt to close this by applying the `isSensitive` keyword filter inside `extractLatestOtp`: every OTP notification matches those keywords, so the tool would permanently report that no code was found. The keyword list is a topic filter for the general notification reader, not a redaction primitive for the extraction tool.\n5. Treat the notification-listener grant as a coarse capability rather than consent for any individual secret: audit the other notification-derived tools (`read_recent_notifications`) for the same assumption, and consider an explicit, revocable in-app toggle specifically for second-factor extraction.\n6. Consider whether the long-term memory vault should accept secret-shaped content at all, given that the auto-executing `save_memory` tool can persist an extracted code into an unencrypted SQLite database; at minimum, exclude OTP-shaped values from memory writes or encrypt the vault.",
              "counterevidence": "Argued both directions and tested each claim against the source rather than accepting it:\n\n1. \"It is a documented, intentional feature.\" TRUE — the tool description, the `notification_triage` skill text, and the repository's own integration test all establish that OTP extraction is intended. This does NOT neutralise the finding, because the objection addresses the feature's existence while the finding is about the control around it. The app's own sibling path deliberately excludes these exact notifications, and the feature ships with the weakest gate the registry has (auto-execute). A sensitive feature can be intended and still be under-gated.\n\n2. \"The user granted notification access.\" TRUE but does not neutralise it. That grant is a single coarse OS-level toggle whose system-dialog wording covers reading notifications generally; it is not a per-use consent for a specific live second-factor code, and it is never re-requested. The design distinction the app itself draws is per-use: Tier 3 tools prompt the user every time via the approval sheet. This tool never does.\n\n3. \"The user configured the LLM endpoint themselves.\" TRUE and it genuinely limits the threat model — the OTP goes to an origin the user chose, not to an arbitrary attacker origin. It does NOT eliminate the risk: it converts the finding from \"attacker exfiltration\" to \"unnecessary third-party disclosure of a live credential,\" and most deployments point at a cloud service.\n\n4. \"The model must cooperate (stochastic).\" TRUE — a real trigger is not guaranteed and depends on model behaviour. This is a genuine constraint and the reason the finding is medium rather than high; it is reflected in the CVSS complexity rating rather than treated as a kill.\n\n5. \"The OTP extraction was not executed.\" TRUE — no device was available. This is the reason for medium confidence, not a reason to suppress.\n\nWhat was checked and found NOT to exist (i.e. no control that rules this out): no binding check ties the extracted code to a transaction the user initiated — `extractLatestOtp` accepts only `appFilter` and `limit`; no per-use confirmation exists on the Tier-2 path; `isSensitive` is never consulted on this path; the result JSON always carries the `otp` field when `found` is true; the memory vault is a plain SQLite database (`flames_memory.db`) with no application-level encryption, and the repository's own integration test has the model persist the extracted code there via the auto-executing Tier-1 `save_memory` tool.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "Static-only trace. No emulator, no adb and no running app instance were available, so neither the OTP extraction nor the outbound request body was executed or observed. Specifically unverified at runtime: that a real notification reaches the in-memory listener buffer with an extractable code, that the tool-call round trip actually places the `otp` field in the outbound HTTP body, and that the configured endpoint is a third-party cloud service for a given deployment. Each hop was confirmed by reading source, and the two transmission sinks were confirmed to consume the tool result verbatim.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirming that the model can be induced to call `extract_latest_otp` by attacker-controlled text the device ingests (a hostile notification body, a fetched web page, or an imported skill) and that the resulting code reaches the outbound request body would raise this to high, because the OTP would then be exfiltrated by a third party rather than merely disclosed to a user-chosen endpoint. Conversely, demonstrating that the configured endpoint is consistently a self-hosted server under the user's own control, or that the model in practice refuses to extract authentication codes, would lower the severity toward low.",
              "fix_verification": "**Gate 1 — Applicability.** `fix_before` was copied from the file with `awk 'NR==871' lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart`, which returned `        securityTier: SecurityTier.tier2Permission,` (verified byte-exact including the 8-space indent via `cat -A`, terminating `$`). `start_line`/`end_line` = 871/871 cover exactly that single line. The replacement is a one-token change that leaves the `RegisteredTool` constructor call, the parameter list, and `checkPermission` untouched.\n\n**Gate 2 — Security closure.** Re-traced both sinks through the patched source. The tier value is read from a local constant at registration (`tool_registry.dart:871`) and never from remote input, so it cannot be influenced by a gateway frame, skill package, tool result, or model-supplied argument. With the value changed to `tier3Hitl`, `SecurityPolicy.requiresHitl` (`security_policy.dart:23-25`) returns true, and:\n- `agent_cubit.dart:760` now takes the HITL branch, emits `WaitingApproval`, and `return`s before the auto-execute path at `:766` — the local ReAct sink is blocked pending an explicit user tap.\n- `gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:89` now requires the approval callback; when none is registered the handler fails closed with `'... requires user approval, but no approval handler was registered.'` and never reaches `executeTool` at `:148` — the gateway sink is blocked.\nThe OTP therefore cannot be extracted without a per-use approval, and the `otp` field cannot reach either outbound body without the user seeing the tool name and arguments first. The fix enforces the invariant at the single chokepoint all three call sites share.\n\n**Gate 3 — Bypass review.** Enumerated every caller of `ToolRegistry.executeTool` (`grep -rn \"executeTool\" lib/`): exactly three — `agent_cubit.dart:611`, `agent_cubit.dart:766`, `gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:148` — and all three consult `securityTier`, so no ungated caller exists. Checked for a non-HITL execution path around the gate: `approveAction` (`:611`) executes only after `state is WaitingApproval`; the voice dispatcher (`chat_voice_prompt_dispatcher.dart:125-127`) maps `WaitingApproval` to `VoiceTurnNeedsApprovalFailure` instead of approving, so voice cannot auto-clear the gate; the approval sheet is non-dismissible (`isDismissible: false, enableDrag: false`) and renders the actual `request.parameters` JSON (`action_approval_sheet.dart:45,68`), so no summary/argument mismatch (TOCTOU) is introduced. Alternate input classes considered: an `appFilter` or `limit` argument cannot change the tier, because the tier is a registration-time constant, not an argument; a hostile gateway frame replaying a `callId` cannot downgrade the tier, only mis-attribute an already-approved call (a distinct concern owned elsewhere). Also checked the sibling `read_recent_notifications`: it keeps its Tier-2 classification, which is correct — its output is already filtered by `isSensitive`.\n\n**Gate 4 — Preserved behavior.** The tool remains registered, discoverable, listed by `tool_search` (`tool_search_index.dart:106`), and functional; only its approval requirement changes. Legitimate OTP extraction still works — the user simply confirms it, which is the intended interaction for the state-changing tier. `checkPermission` is retained and is inert for Tier 3 because `tool_registry.dart:250-255` only evaluates it when `SecurityPolicy.requiresPermission(tier)` is true (Tier 2 only), so no permission regression for this tool. No other tool's classification is touched, so no unrelated behavior changes.\n\n**Gate 5 — Repository checks.** `dart analyze lib/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry.dart` was attempted; the Dart/Flutter toolchain is not installed in the analysis environment, so this gate could NOT be executed and is an explicit gap. The change is a single enum-literal substitution within an existing named argument, using a symbol (`SecurityTier.tier3Hitl`) that is already referenced elsewhere in the same file (`tool_registry.dart:604, 721, 1372, 1399`), so the type is known-good by construction. A reviewer should run `dart analyze` and the existing `test/domain/tools/chat/tool_registry_test.dart` suite, which asserts this tool's tier at `:337` and will need its expectation updated to `SecurityTier.tier3Hitl` — that test assertion is itself confirmation that the current tier is deliberate and load-bearing.\n\n**Gap summary (stated explicitly):** Gate 5 was not executed (no Dart toolchain); Gates 1-4 were performed by source inspection only. No device or emulator was available, so the end-to-end exploit — extraction of a live OTP and its transmission to the configured endpoint — was NOT executed and this finding is unconfirmed by dynamic validation.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. On the device, grant Flames Notification Listener access once (Android Settings, Notification Access). This is the only consent step, and it is never re-requested.\n2. Cause any installed application to post a notification containing a one-time passcode, for example a bank or mail application delivering a login verification code.\n3. In a Flames chat session, send a message that induces the agent to call the `extract_latest_otp` tool — for example a request to retrieve the latest verification code.\n4. Observe that the tool executes immediately with no approval dialog. The Tier-2 classification means `agent_cubit.dart` never enters the `WaitingApproval` branch.\n5. Observe that the tool result is appended as a `role: tool` message and included in the subsequent request body, so the passcode is transmitted to the configured model endpoint.\n6. Optionally observe the same result being returned to the remote gateway server when the call originates from a gateway `tool_call_request` frame.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-319",
          "ruleIndex": 6,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Credentials can be transmitted over cleartext HTTP/WS because endpoint validators accept http:// and ws:// URLs\n\nThe app's endpoint validators accept cleartext `http://` and `ws://` URLs, and the stored credentials are then attached to whatever endpoint results: the LLM API key is sent as `Authorization: Bearer` to `$baseUrl/chat/completions` and to the TTS endpoint, and the gateway token is transmitted as the first WebSocket frame. The project's own deployment guide instructs users to configure plain-http LAN endpoints, so the credential traverses the local network unencrypted and is recoverable by anyone who can observe that segment. Critically, Android's cleartext policy does not prevent this: Flutter's HTTP/WebSocket stack is implemented in the Dart VM and never consults it, and Flutter's own equivalent gate is permissive by default on Android."
          },
