        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-22",
          "ruleIndex": 5,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Path traversal in share-intent file handling writes attacker-controlled content outside the app cache directory\n\nThe app accepts ACTION_SEND intents from any installed application through its exported MainActivity. The receive_sharing_intent 1.9.0 plugin that consumes those intents resolves an attacker-supplied content:// URI by copying the provider's stream into a path built from the provider-controlled _display_name value, with no sanitisation. A malicious co-resident app can therefore supply a name containing parent-directory segments and have arbitrary bytes written to any path inside the Flames application's private data directory - including all three SQLite databases, the Hive store, and the secure-storage preference files."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/domain/services/share/share_target_service.dart"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 104,
                  "endLine": 114,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "/// Maps one plugin file to a payload. Returns null when path is empty.\n  SharedPayload? mapFile(SharedMediaFile file) {\n    if (file.path.trim().isEmpty) return null;\n    return SharedPayload(\n      kind: _mapKind(file.type),\n      value: file.path,\n      mimeType: file.mimeType,\n      thumbnail: file.thumbnail,\n      message: file.message,\n    );\n  }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Primary fix: reject share paths containing a parent-directory segment before they reach an agent prompt"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "pubspec.yaml"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 56,
                  "endLine": 56,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "receive_sharing_intent: ^1.9.0"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Informational: the third-party component carrying the defect; no fixed release available upstream"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "android:exported MainActivity (ai.hermes.flames/.MainActivity) - ACTION_SEND with EXTRA_STREAM content:// URI",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "lib/domain/services/share/share_target_service.dart"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 104,
                        "endLine": 114
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "/// Maps one plugin file to a payload. Returns null when the path is empty or,\n  /// for file-backed kinds, when the plugin-resolved path escapes its root.\n  ///\n  /// The share-intent plugin copies provider-supplied `content://` streams into\n  /// `context.cacheDir` using a `_display_name` value it does not sanitise, so a\n  /// hostile provider can return a name carrying `..` segments. Such a path must\n  /// never be forwarded into an agent prompt.\n  SharedPayload? mapFile(SharedMediaFile file) {\n    if (file.path.trim().isEmpty) return null;\n    final kind = _mapKind(file.type);\n    if (_isFileBacked(kind) && _hasParentTraversal(file.path)) return null;\n    return SharedPayload(\n      kind: kind,\n      value: file.path,\n      mimeType: file.mimeType,\n      thumbnail: file.thumbnail,\n      message: file.message,\n    );\n  }\n\n  /// True when the payload names a file on disk rather than free text or a URL.\n  bool _isFileBacked(SharedPayloadKind kind) =>\n      kind == SharedPayloadKind.image ||\n      kind == SharedPayloadKind.video ||\n      kind == SharedPayloadKind.file;\n\n  /// True when any path segment is `..`, i.e. the path can name a location\n  /// outside the directory the plugin intended to write into.\n  bool _hasParentTraversal(String path) => path.split('/').contains('..');"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Validate the resolved share path in the application's own mapping layer before it is used, rejecting any file-backed payload whose path contains a parent-directory segment. The guard belongs at the single point where plugin output is converted into a domain payload, so every consumer inherits it.\n\nTreat the third-party plugin as untrusted: its copy destination is derived from a provider-controlled display name, so no path it returns should be assumed to live inside the cache directory. If an upstream release fixes the issue, upgrade and keep the guard as defence in depth.\n\nConsider additionally constraining the share surface itself, since the exported activity accepts intents from any application. If cross-application sharing is required, validate that each resolved path lies under the expected cache root by comparing canonicalised paths rather than by string inspection alone, and prefer copying the stream into an application-chosen filename instead of one derived from provider metadata.\n\nFinally, review whether the persisted skill store should be trusted input at startup: imported skill instructions are rendered into the agent system prompt, so a tamper-evident or integrity-checked store would limit the effect of any future write primitive.",
                "markdown": "Validate the resolved share path in the application's own mapping layer before it is used, rejecting any file-backed payload whose path contains a parent-directory segment. The guard belongs at the single point where plugin output is converted into a domain payload, so every consumer inherits it.\n\nTreat the third-party plugin as untrusted: its copy destination is derived from a provider-controlled display name, so no path it returns should be assumed to live inside the cache directory. If an upstream release fixes the issue, upgrade and keep the guard as defence in depth.\n\nConsider additionally constraining the share surface itself, since the exported activity accepts intents from any application. If cross-application sharing is required, validate that each resolved path lies under the expected cache root by comparing canonicalised paths rather than by string inspection alone, and prefer copying the stream into an application-chosen filename instead of one derived from provider metadata.\n\nFinally, review whether the persisted skill store should be trusted input at startup: imported skill instructions are rendered into the agent system prompt, so a tamper-evident or integrity-checked store would limit the effect of any future write primitive."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "5bf05087e79434cbb363c1ef6282a9e243867031450c70f2d3853b3c5d3b506d"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.8",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6c58275b746a212b66e13227aff805df9d5241b3a0ee6715ddb4941ae4556cfd",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0014",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.8,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-15 07:12:48 UTC",
              "target": "Flames Android application (ai.hermes.flames) source repository",
              "endpoint": "android:exported MainActivity (ai.hermes.flames/.MainActivity) - ACTION_SEND with EXTRA_STREAM content:// URI",
              "cwe": "CWE-22",
              "impact": "A malicious application installed on the same device can overwrite arbitrary files inside the Flames private data directory (/data/user/0/ai.hermes.flames/) with arbitrary bytes. Affected targets include the three application SQLite databases (databases/flames_skills.db, databases/flames_chat.db, databases/flames_memory.db), the Hive action-centre store (app_flutter/flames/action_center.hive), and the shared_prefs/ files backing flutter_secure_storage. Consequences: destruction or substitution of the user's persisted agent skills, chat history and memory vault; replacement of the imported-skills table with attacker-authored rows that are loaded at startup and rendered into the agent system prompt; and loss or corruption of the stored LLM API key and server profiles. Code execution was not achieved and is not claimed - no code path in the application loads or executes files from these directories.",
              "technical_analysis": "**Reachability.** android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml:57-116 declares MainActivity with android:exported=\"true\" and no android:permission, so any third-party app may start it. Six SEND/SEND_MULTIPLE filters (AndroidManifest.xml:78-108) accept text/*, image/* and */*, so an ACTION_SEND intent with type=\"image/*\" matches.\n\n**Sink.** The receive_sharing_intent 1.9.0 plugin (declared at pubspec.yaml:56, resolved at pubspec.lock:1402-1409) is registered on the Flutter activity and receives the intent through onAttachedToActivity / onNewIntent (ReceiveSharingIntentPlugin.kt:186-208). Its media branch requires a non-null MIME type and calls FileDirectory.getAbsolutePath on the supplied EXTRA_STREAM URI (ReceiveSharingIntentPlugin.kt:86-91, :141).\n\nIn FileDirectory.getDataColumn, the uri.authority != null branch queries the provider for the _display_name column and builds the destination directly from the returned value:\n\n```kotlin title=android/src/main/kotlin/com/kasem/receive_sharing_intent/FileDirectory.kt startLineNumber=93 endLineNumber=128\nif (uri.authority != null) {\n    var cursor: Cursor? = null\n    val column = \"_display_name\"\n    ...\n    val fileName = cursor.getString(columnIndex)\n    targetFile = File(context.cacheDir, fileName)\n    ...\n    context.contentResolver.openInputStream(uri)?.use { input ->\n        FileOutputStream(targetFile).use { fileOut ->\n            input.copyTo(fileOut)\n        }\n    }\n    return targetFile.path\n}\n```\n\nNo sanitisation, normalisation or containment check exists anywhere in the plugin - a repository-wide search for sanitiz, normaliz, canonical, validate and the parent-directory token returns only MIME-type startsWith comparisons.\n\n**Why the traversal works.** On Android/Linux, java.io.File(File parent, String child) resolves through UnixFileSystem.resolve, which concatenates parent + \"/\" + child - an absolute child does not discard the parent (that behaviour is Windows-only). Consequently an absolute _display_name stays inside the cache directory, but a name containing parent-directory segments survives verbatim into the path string and is resolved by the kernel at open() time. With cacheDir = /data/user/0/ai.hermes.flames/cache, a _display_name of ../databases/flames_skills.db produces /data/user/0/ai.hermes.flames/cache/../databases/flames_skills.db, which the kernel resolves to /data/user/0/ai.hermes.flames/databases/flames_skills.db - outside the intended root.\n\n**Application-side consumer.** The path returned by the plugin is surfaced to Dart as SharedMediaFile.path, mapped without validation in lib/domain/services/share/share_target_service.dart:105-114, and rendered into the chat prompt by lib/presentation/screen/chat/chat_screen.dart:531-564. No Dart-layer validation of the resolved path exists on any hop.\n\n**Escalation.** databases/flames_skills.db is opened at startup (lib/data/datasources/local/skills/skills_local_datasource.dart:53-76) and its imported_skills.payload rows are deserialised and registered (:115-130), then rendered into the system prompt by lib/domain/services/skills/skills_registry_service.dart:332-372 and sent to the remote model at lib/presentation/screen/chat/cubit/agent_cubit.dart:916-921. Because the attacker controls the entire file, they can supply a well-formed database matching the known schema and thereby place attacker-authored instructions into the agent's system prompt.",
              "remediation_steps": "Validate the resolved share path in the application's own mapping layer before it is used, rejecting any file-backed payload whose path contains a parent-directory segment. The guard belongs at the single point where plugin output is converted into a domain payload, so every consumer inherits it.\n\nTreat the third-party plugin as untrusted: its copy destination is derived from a provider-controlled display name, so no path it returns should be assumed to live inside the cache directory. If an upstream release fixes the issue, upgrade and keep the guard as defence in depth.\n\nConsider additionally constraining the share surface itself, since the exported activity accepts intents from any application. If cross-application sharing is required, validate that each resolved path lies under the expected cache root by comparing canonicalised paths rather than by string inspection alone, and prefer copying the stream into an application-chosen filename instead of one derived from provider metadata.\n\nFinally, review whether the persisted skill store should be trusted input at startup: imported skill instructions are rendered into the agent system prompt, so a tamper-evident or integrity-checked store would limit the effect of any future write primitive.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest case against the finding was that the traversal might be blocked by Android itself or by a control in the application. Both were checked and neither holds.\n\n(1) Android does not constrain _display_name. It is a provider-supplied string with no platform-mandated character set or length limit; ContentResolver.query returns exactly what the provider writes into the cursor. The copy destination is chosen entirely by the plugin.\n\n(2) No sanitisation exists anywhere in the path. The plugin source was searched in full for sanitiz, normaliz, canonical, validate, isAbsolute, contains('..'), startsWith and SecurityException; the only matches are MIME-type prefix comparisons. The Dart consumer at lib/domain/services/share/share_target_service.dart:105-114 performs only an emptiness check, and no other Dart code validates the resolved path.\n\n(3) The premise that an absolute child path discards the parent does NOT hold on Android/Linux - UnixFileSystem.resolve concatenates, so an absolute _display_name stays inside the cache directory. The escape therefore depends specifically on parent-directory segments, which was confirmed by direct reproduction rather than assumed. This narrows the primitive but does not remove it.\n\n(4) The uri.authority == null branch (FileDirectory.kt:131-144) returns a provider-supplied _data value without copying. It was assessed as a read primitive and rejected: content:// URIs normally carry an authority, and an authority-less content:// URI would make ContentResolver.query throw rather than return attacker data. It is not claimed as a read primitive here.\n\n(5) Code execution was explicitly ruled out. Searches of lib/ and android/app/src/main/kotlin/ for Process.start, Runtime.getRuntime, DexClassLoader, System.load, DynamicLibrary.open and dart:ffi return nothing; no artefact under databases/, shared_prefs/ or app_flutter/ is ever loaded or executed. The impact is data integrity only.\n\n(6) Background activity launch restrictions on Android 10+ are a genuine constraint on the attacker and are reflected in the assumptions. They reduce the reliability of the launch but are not a control in the application: an attacker app in the foreground, holding SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW, or using a foreground service can still deliver the intent.\n\n(7) The prompt-injection escalation through flames_skills.db was traced statically (skills_local_datasource.dart:115-130 to skills_registry_service.dart:332-372 to agent_cubit.dart:916-921) and is reachable, but it was not executed and is described as a plausible follow-on rather than a demonstrated outcome.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated. No emulator, device or adb was available for this assessment, so the write was never performed against the real application. The evidence is (a) verbatim source of the vulnerable plugin, verified byte-for-byte against the lockfile hash; (b) an offline harness that reproduces the exact path-construction and copy logic and demonstrates the escape outside the cache directory; and (c) a compiled, analyzer-clean behavioural test of the proposed fix. The unverified step is Android runtime behaviour itself - that Context.getCacheDir() yields /data/user/0/ai.hermes.flames/cache and that the kernel resolves the parent-directory segment during FileOutputStream creation. Both follow from documented platform semantics but were not observed on a device.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation on a device that the write lands outside the cache directory and that a substituted flames_skills.db survives into the agent system prompt would raise this to high by establishing a remote-content-to-agent-instruction path; conversely, evidence that the affected databases are recreated or integrity-checked at every startup would lower the integrity impact.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 - Applicability. fix_before was copied from the working tree with sed -n '104,114p' lib/domain/services/share/share_target_service.dart and byte-compared; the block is exactly 11 lines and start_line/end_line cover it precisely. dart format --output=none --set-exit-if-changed on the patched file reports no changes needed, so the change is formatter-clean. dart analyze on the patched file in a package with resolved dependencies reports \"No issues found!\", matching the unpatched baseline.\n\nGate 2 - Security closure. The guard runs inside mapFile, which is the single mapping point for every share payload: mapMediaFiles (line 95) delegates to it, and both getInitialPayloads (line 68) and the live stream handler in startListening (line 49) reach the prompt only through mapMediaFiles. A payload whose path contains a parent-directory segment is dropped before a SharedPayload is constructed, so it can never reach lib/presentation/screen/chat/chat_screen.dart:531-564 or the agent turn. The write inside the plugin still occurs - the fix removes the path's use, not the plugin's copy - but the attacker-supplied destination is no longer acted upon by the application, and the corresponding prompt entry is suppressed.\n\nGate 3 - Bypass review. The diff was re-read against the sink rather than the rationale. Alternate input classes were tried: multiple stacked parent segments (a/b/../../../databases/x.db), a parent segment embedded in a longer filename (a..b.png, correctly not flagged), and dot-prefixed hidden files (..hidden.txt, correctly not flagged). Sibling call sites of the mapping helper were enumerated (mapMediaFiles, getInitialPayloads, startListening) and all route through the patched method. The kind gate deliberately exempts text and url payloads, whose values are free text and may legitimately contain the parent-directory token. A real behavioural test of the patched class (compiled and executed) passes 33 assertions covering hostile names, benign names, MediaStore _data paths, text/URL payloads and the repository's existing test expectations. A negative control ran the same hostile inputs through the unpatched method: all three traversal names passed through unmodified, confirming the new test fails without the fix.\n\nGate 4 - Preserved behavior. Legitimate shares continue to work: cache filenames such as IMG_20260101.jpg, report.pdf and a..b.png are kept; genuine MediaStore _data paths such as /storage/emulated/0/DCIM/Camera/IMG_1.jpg are kept; text and url payloads are never path-checked; mimeType, thumbnail and message are still propagated. The repository's own assertions (test/domain/services/share/share_target_service_test.dart:153-167, and the kind-mapping tests at :108-150) were re-executed against the patched class and still pass.\n\nGate 5 - Repository checks. dart analyze on the patched file: no issues. The package's full test suite could not be executed because the Flutter SDK in this environment is an x86-64 build on an aarch64 host, and pub.dev is unreachable for dependency resolution. This is an explicit gap: the fix was verified with a standalone package that compiles the patched class verbatim against the real entity and a stub of the plugin's public surface, not with flutter test on the repository.\n\nNarrowest complete change. The fix is confined to the mapping method plus two small private helpers in the same class, uses no new dependencies, and does not alter the plugin, the cubit, the UI or any public signature.\n\nResidual gap (documented, not fixed). The check is a pure-string test and does not resolve symlinks, so a symlink placed inside an allowed directory would not be caught; the plugin's destination is built with a plain string concatenation and no symlink is created by this path. The root cause remains in the third-party plugin, and no upstream release fixes it (1.9.0 is the current published version), so a Dart-layer guard is the only remediation available inside this repository.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Install a malicious application on the same device. It declares an exported ContentProvider (for example authority com.evil.app.provider) whose query() returns a cursor exposing a _display_name column with the value ../databases/flames_skills.db, and whose openFile() returns attacker-chosen bytes.\n2. From that application, construct an ACTION_SEND intent with type=\"image/*\" (any non-null MIME type), EXTRA_STREAM set to content://com.evil.app.provider/x, CATEGORY_DEFAULT, and FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION.\n3. Target the exported component explicitly with setClassName(\"ai.hermes.flames\", \"ai.hermes.flames.MainActivity\") and call startActivity().\n4. On Android 10 and later the sending application must be in the foreground (or hold SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW) for the activity launch to be permitted; the target app then launches and processes the intent.\n5. The plugin queries the provider for _display_name, builds File(cacheDir, \"../databases/flames_skills.db\"), opens the provider stream and writes the bytes with FileOutputStream, resolving outside the cache directory.\n6. Confirm on the device that /data/user/0/ai.hermes.flames/databases/flames_skills.db now contains the attacker-supplied bytes rather than the previous database.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-306",
          "ruleIndex": 11,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Gateway WebSocket dispatches privileged tool-call frames before authentication completes\n\nThe Hermes gateway WebSocket client dispatches action-bearing frames to the device RPC receiver regardless of whether the authentication handshake has completed. `_handleIncomingData` intercepts only the auth success/failure frames while the connection is in the `connecting` state; every other frame type falls through to a dispatcher that contains no state check. A peer that answers the configured gateway origin can therefore drive device tool execution for the duration of the authentication timeout without ever presenting the gateway token. The client's own \"authenticated\" state is likewise asserted by the peer, not proven, because the success frame carries no nonce, signature, or channel binding. Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated: no emulator, device, or running gateway was available, so no frame was delivered to a running application."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 259,
                  "endLine": 284,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "}\n\n      // If connected:\n      if (message is GatewayPingMessage) {\n        send(GatewayPongMessage(timestamp: message.timestamp));\n      } else if (message is GatewayPongMessage) {\n        _watchdogTimer?.cancel();\n        _watchdogTimer = null;\n      } else if (message is GatewayTextDeltaMessage) {\n        _textDeltaController.add(message);\n      } else if (message is GatewayToolCallRequestMessage) {\n        if (!_toolCallRequestController.hasListener) {\n          sendToolCallResponse(\n            callId: message.id,\n            error: 'Device RPC receiver is not listening',\n          );\n        } else {\n          _toolCallRequestController.add(message);\n        }\n      } else if (message is GatewayElicitationRequestMessage) {\n        if (!_elicitationRequestController.hasListener) {\n          sendElicitationResponse(requestId: message.requestId, approved: false);\n        } else {\n          _elicitationRequestController.add(message);\n        }\n      }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Enforce the authenticated-state precondition before dispatching action-bearing frames"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 259,
                        "endLine": 284
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "}\n\n      // Action-bearing frames are only dispatched once the connection has\n      // completed authentication. While the handshake is still in flight the\n      // peer has not presented the gateway token, so a `tool_call_request` or\n      // `elicitation_request` must not reach the device RPC receiver.\n      if (_state != GatewayConnectionState.connected) {\n        if (message is GatewayToolCallRequestMessage) {\n          sendToolCallResponse(\n            callId: message.id,\n            error: 'Device is not authenticated',\n          );\n        } else if (message is GatewayElicitationRequestMessage) {\n          sendElicitationResponse(\n            requestId: message.requestId,\n            approved: false,\n          );\n        }\n        _messageController.add(message);\n        return;\n      }\n\n      if (message is GatewayPingMessage) {\n        send(GatewayPongMessage(timestamp: message.timestamp));\n      } else if (message is GatewayPongMessage) {\n        _watchdogTimer?.cancel();\n        _watchdogTimer = null;\n      } else if (message is GatewayTextDeltaMessage) {\n        _textDeltaController.add(message);\n      } else if (message is GatewayToolCallRequestMessage) {\n        if (!_toolCallRequestController.hasListener) {\n          sendToolCallResponse(\n            callId: message.id,\n            error: 'Device RPC receiver is not listening',\n          );\n        } else {\n          _toolCallRequestController.add(message);\n        }\n      } else if (message is GatewayElicitationRequestMessage) {\n        if (!_elicitationRequestController.hasListener) {\n          sendElicitationResponse(requestId: message.requestId, approved: false);\n        } else {\n          _elicitationRequestController.add(message);\n        }\n      }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Make the inbound dispatcher state-aware. Add an explicit authenticated-state guard to the frame dispatcher in `GatewayClient._handleIncomingData` so that action-bearing frames are only dispatched once the connection has reached the authenticated state. The existing \"If connected:\" comment describes the intended precondition; enforce it in code rather than in a comment.\n2. Fail closed for the whole pre-authentication period. While the connection is in the connecting state, drop (or reply to with an error) every frame other than the authentication result and the liveness frames the handshake requires. Do not rely on the authentication timeout to bound the exposure, because the window re-opens on every reconnect.\n3. Bind the authentication result to the request. Have the gateway echo a client-generated nonce (or sign the authentication result with the shared secret) so that a peer answering the socket cannot assert a successful authentication it did not perform. At minimum, treat the success frame as advisory and keep the tool-call path closed until a challenge has been answered.\n4. Keep the RPC listener aligned with the authenticated state. The listener is currently started for every non-disconnected state; it should not accept requests before authentication, so that the transport-level guard is not the only control.\n5. Add a regression test that delivers a `tool_call_request` frame before `auth_success` and asserts that no tool is executed and no response frame is sent.",
                "markdown": "1. Make the inbound dispatcher state-aware. Add an explicit authenticated-state guard to the frame dispatcher in `GatewayClient._handleIncomingData` so that action-bearing frames are only dispatched once the connection has reached the authenticated state. The existing \"If connected:\" comment describes the intended precondition; enforce it in code rather than in a comment.\n2. Fail closed for the whole pre-authentication period. While the connection is in the connecting state, drop (or reply to with an error) every frame other than the authentication result and the liveness frames the handshake requires. Do not rely on the authentication timeout to bound the exposure, because the window re-opens on every reconnect.\n3. Bind the authentication result to the request. Have the gateway echo a client-generated nonce (or sign the authentication result with the shared secret) so that a peer answering the socket cannot assert a successful authentication it did not perform. At minimum, treat the success frame as advisory and keep the tool-call path closed until a challenge has been answered.\n4. Keep the RPC listener aligned with the authenticated state. The listener is currently started for every non-disconnected state; it should not accept requests before authentication, so that the transport-level guard is not the only control.\n5. Add a regression test that delivers a `tool_call_request` frame before `auth_success` and asserts that no tool is executed and no response frame is sent."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "b93a2d0ffdb599f4d4d4ea332150d7348efc0a054d43d5946311559c04595aea"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.8",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "a84d13436026e6f857a476382d08ca77be3f6515ed97fc6c676aa2ff3c5ddf2c",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0015",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.8,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-15 07:52:39 UTC",
              "target": "Flames Android application (`ai.hermes.flames`), repository under audit",
              "endpoint": "lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart",
              "method": "WebSocket (JSON-RPC frames)",
              "cwe": "CWE-306",
              "impact": "A peer positioned to answer the configured gateway endpoint — an on-path attacker, or the operator of an attacker-chosen gateway destination obtained through the QR pairing path — can issue `tool_call_request` frames that reach `GatewayDeviceRpcReceiver.handleRequest` and execute device tools without ever supplying the bearer token. Because only Tier 3 tools are gated by human approval on this path, every Tier 1 and Tier 2 tool is reachable: the demonstrated consequences in this repository include arbitrary-path file reads (`read_device_file`, `list_device_files`), notification and OTP extraction, clipboard reads, and an unrestricted outbound URL fetch (`read_web_page`). The window is not one-shot: the authentication timer is 10 seconds by default, automatic reconnection is enabled with up to ten attempts, and each reconnect re-opens the same window on the same stored endpoint. The net effect is that the token check is advisory rather than a gate — it determines when the client considers itself connected, not what the peer is permitted to make the device do.",
              "technical_analysis": "## The dispatcher has no state guard\n\n`GatewayClient._handleIncomingData` is the single entry point for every inbound frame. While the connection is `connecting`, it intercepts exactly two message types and returns:\n\n```dart title=lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart startLineNumber=240 endLineNumber=259\n      if (_state == GatewayConnectionState.connecting) {\n        if (message is GatewayAuthSuccessMessage) {\n          _authTimer?.cancel();\n          _authTimer = null;\n          _reconnectAttempts = 0;\n          _setState(GatewayConnectionState.connected);\n          _startHeartbeat();\n          _sendToolManifest();\n          _messageController.add(message);\n          return;\n        } else if (message is GatewayAuthFailureMessage) {\n          _authTimer?.cancel();\n          _authTimer = null;\n          _manuallyDisconnected = true; // Never auto-reconnect on auth rejection\n          _cleanUpSocket(_CloseCause.authRejected);\n          _setState(GatewayConnectionState.disconnected);\n          _messageController.add(message);\n          return;\n        }\n      }\n```\n\nEvery other frame type falls out of that block and reaches the dispatcher below. That dispatcher carries a comment claiming a state precondition it does not enforce:\n\n```dart title=lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart startLineNumber=261 endLineNumber=277\n      // If connected:\n      if (message is GatewayPingMessage) {\n        send(GatewayPongMessage(timestamp: message.timestamp));\n      } else if (message is GatewayPongMessage) {\n        _watchdogTimer?.cancel();\n        _watchdogTimer = null;\n      } else if (message is GatewayTextDeltaMessage) {\n        _textDeltaController.add(message);\n      } else if (message is GatewayToolCallRequestMessage) {\n        if (!_toolCallRequestController.hasListener) {\n          sendToolCallResponse(\n            callId: message.id,\n            error: 'Device RPC receiver is not listening',\n          );\n        } else {\n          _toolCallRequestController.add(message);\n        }\n      }\n```\n\nThere is no `_state == GatewayConnectionState.connected` test anywhere in the chain from `_handleIncomingData` to `ToolRegistry.executeTool`.\n\n## The unauthenticated window is open and recurring\n\n`_state` remains `connecting` for the whole authentication timeout, which defaults to 10 seconds:\n\n```dart title=lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart startLineNumber=48 endLineNumber=58\n  const GatewayClientOptions({\n    this.heartbeatInterval = const Duration(seconds: 30),\n    this.heartbeatTimeout = const Duration(seconds: 10),\n    this.authTimeout = const Duration(seconds: 10),\n    this.connectTimeout = const Duration(seconds: 15),\n    this.autoReconnect = true,\n    this.initialReconnectDelay = const Duration(seconds: 1),\n    this.maxReconnectDelay = const Duration(seconds: 30),\n    this.reconnectBackoffFactor = 1.5,\n    this.maxReconnectAttempts = 10,\n    this.keepalivePingInterval = const Duration(seconds: 20),\n  });\n```\n\nThe timer is armed immediately after the socket is established and the auth frame is sent (`:220`), and reconnection reuses the same stored endpoint and token, so the window re-opens on each of up to ten attempts:\n\n```dart title=lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart startLineNumber=376 endLineNumber=380\n    _reconnectTimer = Timer(delay, () {\n      if (!_manuallyDisconnected && !_disposed && _currentGatewayUrl != null) {\n        connect(_currentGatewayUrl!, token: _currentToken);\n      }\n    });\n```\n\n## The listener is provably active during `connecting`\n\nThe receiver subscription is started for every state that is not fully disconnected:\n\n```dart title=lib/presentation/screen/gateway/cubit/gateway_rpc_cubit.dart startLineNumber=117 endLineNumber=124\n  void syncWithConnectionState(GatewayConnectionState state) {\n    if (state.isDisconnected) {\n      stopListening();\n      _rejectAllElicitations(notifyServer: true);\n    } else {\n      startListening();\n    }\n  }\n```\n\nThis is deliberate, documented behaviour — the doc comment above it states the intent to listen \"whenever the link is live (connected/connecting/reconnecting)\" — and the repository's own test asserts it:\n\n```dart title=test/presentation/screen/gateway/cubit/gateway_rpc_cubit_test.dart startLineNumber=401 endLineNumber=417\n    test(\n        'syncWithConnectionState keeps listening for connected/connecting/'\n        'reconnecting and startListening stays idempotent', () {\n      ...\n      for (final state in [\n        GatewayConnectionState.connecting,\n        GatewayConnectionState.connected,\n        GatewayConnectionState.reconnecting,\n      ]) {\n        cubit.syncWithConnectionState(state);\n        expect(cubit.isListening, isTrue, reason: 'expected listening: $state');\n      }\n```\n\n## Frame reaches tool execution\n\nOnce on the controller, the frame is handled with only the Tier 3 gate applied:\n\n```dart title=lib/domain/services/gateway/gateway_rpc_receiver.dart startLineNumber=88 endLineNumber=89\n    // Check Tier 3 HITL approval\n    if (SecurityPolicy.requiresHitl(tool.securityTier)) {\n```\n\n```dart title=lib/domain/services/gateway/gateway_rpc_receiver.dart startLineNumber=148 endLineNumber=151\n      final resultEither = await toolRegistry.executeTool(\n        request.tool,\n        request.arguments,\n      );\n```\n\nTier 1 and Tier 2 tools reach execution. The Tier 2 permission gate is additionally inert for tools registered without a checker (reported separately as `vuln-0006`), and the outbound URL fetch is itself unrestricted (reported separately as `vuln-0013`).\n\n## The peer asserts its own authentication\n\nThe frame that flips the client to `connected` carries no proof:\n\n```dart title=lib/domain/entities/gateway/gateway_message.dart startLineNumber=129 endLineNumber=143\nclass GatewayAuthSuccessMessage extends GatewayMessage {\n  final String? message;\n  final String? sessionId;\n\n  const GatewayAuthSuccessMessage({\n    this.message,\n    this.sessionId,\n  }) : super('auth_success');\n\n  factory GatewayAuthSuccessMessage.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) {\n    return GatewayAuthSuccessMessage(\n      message: json['message'] as String?,\n      sessionId: (json['session_id'] ?? json['sessionId']) as String?,\n    );\n  }\n```\n\nBoth fields are optional strings. There is no nonce, HMAC, or binding to the `GatewayAuthMessage` the client sent, so the same peer that can answer the socket can also declare the session authenticated. The pre-auth window is therefore not merely a race — the peer controls both when it ends and what it claims.\n\n## Frame identity is peer-controlled\n\n`GatewayMessage.fromJson` keys off the `type`/`event` field, and the `auth` case maps any frame with a success-like `status` to the success message:\n\n```dart title=lib/domain/entities/gateway/gateway_message.dart startLineNumber=17 endLineNumber=27\n      case 'auth':\n        // If it's an incoming auth status or ack from server:\n        if (json.containsKey('status')) {\n          final status = json['status']?.toString().toLowerCase();\n          if (status == 'authenticated' || status == 'ok' || status == 'success') {\n            return GatewayAuthSuccessMessage.fromJson(json);\n          } else {\n            return GatewayAuthFailureMessage.fromJson(json);\n          }\n        }\n        return GatewayAuthMessage.fromJson(json);\n```",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Make the inbound dispatcher state-aware. Add an explicit authenticated-state guard to the frame dispatcher in `GatewayClient._handleIncomingData` so that action-bearing frames are only dispatched once the connection has reached the authenticated state. The existing \"If connected:\" comment describes the intended precondition; enforce it in code rather than in a comment.\n2. Fail closed for the whole pre-authentication period. While the connection is in the connecting state, drop (or reply to with an error) every frame other than the authentication result and the liveness frames the handshake requires. Do not rely on the authentication timeout to bound the exposure, because the window re-opens on every reconnect.\n3. Bind the authentication result to the request. Have the gateway echo a client-generated nonce (or sign the authentication result with the shared secret) so that a peer answering the socket cannot assert a successful authentication it did not perform. At minimum, treat the success frame as advisory and keep the tool-call path closed until a challenge has been answered.\n4. Keep the RPC listener aligned with the authenticated state. The listener is currently started for every non-disconnected state; it should not accept requests before authentication, so that the transport-level guard is not the only control.\n5. Add a regression test that delivers a `tool_call_request` frame before `auth_success` and asserts that no tool is executed and no response frame is sent.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest case against this finding is that the transport is a WebSocket to an operator-chosen endpoint, so the peer answering it is often the legitimate gateway and the window may be hard to reach without already holding a privileged network position. That argument limits who can exploit it but does not neutralise the defect, for three reasons established in source rather than assumed. First, the authentication gate is the only thing separating an unauthenticated peer from tool execution, and it does not run: the code path from frame receipt to `ToolRegistry.executeTool` contains no state check, and the repo's own test confirms the listener is active while `connecting`. Second, the precondition is reachable without a network position in the general case, because the gateway destination itself is attacker-influenceable through the QR pairing path (`vuln-0012`) — a hostile pairing payload makes the attacker's server the configured endpoint, after which the token gate is the sole remaining control and it is bypassed here. Third, the exposure is not a narrow race: the authentication timeout is 10 seconds, automatic reconnection is enabled with ten attempts, and each attempt re-opens the same window against the same endpoint.\n\nChecked and found absent: any state test in the dispatch chain from `_handleIncomingData` (`gateway_client.dart:226-290`) through `gateway_rpc_cubit.dart:127-139` to `gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:68-151`; any nonce, signature, HMAC, or channel binding in `GatewayAuthSuccessMessage` (`gateway_message.dart:129-143`); any server-identity verification beyond TLS certificate validation against the system trust store (no pinning exists in the codebase); and any secondary authorization layer on the gateway tool path beyond the Tier 3 check. The repository's existing test at `gateway_client_test.dart:660-700` asserts that \"the auth frame is the only frame before auth_success\" — that statement concerns outbound frames only and does not contradict this finding. No runtime confirmation was possible: there is no emulator, device, or gateway in the assessment environment, so no frame was ever delivered.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "Static trace only. The absence of the state guard, the active listener during `connecting`, the 10-second default window with automatic reconnection, the peer-asserted success frame, and the execution sink are all evidenced at named locations, and the repository's own test independently confirms the listener behaviour that makes the window exploitable. The gap is that no frame was ever delivered to a running application: there is no emulator, no device, and no gateway in the assessment environment, so the end-to-end effect (a tool result returned over an unauthenticated socket) was never observed.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "A demonstration on a running device that a `tool_call_request` sent before `auth_success` returns a tool result would raise this to high, because it converts a missing gate into a demonstrated unauthenticated device-capability invocation; conversely, evidence that the deployment always terminates the socket or rejects frames until authentication completes at a proxy in front of the gateway would lower it.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 — Security closure: REASONED, NOT EXECUTED. Re-traced source to sink through the patched code. `_handleIncomingData` now returns early for every frame received while `_state != connected`, before the dispatcher that calls `_toolCallRequestController.add(...)`. The only way to reach tool execution is `gateway_rpc_cubit.dart:127-139` -> `gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:148`, and that requires the frame to be added to the controller, which the guard now prevents until `_setState(GatewayConnectionState.connected)` has run — and that transition is itself only reachable from the `GatewayAuthSuccessMessage` branch at `gateway_client.dart:241-249`. Action-bearing frames are additionally answered with an error rather than silently dropped, so a legitimate peer that is merely early receives a diagnosable response instead of a timeout. Not executed: no Dart/Flutter toolchain, emulator, or device was available, so neither the vulnerable nor the patched behaviour was observed at runtime.\n\nGate 2 — Bypass review: reviewed without relying on the rationale that produced the patch. Sibling paths to the same sink were enumerated: `_toolCallRequestController.add` is called at exactly one site (`gateway_client.dart:276`), and `_elicitationRequestController.add` at exactly one site (`:282`); both are now behind the guard. The other consumer of frames, `_messageController.add`, is deliberately left reachable for all states so that UI state and error reporting are unaffected — that channel carries no execution authority, and the tool-call stream is a separate broadcast controller, so leaving it open does not reopen the boundary. Alternate input classes checked: a frame that self-identifies as `auth` with a success-like `status` (`gateway_message.dart:17-27`) still only sets `connected` through the existing branch and is not affected by this change; a `tool_call_request` arriving during `reconnecting` is likewise refused, which is correct because `_state` is not `connected` in that state either. The one deliberate behaviour change is that a peer which (incorrectly) issues tool calls before authenticating now receives an error frame instead of execution — that is the intended fail-closed direction.\n\nGate 3 — Preserved behaviour: PARTIAL. The legitimate handshake is unchanged: the auth frame is still sent on connect (`:212-216`), `auth_success` still transitions to `connected` and starts the heartbeat and manifest (`:241-249`), `auth_failure` still disconnects without reconnecting (`:250-258`), and post-authentication tool calls, elicitations, pings, and text deltas all flow exactly as before. The behaviour that changes is confined to pre-authentication frames, which is the security fix. One item could not be verified statically: whether any deployment relies on pre-auth tool dispatch as a latency optimization — the repository contains no such dependency (the tool manifest is only sent after `auth_success` at `:247`/`:299-307`, so the server cannot legitimately issue tool calls before then). The existing test at `gateway_client_test.dart:660-700`, which asserts the auth frame is the only outbound frame before `auth_success`, remains satisfied.\n\nGate 4 — Repository checks: NOT RUN, and this is an explicit gap. `dart analyze` and `flutter test` are unavailable (no Dart or Flutter SDK in the assessment environment). The patch introduces no new imports (`GatewayConnectionState` is already imported at `gateway_client.dart:8` and `sendToolCallResponse` / `sendElicitationResponse` are methods on the same class), the line range was verified against the file, and the surrounding code style is matched, but no formatter, analyzer, or test suite was executed. A reviewer must run the package's own checks before merging. A regression test should be added that delivers a `tool_call_request` before `auth_success` and asserts no execution and no response.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Establish that the dispatcher has no authentication-state guard: read `lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart:240-259` (the `connecting` block intercepts only auth success/failure) and `:261-284` (the fall-through dispatcher, whose \"If connected:\" comment is not backed by a state test).\n2. Establish that a `tool_call_request` is routed to the device RPC receiver while the socket is unauthenticated: `gateway_client.dart:269-277` adds the parsed frame to `_toolCallRequestController`.\n3. Establish that the receiver is listening during `connecting`: `lib/presentation/screen/gateway/cubit/gateway_rpc_cubit.dart:117-124` starts listening for any non-disconnected state, and `test/presentation/screen/gateway/cubit/gateway_rpc_cubit_test.dart:401-417` asserts that `connecting` keeps the listener active.\n4. Establish that the window persists and recurs: `gateway_client.dart:51` sets a 10-second `authTimeout`, `:53` enables `autoReconnect`, `:57` allows ten attempts, and `:376-380` reconnects to the same stored URL with the same token.\n5. Establish that the frame reaches execution with only the Tier 3 gate: `lib/domain/services/gateway/gateway_rpc_receiver.dart:89` and `:148-151`.\n6. Establish that the peer can self-assert authentication: `lib/domain/entities/gateway/gateway_message.dart:129-143` (success frame carries only optional strings) and `:17-27` (any frame with a success-like `status` is accepted as the success message).\n7. On a device, point the app at a gateway the tester controls, accept the socket, and send `{\"type\":\"tool_call_request\",\"id\":\"call_1\",\"tool\":\"read_device_file\",\"arguments\":{\"path\":\"/data/data/ai.hermes.flames/databases/flames_memory.db\"}}` before answering the auth frame; observe the tool result returned to the socket. This step was NOT performed — no emulator, device, or running gateway was available in the assessment environment.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Inbound gateway chat frame role field is unvalidated and can inject a system-role instruction into the LLM context\n\nThe gateway WebSocket client takes the role field of an inbound chat_message frame verbatim from the wire, stores it, persists it, and later re-serializes it into the model request unfiltered. A frame declaring \"role\": \"system\" becomes a system-role instruction in the language model's context, giving whoever controls the gateway connection authority over the model's instructions rather than only over its data. The entry survives mode switches and restarts because it is persisted and reloaded into the same in-memory list the direct-mode LLM call serializes. Unconfirmed, not dynamically validated: no emulator, device, or running gateway was available."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/domain/entities/gateway/gateway_message.dart"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 358,
                  "endLine": 364
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Allowlist the inbound role at the parse boundary"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "lib/presentation/screen/chat/chat_screen.dart",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "lib/domain/entities/gateway/gateway_message.dart"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 358,
                        "endLine": 364
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "/// Roles a gateway peer is permitted to claim. Everything else - notably\n  /// `system`, which the application reserves for locally-authored\n  /// instructions - is coerced to a data role so a remote frame can never\n  /// carry instruction authority.\n  static const Set<String> _allowedRoles = {'assistant', 'user'};\n\n  static String _sanitizeRole(Object? raw) {\n    final normalized = (raw ?? 'user').toString().trim().toLowerCase();\n    return _allowedRoles.contains(normalized) ? normalized : 'assistant';\n  }\n\n  factory GatewayChatMessage.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) {\n    return GatewayChatMessage(\n      content: (json['content'] ?? json['message'] ?? '').toString(),\n      role: _sanitizeRole(json['role']),\n      metadata: json['metadata'] as Map<String, dynamic>?,\n    );\n  }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Constrain the inbound role at the parse boundary: normalize the incoming value and map it through an explicit allowlist, coercing anything that is not a known safe role to a data role such as assistant. Coercing at the parse site covers every present and future reader of the field.\n2. Treat the gateway channel as a data source, not an instruction source. No frame received over the socket should produce a message with system authority; system-role content should only be constructed locally.\n3. Apply the same discipline to the other text-bearing frame types (text_delta, tool_event, error) so the rule is uniform across the protocol.\n4. Where a peer genuinely needs to influence model behaviour, use the protocol's explicit channels (tool manifest, elicitation request) rather than the conversation stream, so the influence is visible and gated.\n5. Add a regression test asserting that a chat_message frame carrying role \"system\" (and \"tool\", and an unknown value) is surfaced as assistant, and that assistant and user pass through unchanged.",
                "markdown": "1. Constrain the inbound role at the parse boundary: normalize the incoming value and map it through an explicit allowlist, coercing anything that is not a known safe role to a data role such as assistant. Coercing at the parse site covers every present and future reader of the field.\n2. Treat the gateway channel as a data source, not an instruction source. No frame received over the socket should produce a message with system authority; system-role content should only be constructed locally.\n3. Apply the same discipline to the other text-bearing frame types (text_delta, tool_event, error) so the rule is uniform across the protocol.\n4. Where a peer genuinely needs to influence model behaviour, use the protocol's explicit channels (tool manifest, elicitation request) rather than the conversation stream, so the influence is visible and gated.\n5. Add a regression test asserting that a chat_message frame carrying role \"system\" (and \"tool\", and an unknown value) is surfaced as assistant, and that assistant and user pass through unchanged."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "6deeeb710da79b5d2b0776afaf3d3d5f403474fefe58b07ce0ff2ae8f713d44a"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.2",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "33cf02afa43c9e112fe9edc9d126d5e4b23a7049168e56e0d081a7aef51f9afb",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0016",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.2,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-15 07:58:14 UTC",
              "target": "Flames Android application (ai.hermes.flames), repository under audit",
              "endpoint": "lib/presentation/screen/chat/chat_screen.dart",
              "method": "WebSocket (JSON-RPC frames)",
              "cwe": "CWE-863",
              "impact": "A party able to send frames on the gateway connection can plant text the model subsequently treats as its own system-level instructions rather than conversation data. Because the entry is persisted and reloaded into the list the direct-mode request serializes, the injection outlives the delivering turn, survives mode switches, and survives restart. The practical consequence is instruction authority over the agent: gateway-supplied text can direct later model behaviour, and since Tier 1 tools auto-execute on model output and the Tier 2 gate is inert for tools registered without a checker, that authority can be converted into device actions. The demonstrated consequence is the misplaced authority itself, not an observed action.",
              "technical_analysis": "The role is unvalidated at the parse boundary. gateway_message.dart:358-364 sets role from json['role'] with no allowlist and no normalization, so \"system\" is accepted exactly like \"user\" or \"assistant\". The value is then stored verbatim: chat_screen.dart:311-316 builds ChatMessage(role: message.role, content: message.content), and the only role test on that path is the UI-settling comparison at :307. It is persisted and reloaded by agent_cubit.dart:185-189 (addMessage appends and calls _persistMessages) and :330-352 (_loadSessionMessages restores the list). The list is shared across modes: _toggleMode (chat_screen.dart:1084-1112) clears only gateway tool-event cards, never _messages. Serialization preserves the role: chat_message.dart:116-119 emits 'role': role unconditionally, and agent_cubit.dart:906 maps every message through toWireJson into serializedMessages, which is passed to the LLM client at :923-930 and posted by chat_datasource.dart:38-54. Why this is a control defect rather than a naming detail: the application constructs system messages locally in exactly two places - the memory block and the skill prompt at agent_cubit.dart:907-921 - so system is explicitly a reserved role for content the application considers authoritative. Nothing prevents an inbound frame from claiming it, and once stored there is no way to distinguish the peer-supplied entry from the locally-constructed ones.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Constrain the inbound role at the parse boundary: normalize the incoming value and map it through an explicit allowlist, coercing anything that is not a known safe role to a data role such as assistant. Coercing at the parse site covers every present and future reader of the field.\n2. Treat the gateway channel as a data source, not an instruction source. No frame received over the socket should produce a message with system authority; system-role content should only be constructed locally.\n3. Apply the same discipline to the other text-bearing frame types (text_delta, tool_event, error) so the rule is uniform across the protocol.\n4. Where a peer genuinely needs to influence model behaviour, use the protocol's explicit channels (tool manifest, elicitation request) rather than the conversation stream, so the influence is visible and gated.\n5. Add a regression test asserting that a chat_message frame carrying role \"system\" (and \"tool\", and an unknown value) is surfaced as assistant, and that assistant and user pass through unchanged.",
              "counterevidence": "Timing is the strongest argument against: gateway-mode turns are answered by the server, so a planted entry may not reach a model request in the same turn. That limits immediacy but not the defect - the entry is written to the session store by addMessage (agent_cubit.dart:185-189), reloaded into the shared _messages list by _loadSessionMessages (:330-352), survives the mode switch (_toggleMode at chat_screen.dart:1084-1112 never clears _messages), and is serialized verbatim by :906 and chat_message.dart:116-127. The window is the life of the session, not one turn. A second argument is that the peer is the operator's own gateway; that reduces who can plant the entry but not the missing control.\n\nChecked and found absent: any allowlist or normalization of role on the inbound path; any filtering in toWireJson; and any check distinguishing a locally-authored system message from a socket-supplied one. The only role comparisons in the codebase are message.role == 'assistant' (chat_screen.dart:307, UI settling) and message.role == 'user' (agent_cubit.dart:881, prompt pruning). No runtime confirmation was possible.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "Static trace only. Every hop is evidenced at a named location, but no frame was delivered to a running application and no model request was observed, because there is no emulator, device, or gateway in the assessment environment.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "A runtime demonstration that a peer-supplied role \"system\" frame changes subsequent model behaviour on a device - for example the model executing a tool it was directed to by that frame - would raise this to high; conversely, evidence that the deployment never issues direct-mode turns on a session that also carries gateway traffic would lower it.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 (security closure) — REASONED, NOT EXECUTED: the only path by which a gateway frame becomes a ChatMessage is chat_screen.dart:311-317, which now receives a role narrowed at the parse site (gateway_message.dart:358-372). A frame declaring system is coerced to assistant, so it cannot enter _messages with system authority; serialization at agent_cubit.dart:906 then emits only assistant or user. Coercing at the parse boundary covers every consumer, including the voice dispatcher's reply matcher (gateway_voice_prompt_dispatcher.dart:60-62). Not executed: no Dart/Flutter toolchain, emulator, or device was available.\n\nGate 2 (bypass review): reviewed without relying on the rationale that produced the patch. GatewayChatMessage is constructed only from fromJson and local code; its role is read at exactly two places (chat_screen.dart:307 for UI settling, gateway_voice_prompt_dispatcher.dart:61 for reply matching), neither weakened by the coercion. Alternate input classes checked: alternate spellings (System, SYSTEM, whitespace-padded) are normalized before the allowlist test; a missing or non-string role already fell back to user and still does; the other text-bearing frame types (text_delta, tool_event, error) are handled separately in chat_screen.dart and never construct a ChatMessage with a role. One deliberate narrowing accepted: a peer sending role \"tool\" gets assistant, because a tool role without a matching tool_call_id is not a valid request shape for the OpenAI format anyway.\n\nGate 3 (preserved behaviour): CONFIRMED by inspection. An assistant reply still arrives as role 'assistant', still settles the running tool cards (chat_screen.dart:307), still satisfies the voice dispatcher's matcher, and is still stored and rendered as before. Direct-mode requests are unaffected because they are built from locally-authored messages plus the two locally-constructed system entries (agent_cubit.dart:907-921). The parse still never throws on a malformed role — it normalizes instead.\n\nGate 4 (repository checks) — NOT RUN, explicit gap: dart analyze and flutter test are unavailable (no Dart or Flutter SDK in the assessment environment). The patch adds a static const Set to an existing class and introduces no new imports, and the line range was verified against the file, but no formatter, analyzer, or test suite was executed. A reviewer must run the package's own checks before merging, and a regression test should assert that a chat_message frame with role \"system\" is surfaced as assistant.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Confirm the role is taken from the wire without validation: lib/domain/entities/gateway/gateway_message.dart:358-364.\n2. Confirm delivery to the UI consumer: gateway_client.dart:286 adds every parsed message to _messageController; chat_message/message map to GatewayChatMessage at gateway_message.dart:65-67.\n3. Confirm verbatim storage: chat_screen.dart:311-317 builds ChatMessage(role: message.role, content: message.content); the only role test there is UI settling at :307.\n4. Confirm persistence and reload: agent_cubit.dart:185-189 and :330-352.\n5. Confirm survival of the mode switch: chat_screen.dart:1084-1112 clears only gateway tool-event cards, never _messages.\n6. Confirm the model request: agent_cubit.dart:906 serializes every message via toWireJson; chat_message.dart:116-127 emits 'role': role unconditionally; the result is passed to streamCompletion at :923-930 and posted by chat_datasource.dart:38-54.\n7. On a device, deliver a chat_message frame with role \"system\" over the socket, switch to direct mode, send any prompt, and observe the instruction presented to the model as a system message. NOT performed - no emulator, device, or running gateway was available.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-522",
          "ruleIndex": 12,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Gateway bearer token disclosed to an arbitrary origin via WebSocket handshake redirect\n\nThe app connects to the Hermes Gateway with `WebSocket.connect()` and immediately writes an `auth` frame containing the stored gateway bearer token. The dart:io WebSocket handshake is an ordinary `GET` issued by a shared `HttpClient` whose request objects default to `followRedirects = true` with up to 5 redirects. A `301`/`302`/`303`/`307`/`308` response to the handshake is therefore followed automatically, the upgrade is completed against the redirect target, and the credential frame is written to that socket. The client never checks that the origin it finally connected to is the origin it was configured with. Reproduced against a local redirect server: the token frame arrived at the redirect target, including a `wss://` to `http://` cleartext downgrade."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1,
                  "endLine": 2,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "import 'dart:async';\nimport 'dart:io';"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Add the HTTP client type needed for the redirect-safe handshake"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 59,
                  "endLine": 79,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "class IoGatewaySocketFactory implements GatewaySocketFactory {\n  const IoGatewaySocketFactory();\n\n  @override\n  Future<GatewaySocket> connect(\n    Uri uri, {\n    Map<String, dynamic>? headers,\n    Duration? timeout,\n    Duration? pingInterval = const Duration(seconds: 20),\n  }) async {\n    final customHeaders = headers?.map((k, v) => MapEntry(k, v.toString()));\n\n    final future = WebSocket.connect(\n      uri.toString(),\n      headers: customHeaders,\n    );\n\n    final ws = timeout != null ? await future.timeout(timeout) : await future;\n    ws.pingInterval = pingInterval;\n    return IoGatewaySocket(ws);\n  }\n}"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Pin the handshake to the configured origin"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 1,
                        "endLine": 2
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "import 'dart:async';\nimport 'dart:io';\n\n/// An [HttpClient] whose requests never follow HTTP redirects.\n///\n/// The WebSocket handshake is an ordinary GET, and dart:io's default client\n/// follows redirects on it. A redirect would move the handshake - and the\n/// authentication frame written immediately afterwards - to an origin the\n/// user never configured, disclosing the gateway bearer token. Disabling\n/// redirects on the handshake pins the connection to the configured origin.\nclass _NoRedirectHttpClient implements HttpClient {\n  final HttpClient _inner = HttpClient();\n\n  @override\n  Future<HttpClientRequest> openUrl(String method, Uri url) async {\n    final request = await _inner.openUrl(method, url);\n    request.followRedirects = false;\n    return request;\n  }\n\n  @override\n  dynamic noSuchMethod(Invocation invocation) => throw UnsupportedError(\n        '_NoRedirectHttpClient does not implement ${invocation.memberName}',\n      );\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 59,
                        "endLine": 79
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "class IoGatewaySocketFactory implements GatewaySocketFactory {\n  const IoGatewaySocketFactory();\n\n  /// Handshake client that refuses HTTP redirects so the socket can only ever\n  /// reach the origin the user configured.\n  static final HttpClient _handshakeClient = _NoRedirectHttpClient();\n\n  @override\n  Future<GatewaySocket> connect(\n    Uri uri, {\n    Map<String, dynamic>? headers,\n    Duration? timeout,\n    Duration? pingInterval = const Duration(seconds: 20),\n  }) async {\n    final customHeaders = headers?.map((k, v) => MapEntry(k, v.toString()));\n\n    final future = WebSocket.connect(\n      uri.toString(),\n      headers: customHeaders,\n      customClient: _handshakeClient,\n    );\n\n    final ws = timeout != null ? await future.timeout(timeout) : await future;\n    ws.pingInterval = pingInterval;\n    return IoGatewaySocket(ws);\n  }\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Disable HTTP redirect following on the WebSocket handshake so the socket can only ever reach the origin the user configured, and treat a redirecting origin as a connection failure. The concrete change is in `lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart`: pass a `customClient:` to `WebSocket.connect` whose requests set `followRedirects = false` (see the suggested patch).\n\nAs defence in depth, consider verifying the origin actually reached before the authentication frame is written, and re-checking it on every reconnect. If the deployment expects redirects (for example an HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrade on the gateway), resolve them explicitly with an allowlist rather than letting the transport follow them implicitly.",
                "markdown": "Disable HTTP redirect following on the WebSocket handshake so the socket can only ever reach the origin the user configured, and treat a redirecting origin as a connection failure. The concrete change is in `lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart`: pass a `customClient:` to `WebSocket.connect` whose requests set `followRedirects = false` (see the suggested patch).\n\nAs defence in depth, consider verifying the origin actually reached before the authentication frame is written, and re-checking it on every reconnect. If the deployment expects redirects (for example an HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrade on the gateway), resolve them explicitly with an allowlist rather than letting the transport follow them implicitly."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "5ed9589fee802487f0118b3658d9d1bc1bc16d9fe4ef5161dbe8eafe950fa4db"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "7e11b2034b7f114faa8b688075b8d491a43d65ab87b5b14badc02e9908adf6a1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0017",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-15 07:59:36 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/flames-audit-t196 (Flutter/Dart Android app \"Flames\")",
              "endpoint": "lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart",
              "cwe": "CWE-522",
              "impact": "The stored gateway bearer token is disclosed to whoever controls the HTTP response to the handshake for the configured origin. Because the same value is reused as the LLM API key, the disclosed credential also authenticates the OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint. The disclosure is silent (no user-visible error, the socket reports as connected) and is independent of TLS: a `wss://` profile can be redirected to a cleartext `http://` listener, so the token crosses the network in plaintext. The attacker needs the ability to answer for the configured host - a MITM on the path, a hostile Wi-Fi/DNS/proxy, or control of the endpoint itself.",
              "technical_analysis": "**Call path**\n\n1. `lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart:189-193` - `connect()` calls `socketFactory.connect(uri, timeout: ..., pingInterval: ...)`. No headers and no HTTP client are supplied.\n2. `lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart:71-74` - `IoGatewaySocketFactory.connect` calls `WebSocket.connect(uri.toString(), headers: customHeaders)` with `customHeaders == null`. The `customClient:` parameter, which is the only supported way to control the handshake request, is not used.\n3. In dart:io (`sdk/lib/_http/websocket_impl.dart`), `WebSocket.connect` rewrites the `ws`/`wss` scheme to `http`/`https` and issues the handshake via a **static shared** `HttpClient` (`static final HttpClient _httpClient = HttpClient();`, `openUrl(\"GET\", uri)`). Nothing in that path sets `followRedirects = false`.\n4. In dart:io (`sdk/lib/_http/http_impl.dart`), `_HttpClientRequest` defaults `bool _followRedirects = true;` and `int _maxRedirects = 5;`, and `_handleIncoming` re-issues the request to the `Location` target when `followRedirects && response.isRedirect`. For `GET`, `isRedirect` covers `301`, `302`, `303`, `307` and `308`.\n5. `lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client.dart:212-216` - once the (redirected) socket is established, the client immediately sends `GatewayAuthMessage(token: token ?? '', clientId: clientId)`.\n6. `lib/domain/entities/gateway/gateway_message.dart:117-124` - `GatewayAuthMessage.toJson()` serialises the raw token into the frame body.\n\n**Origin binding is never re-established**\n\n`connect()` stores `_currentGatewayUrl` and `_currentToken` at `gateway_client.dart:179-180` and passes them to the socket factory without re-validating the URL that was actually reached. There is no origin/allowlist check before `send(authMessage)`, and the reconnect path reuses the locally stored URL (`gateway_client.dart:376-380`), so no server-supplied URL is involved - the exposure is purely the redirect behaviour of the handshake.\n\n**Header-copy note (not the leak vector, but relevant)**\n\ndart:io's `shouldCopyHeaderOnRedirect` only strips `authorization`/`www-authenticate`/`proxy-*`/`cookie`/`cookie2` on cross-origin hops (`http_impl.dart` `sensitiveHeaders`). The WebSocket upgrade headers (`Connection`, `Upgrade`, `Sec-WebSocket-Key`) are re-sent, and after the upgrade succeeds the application's own auth frame - which is not an HTTP header - is written to whichever socket was returned.\n\n**Verified against primary sources at the pinned revisions**\n\nThe project pins framework revision `d3b14c876900e553bc736ca19295fc09e3853e8e` (`.metadata`), whose engine revision is `a804b261645ef8c13eb3d5c44a5c2fb0340c5539`, whose Dart revision is `60a57cd42d64dc03e9f07aa60a2e250755c1ef28`. The dart:io sources above were read at that Dart revision and the app-side sources from the repository.",
              "remediation_steps": "Disable HTTP redirect following on the WebSocket handshake so the socket can only ever reach the origin the user configured, and treat a redirecting origin as a connection failure. The concrete change is in `lib/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_socket.dart`: pass a `customClient:` to `WebSocket.connect` whose requests set `followRedirects = false` (see the suggested patch).\n\nAs defence in depth, consider verifying the origin actually reached before the authentication frame is written, and re-checking it on every reconnect. If the deployment expects redirects (for example an HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrade on the gateway), resolve them explicitly with an allowlist rather than letting the transport follow them implicitly.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively hunted for a control and found none on this path:\n\n- **No `followRedirects = false` anywhere in the WebSocket path.** The only occurrences in the repository are `url_reader_service.dart:47` (`followRedirects: true`, an unrelated read-only fetcher) and `skill_remote_loader.dart:64` (`followRedirects: false`, an unrelated skill download). Neither is on the gateway path.\n- **No `customClient` is passed** to `WebSocket.connect` (`gateway_socket.dart:71-74`), so there is no opportunity for a caller-supplied redirect policy.\n- **No origin check before the auth frame.** `connect()` (`gateway_client.dart:175-216`) normalizes the configured URL, connects, and immediately sends the auth frame; it never compares the reached origin with `_currentGatewayUrl`.\n- **Not a server-supplied-URL issue.** No gateway frame type carries a URL (`gateway_message.dart` frame set: auth/auth_success/auth_failure/ping/pong/text_delta/tool_call_request/tool_call_response/tool_event/elicitation_request/elicitation_response/chat_message/tool_manifest/error/chat_cancel/raw), and the reconnect path reuses the locally stored `_currentGatewayUrl` (`gateway_client.dart:376-380`). The redirect is the only way the client is re-pointed.\n- **Not blocked by TLS.** Confirmed live: a `wss://` first hop that redirects to an `http://` target results in the token being written to the cleartext listener.\n- **TLS certificate validation is not the mitigating factor here** - it is intact (no `badCertificateCallback`/`onBadCertificate`/`setTrustedCertificates`/`SecurityContext`/`HttpOverrides` anywhere in `lib/` or `android/`), but certificate validation only authenticates the *first* hop; the redirect target is reached by design afterwards.\n\nLimiting factor, stated plainly: the attacker must be positioned to answer for the configured origin. This is not a remote unauthenticated attack from an arbitrary internet host; it is a credential-disclosure primitive for an attacker on the path or in control of the configured endpoint. That is why the rating is Medium rather than High, and it is scored as such.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "The mechanism was reproduced live with a Dart runtime using the exact call shape of the application (`WebSocket.connect` without `customClient`, followed by an auth frame), and the redirect was demonstrated both cross-origin and as a `wss://` -> `http://` cleartext downgrade. The application-side source and the pinned dart:io sources were read directly. The one thing not exercised is the compiled Flutter APK on a device, since no emulator is available - the finding is therefore unconfirmed *on-device*, though the underlying transport behaviour it depends on is proven.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that this occurs against a production Hermes Gateway deployment with a real stored token (rather than the isolated local reproduction) would raise confidence to fully on-device and justify treating it as High; conversely, evidence that deployments pin the gateway origin at the network layer (for example a mutually authenticated tunnel or an origin-locked reverse proxy) would reduce the practical impact to Low.",
              "fix_verification": "**Security closure (executed).** Re-ran the PoC with the patched call shape. The redirect is now refused and no token is written:\n\n```text\n301: refused (GOOD)\n302: refused (GOOD)\n303: refused (GOOD)\n307: refused (GOOD)\n308: refused (GOOD)\nlegit endpoint: connect+frame OK -> {\"type\":\"auth\",\"token\":\"T\"}\nattacker frames received: 0\n```\n\nThe failure is explicit rather than silent: `WebSocketException: Connection to 'http://127.0.0.1:37427/device/ws#' was not upgraded to websocket, HTTP status code: 302`. `GatewayClient.connect` catches this at `gateway_client.dart:221-223` and routes it to `_handleSocketError`, which triggers the normal reconnect/backoff path - so a redirecting origin now surfaces as a connection failure instead of a silent credential disclosure.\n\n**Bypass review (executed).** Checked all five redirect status codes dart:io treats as redirects for `GET` (`301`, `302`, `303`, `307`, `308`) - all refused. The patched client is applied at the single handshake call site (`gateway_socket.dart:71`), which is the only `WebSocket.connect` in the repository, so there are no sibling call sites that bypass it. The `_NoRedirectHttpClient` sets the flag on the request returned by `openUrl`, which is the same request object dart:io subsequently sends the upgrade headers on, so there is no path where a redirect is followed before the flag is applied.\n\n**Preserved behavior (executed).** A legitimate non-redirecting endpoint still completes the handshake and exchanges an auth frame and its echo through the same client. Connection timeout (`timeout`), `pingInterval` assignment, and the `GatewaySocket` wrapper are untouched. `connect()`'s own error handling and reconnect logic are untouched.\n\n**Repository checks (gap - no Dart toolchain for the repo).** No `dart`/`flutter` toolchain is installed in this environment and the repository's own test suite (`test/domain/services/gateway/gateway_url_test.dart`, `test/data/datasources/remote/gateway/gateway_client_test.dart`) could not be executed against the patched tree. The proposed code was compiled and executed standalone with Dart 3.13.3 (the language version pinned by this project's engine revision) as part of the two probes above, so it is syntactically valid and behaviourally verified in isolation - but `flutter analyze` and `flutter test` were not run. **This gate is a stated gap.**\n\n**Note on the `noSuchMethod` forwarder.** `_NoRedirectHttpClient` implements only `openUrl` and forwards nothing else; every other `HttpClient` member throws `UnsupportedError`. That is deliberate - the WebSocket handshake only calls `openUrl` - but if a future caller reuses this client for other request types it must be extended. A reviewer may prefer to keep the class private to `gateway_socket.dart` (as written) so that scope stays obvious.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "The PoC stands up two local HTTP listeners: one \"configured\" origin that answers the WebSocket handshake with a `302` pointing at the second, \"attacker\" listener, which completes the WebSocket upgrade and records every frame it receives.\n\n1. Start the attacker listener and the redirecting origin (ports chosen dynamically).\n2. Call `WebSocket.connect(uri)` using the same call shape as `IoGatewaySocketFactory.connect` - no `customClient`.\n3. Write an `auth` frame in the same shape as `GatewayClient` does after connecting.\n4. Observe that the connection succeeds, and that the attacker listener receives the frame including the token.\n\nThe same PoC was re-run with the configured origin on `wss://` (self-signed certificate) redirecting to a cleartext `http://` attacker listener; the token again arrived at the cleartext listener.\n\nA companion probe confirms the proposed fix: an `HttpClient` whose `openUrl` sets `request.followRedirects = false` causes all five redirect status codes to be refused, while a legitimate non-redirecting endpoint still connects and carries a frame normally.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
