        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/hitl-approval-gate-integrity-across-concurrent-turns-shared-pending-approval-slot-overwrite-approving-one-tool-may-execute-another",
          "ruleIndex": 95,
          "kind": "open",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "HITL approval-gate integrity across concurrent turns (shared pending-approval slot overwrite -> approving one tool may execute another) — Requires further review: AgentCubit._pendingToolCall approval slot (agent_cubit.dart:64, :752-762, :602-614) with unguarded second-turn entry points (share intent, NFC)\n\nDistinct from the already-recorded single-turn check (entry c8730f, which asked whether the sheet's displayed arguments match the executed ones — they do, same map object). This row is the CROSS-TURN case: approval is bound to the shared mutable slot `_pendingToolCall` (agent_cubit.dart:64) rather than to the request the user is looking at. `_processPendingToolCalls` assigns the slot then emits `WaitingApproval` (:759-761); `approveAction` re-reads the slot (:606-614) and never compares it with `state.request`. A second turn can start while a tier-3 approval is pending because `sendMessage` has no busy/awaiting-approval guard (:502-536, it only bumps `_runGeneration`), and two unguarded entry points reach it — the exported share intent (chat_screen.dart:386-398 -> :531-565 -> :1494+) and the NFC trigger (agent_cubit.dart:561-580). If that second turn also pauses on a tier-3 tool, the slot is overwritten with a different request while the older sheet is still mounted (the listener pushes a new modal at chat_screen.dart:1572-1585 and never dismisses the previous one; the sheet is isDismissible:false / enableDrag:false with PopScope canPop:false at action_approval_sheet.dart:29-48). A tap landing in the window between the overwrite emit and the new sheet being pushed would execute the request that was never displayed. I could NOT name a control that closes this: the `if (state is! WaitingApproval) return;` guard at :603 only covers the case where the state has already left WaitingApproval (e.g. after `stop()`, which nulls the slot at :844 and emits AgentIdle at :846), and it does not detect that the slot was replaced by a different request while the state stayed WaitingApproval. I also could not confirm it — it needs a real device to observe the emit-to-push frame window and to make the model emit a second tier-3 call, and no emulator/adb exists in this environment. Recorded as open rather than ruled out."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "AgentCubit._pendingToolCall approval slot (agent_cubit.dart:64, :752-762, :602-614) with unguarded second-turn entry points (share intent, NFC)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "needs_follow_up",
              "risk_area": "HITL approval-gate integrity across concurrent turns (shared pending-approval slot overwrite -> approving one tool may execute another)",
              "surface": "AgentCubit._pendingToolCall approval slot (agent_cubit.dart:64, :752-762, :602-614) with unguarded second-turn entry points (share intent, NFC)",
              "recorded_by": "Voice Action Center And Local Storage",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/nfc-tag-triggering-an-autonomous-agent-prompt-from-attacker-supplied-content",
          "ruleIndex": 96,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "NFC tag triggering an autonomous agent prompt from attacker-supplied content — Ruled out: NFC prompt-binding trigger (agent_cubit.dart:561-580 + nfc_prompt_resolver.dart)\n\nNamed control: an NFC tap dispatches a prompt ONLY when the tag UID already has a binding the user created in Settings. `scanNfcAndDispatchPrompt` (lib/presentation/screen/chat/cubit/agent_cubit.dart:561-580) returns false before `sendMessage` unless `NfcPromptResolver.resolve(uid)` finds a binding (:576-577), and the resolver matches the card UID exactly after trim/lowercase against bindings read from the encrypted store (lib/domain/services/tools/nfc_prompt_resolver.dart:10-18; secure_storage_service.dart:321-331). The bindings themselves are written only from the Settings UI confirmation flow (settings_cubit.dart:340-346, settings_screen.dart:1144). So an attacker-controlled NFC tag cannot supply or select a prompt; it can only trigger a prompt the device owner already bound to that exact card, which is the feature working as intended. The dispatch also goes through the normal `sendMessage` path and therefore remains subject to the tier gate (this is the same path as the open approval-slot row above, which is recorded separately)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "NFC prompt-binding trigger (agent_cubit.dart:561-580 + nfc_prompt_resolver.dart)"
                }
