        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/credential-forwarded-to-a-cross-origin-redirect-target-authorization-header-handling-on-redirect",
          "ruleIndex": 97,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Credential forwarded to a cross-origin redirect target (Authorization header handling on redirect) — Ruled out: chat_datasource.dart + server_profile_probe_service_impl.dart redirect handling (followRedirects default true)\n\nRESOLVED — ruled out for the redirect vector specifically. dart:io's `_HttpClient.shouldCopyHeaderOnRedirect` (sdk/lib/_http/http_impl.dart, applied in `_openUrlFromRequest`) strips `authorization`, `cookie`, `proxy-authorization` and the other sensitive headers unless the redirect target is same-origin (scheme AND host AND port equal). dio's default `followRedirects: true` therefore does not forward the bearer credential to a cross-origin redirect target on either the chat path (chat_datasource.dart:38-52) or the probe path. This is a named control, not an absence of evidence. Separately, the cleartext-exposure half of these same clients (a non-TLS origin, not a redirect) is real and is filed as vuln-0009."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "chat_datasource.dart + server_profile_probe_service_impl.dart redirect handling (followRedirects default true)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "ruled_out",
              "risk_area": "Credential forwarded to a cross-origin redirect target (Authorization header handling on redirect)",
              "surface": "chat_datasource.dart + server_profile_probe_service_impl.dart redirect handling (followRedirects default true)",
              "recorded_by": "Cleartext Credential Transport Validator",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/tar-slip-path-traversal-symlink-escape-on-archive-extraction",
          "ruleIndex": 98,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Tar-slip / path traversal / symlink escape on archive extraction — Ruled out: lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/piper_tts_engine.dart:233-249 (Piper voice tarball extraction loop)\n\nINDEPENDENTLY RE-CONFIRMED after adversarial retry (was ruled_out; outcome unchanged, evidence strengthened). Built a second harness reproducing the loop verbatim and executed 39 + 30 hand-crafted byte-level tar vectors through the real `archive` 4.0.9 (`BZip2Decoder`→`TarDecoder`→loop). Every vector CONTAINED (0 escapes, canary files checked outside the destination):\n\nPAX `path=` rewriting (tar_decoder.dart:56-86 `nextName`): `../../`, absolute, nested `sub/../../../`, `./..`, backslash, `//tmp/`, prefix-only, trailing space, NUL-embedded, chained PAX headers, newline-in-value splitting, malformed-first-record, `./` component — all either rejected at :242 (`startsWith(prefix)`) or at :244 (`relative.contains('..')`). Only benign in-prefix paths landed (verified control: `PAX path=vits-piper-v/PWN_pax_legit` → written).\n\nGNU long-name (tar_decoder.dart:43-46): `../../`, absolute, traversal+NUL tail, 300-char nested, no-following-entry, and state-leak sequences (LongLink→directory, LongLink→PAX-override) — all rejected by the same two guards.\n\nUStar `filenamePrefix` (tar_file.dart:98-101 `'$filenamePrefix/$filename'`): prefix=`vits-piper-v` + name=`../…`, prefix=`vits-piper-v/..`, prefix=`vits-piper-v/../..`, name=`/tmp/…` — the concatenated name is always caught by the `..` check or confined by `_joinPath` to `<dest>/tmp/…`.\n\nEntry types passing `isFile` (`typeFlag != '5'`): '3' char, '4' block, '6' fifo, '7' contFile, NUL, 'A', 'L', 'x' — content IS yielded (`entry.content` non-null) and IS written, but always under `destDir`; traversal-named variants of '3'/'6'/'7' rejected at :244.\n\nSymlink/hardlink: typeflag '2' with linkname `/etc` → skipped at :240; typeflag '1' hardlink → decoder sets `symbolicLink` from `nameOfLinkedFile` (tar_decoder.dart:100-102) so also skipped; the EMPTY-linkname asymmetry (`isSymbolicLink` is `symbolicLink?.isNotEmpty` — empty linkname ⇒ `false`) was exercised for both '1' and '2': the entry is then treated as a regular file and its bytes are written INSIDE dest — a benign content-write, not a link creation. Confirmed no `Link(` call exists anywhere in `lib/`, so the app never materialises a symlink; write-through-symlink (F1) is therefore unreachable.\n\nMalformed UTF-8: overlong `C0 AE`, overlong NUL `C0 80`, raw `0xFF`, U+FF0E, U+2024 — all decoded as literal non-`.` characters (never normalised to `..` or `/`) and confined.\n\n`voice.id` provenance: hard allowlist. `downloadVoice` takes a `PiperVoiceDescriptor`; the only production caller resolves it via `piperVoiceById(voiceId)` (settings_cubit.dart:475) which returns null for anything outside the const `kPiperVoiceCatalog` (piper_voice.dart:27-61), and the UI passes `id` from that same catalog (settings_screen.dart:765-846). `savePiperVoiceId` has exactly one writer chain (settings_screen → settings_cubit.dart:438 → secure_storage_service.dart:92); no QR, gateway-RPC, skill, or model-output path writes `piper_voice_id` (grepped the full tree). So a separator/`..` cannot reach `voice.id`, `prefix`, or `destDir`.\n\nDestination is app-private: `getApplicationDocumentsDirectory()` (piper_tts_engine.dart:118) → `/data/user/0/ai.hermes.flames/app_flutter/tts_models/`; no external-storage permission in the manifest.\n\nPost-write steps do not matter: the `isVoiceReady` check (:256-264) and sidecar delete (:265-271) run AFTER the loop but only delete/extend within `<modelsRoot>/<id>`; a mid-loop throw (exercised) leaves a partial dir inside the app-private root, not outside it.\n\nAlso executed the FULL `downloadVoice()` end-to-end (MD5 gate + extraction + isVoiceReady + sidecar) against an archive combining PAX + LongLink + UStar traversal entries: completed, all canaries absent, only intended files under `<root>/tts_models/v/`. Note the MD5 gate (piper_tts_engine.dart:221-228) is a fixed compile-time constant digest over a hardcoded GitHub HTTPS URL, so an attacker cannot substitute a malicious tarball without an MD5 second-preimage (infeasible) — the loop only ever sees bytes matching the pinned digest.\n\nResidual (not a finding): a tar entry declaring a larger `fileSize` than the data actually present causes the decoder to read across subsequent headers, so the written file can contain neighbouring header bytes — still confined to dest."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/piper_tts_engine.dart:233-249 (Piper voice tarball extraction loop)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "ruled_out",
              "risk_area": "Tar-slip / path traversal / symlink escape on archive extraction",
              "surface": "lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/piper_tts_engine.dart:233-249 (Piper voice tarball extraction loop)",
              "recorded_by": "Tar Extraction Bypass Validator",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/attacker-controlled-artifact-or-digest-integrity-check-bypass",
          "ruleIndex": 99,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Attacker-controlled artifact or digest / integrity-check bypass — Ruled out: lib/domain/entities/chat/piper_voice.dart:27-61 + piper_tts_engine.dart:198-229 (Piper voice descriptor: digest provenance and download URL)\n\nBoth the expected MD5 and the download URL are compile-time `const` catalog entries, not a remote manifest: `tarballMd5`/`downloadUrl` at piper_voice.dart:34-37, 45-48, 56-59. `downloadVoice` receives a `PiperVoiceDescriptor`; the only callers pass `piperVoiceById(voiceId)` (settings_cubit.dart:475-480, :519) or catalog entries, and `downloadPiperVoice` rejects unknown ids with 'Unknown voice'. Verified the hardcoded digests against the real upstream artifacts: en_US-lessac-medium = e7e26d0730b3cb9b451efb3f7f700daf (downloaded 67230653 bytes) and id_ID-news_tts-medium = c91a63c2fa185b6f54609019e19232ef (downloaded 67243848 bytes) — both match. No QR/profile/skill/gateway path writes the descriptor. MD5 is therefore a hardening note only (no collision-resistant primitive), not exploitable: an attacker controls neither the artifact nor the digest."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
