        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/archive-content-integrity-entry-boundary-confusion-declared-size-vs-actual-data-entry-skip-via-removefile-mid-loop-abort-state",
          "ruleIndex": 129,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Archive content integrity / entry-boundary confusion (declared size vs actual data, entry-skip via removeFile, mid-loop abort state) — No issue identified: lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/piper_tts_engine.dart:233-249 (Piper voice tarball extraction loop) — content-integrity and abort-state behaviour\n\nExecuted a hand-crafted tar whose entry declares `fileSize` far larger than the data actually present (declared 4096 / 2560 bytes, supplied 5 bytes). Observed decoder behaviour: `TarFile.read` (tar_file.dart:96-113) consumes the declared byte count from the stream, so the entry's content absorbs the following tar header(s) and the next entry's data — the written file was 2560 bytes containing `AAAAA` + a verbatim tar header + `BBBBB`. No security impact and no escape: the write still lands under `<modelsRoot>/<voiceId>/` (guards at piper_tts_engine.dart:240-244 unchanged), the attacker must already fully control the archive bytes so re-synchronising to a chosen header grants nothing they did not already have, and an entry swallowed this way is simply never written (skipping cannot bypass a pre-write guard). Additionally the archive bytes cannot be attacker-chosen at all in practice: the MD5 gate at piper_tts_engine.dart:221-228 compares against a compile-time constant digest (piper_voice.dart:34/45/56) over a hardcoded HTTPS GitHub URL. Also verified the loop's `archive.removeFile(entry)` over `archive.files.toList()` (a defensive copy — archive.dart:16 `UnmodifiableListView`, :40-47 `removeFile`) cannot cause an entry to be skipped: executed with 3- and 5-entry archives, all entries seen and processed. A mid-loop `writeAsBytes` failure (file-then-file-under-that-file, errno 20 ENOTDIR) throws out of the loop, but the `finally` only deletes the tmp tarball and the leftover partial directory is inside the app-private models root. Residual, not a finding: `tarballSizeBytes` (piper_voice.dart:10) is declared but never enforced anywhere, so a compromised/malicious release server could serve an arbitrarily large body into the app-private dir — already recorded separately and not reachable without defeating TLS + the pinned MD5."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/piper_tts_engine.dart:233-249 (Piper voice tarball extraction loop) — content-integrity and abort-state behaviour"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "no_issue_found",
              "risk_area": "Archive content integrity / entry-boundary confusion (declared size vs actual data, entry-skip via removeFile, mid-loop abort state)",
              "surface": "lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/piper_tts_engine.dart:233-249 (Piper voice tarball extraction loop) — content-integrity and abort-state behaviour",
              "recorded_by": "Tar Extraction Bypass Validator",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/arbitrary-file-read-via-provider-supplied-data-absolute-path",
          "ruleIndex": 130,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Arbitrary file read via provider-supplied _data absolute path — Ruled out: FileDirectory.getDataColumn `_data` branch (plugin) → ShareTargetService.mapFile value propagation\n\nAssessed as a read primitive and rejected: FileDirectory.kt:131-144 (the uri.authority == null branch) returns a provider-supplied `_data` value with no copy, but a `content://` URI without an authority makes ContentResolver.query throw rather than return attacker data, and normal `content://` URIs always carry an authority (so they take the authority != null branch at :93). No path in this app produces an authority-less content:// URI. Additionally the value only ever reaches SharedPayload.value (share_target_service.dart:105-114) and is never opened by the Dart layer as a file - grep for File(payload / File(.*\\.value) returns nothing. Not reported."
          },
